const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A street.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Flavius, Marullus and a throng of Citizens.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Hence! home, you idle creatures, get you home.","Is this a holiday? What, know you not,","Being mechanical, you ought not walk","Upon a labouring day without the sign","Of your profession? Speak, what trade art thou?"],"id":2,"m":"Go away! Home, you lazy lot, go home. Is this a holiday? What, don't you know that, as working men, you're not supposed to walk about on a working day without the tools of your trade? You, what's your trade?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CARPENTER","lines":["Why, sir, a carpenter."],"id":3,"m":"Why, sir, I'm a carpenter."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARULLUS","lines":["Where is thy leather apron and thy rule?","What dost thou with thy best apparel on?","You, sir, what trade are you?"],"id":4,"m":"Where's your leather apron and your ruler? Why are you wearing your best clothes? You, sir, what's your trade?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBBLER","lines":["Truly, sir, in respect of a fine workman, I am but, as you would say, a","cobbler."],"id":5,"m":"Well, sir, compared with a fine craftsman, I'm only, as you might say, a cobbler."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARULLUS","lines":["But what trade art thou? Answer me directly."],"id":6,"m":"But what's your trade? Give me a straight answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"COBBLER","lines":["A trade, sir, that I hope I may use with a safe conscience, which is","indeed, sir, a mender of bad soles."],"id":7,"m":"A trade, sir, that I hope I can practise with a clear conscience. I'm a mender of bad soles, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARULLUS","lines":["What trade, thou knave? Thou naughty knave, what trade?"],"id":8,"m":"What trade, you rascal? You good-for-nothing, what trade?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBBLER","lines":["Nay, I beseech you, sir, be not out with me; yet, if you be out, sir, I","can mend you."],"id":9,"m":"Now, please, sir, don't be cross with me. Still, if your soles are worn out, sir, I can mend you."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARULLUS","lines":["What mean'st thou by that? Mend me, thou saucy fellow!"],"id":10,"m":"What do you mean by that? Mend me, you cheeky fellow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBBLER","lines":["Why, sir, cobble you."],"id":11,"m":"Why, sir, cobble you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Thou art a cobbler, art thou?"],"id":12,"m":"You're a cobbler, are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBBLER","lines":["Truly, sir, all that I live by is with the awl; I meddle with no","tradesman's matters, nor women's matters, but withal I am indeed, sir,","a surgeon to old shoes: when they are in great danger, I recover them.","As proper men as ever trod upon neat's leather have gone upon my","handiwork."],"id":13,"m":"Honestly, sir, all I live by is the awl. I don't meddle in other tradesmen's business or in women's business, but I am, sir, a surgeon to old shoes: when they're in great danger, I bring them back to health. As fine men as ever walked on cowhide have walked on my work."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["But wherefore art not in thy shop today?","Why dost thou lead these men about the streets?"],"id":14,"m":"But why aren't you in your shop today? Why are you leading these men about the streets?"},{"t":"sp","who":"COBBLER","lines":["Truly, sir, to wear out their shoes, to get myself into more work. But","indeed, sir, we make holiday to see Caesar, and to rejoice in his","triumph."],"id":15,"m":"Honestly, sir, to wear out their shoes and get myself more work. But really, sir, we're taking a holiday to see Caesar and celebrate his triumph."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARULLUS","lines":["Wherefore rejoice? What conquest brings he home?","What tributaries follow him to Rome,","To grace in captive bonds his chariot wheels?","You blocks, you stones, you worse than senseless things!","O you hard hearts, you cruel men of Rome,","Knew you not Pompey? Many a time and oft","Have you climb'd up to walls and battlements,","To towers and windows, yea, to chimney tops,","Your infants in your arms, and there have sat","The livelong day with patient expectation,","To see great Pompey pass the streets of Rome.","And when you saw his chariot but appear,","Have you not made an universal shout,","That Tiber trembled underneath her banks","To hear the replication of your sounds","Made in her concave shores?","And do you now put on your best attire?","And do you now cull out a holiday?","And do you now strew flowers in his way,","That comes in triumph over Pompey's blood?","Be gone!","Run to your houses, fall upon your knees,","Pray to the gods to intermit the plague","That needs must light on this ingratitude."],"id":16,"m":"Celebrate what? What conquest is he bringing home? What captive kings follow him to Rome, chained to his chariot wheels to decorate his parade? You blocks, you stones, you are worse than senseless things! You hard-hearted, cruel men of Rome, didn't you know Pompey? Many times you climbed up onto walls and battlements, towers and windows, even chimney tops, with your babies in your arms, and sat there all day waiting patiently to see great Pompey pass through the streets of Rome. And when you saw just his chariot appear, didn't you shout together so loudly that the Tiber trembled in its banks to hear the echo of your noise in its hollow shores? And now you put on your best clothes? And now you pick out a holiday? And now you scatter flowers in the path of the man who comes in triumph over Pompey's blood? Go! Run home, fall on your knees, and pray to the gods to hold back the punishment that is sure to fall on such ingratitude."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Go, go, good countrymen, and, for this fault","Assemble all the poor men of your sort,","Draw them to Tiber banks, and weep your tears","Into the channel, till the lowest stream","Do kiss the most exalted shores of all."],"id":17,"m":"Go, go, good countrymen, and to make up for this fault, gather all the poor men like you, take them to the banks of the Tiber, and weep your tears into the river until even its lowest water rises to touch the top of its banks."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Citizens","id":18},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","cont":1,"lines":["See whether their basest metal be not mov'd;","They vanish tongue-tied in their guiltiness.","Go you down that way towards the Capitol;","This way will I. Disrobe the images,","If you do find them deck'd with ceremonies."],"id":19,"m":"See if even their dull metal isn't moved. They slink away, tongue-tied with guilt. You go down that way towards the Capitol; I'll go this way. Strip the statues if you find them decorated for the celebrations."},{"t":"sp","who":"MARULLUS","lines":["May we do so?","You know it is the feast of Lupercal."],"id":20,"m":"Are we allowed to? You know it's the feast of Lupercal."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["It is no matter; let no images","Be hung with Caesar's trophies. I'll about","And drive away the vulgar from the streets;","So do you too, where you perceive them thick.","These growing feathers pluck'd from Caesar's wing","Will make him fly an ordinary pitch,","Who else would soar above the view of men,","And keep us all in servile fearfulness."],"id":21,"m":"It doesn't matter. Don't let any statues be hung with Caesar's trophies. I'll go round and clear the common people off the streets, and you do the same wherever you see them crowding. If we pluck these growing feathers from Caesar's wing, he'll fly at an ordinary height. Otherwise he would soar out of sight of men and keep us all in fear like slaves."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":22},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. A public place.","id":23},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter, in procession, with music, Caesar; Antony, for the course; Calphurnia, Portia, Decius, Cicero, Brutus, Cassius and Casca; a great crowd following, among them a Soothsayer.","id":24},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Calphurnia."],"id":25,"m":"Calphurnia."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Peace, ho! Caesar speaks."],"id":26,"m":"Quiet! Caesar is speaking."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music ceases","id":27},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Calphurnia."],"id":28,"m":"Calphurnia."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALPHURNIA","lines":["Here, my lord."],"id":29,"m":"Here, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Stand you directly in Antonius' way,","When he doth run his course. Antonius."],"id":30,"m":"Stand right in Antonius's path when he runs his race. Antonius."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Caesar, my lord?"],"id":31,"m":"Caesar, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Forget not in your speed, Antonius,","To touch Calphurnia; for our elders say,","The barren, touched in this holy chase,","Shake off their sterile curse."],"id":32,"m":"Don't forget, Antonius, as you run, to touch Calphurnia. Our elders say that childless women who are touched in this holy race lose their curse of barrenness."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["I shall remember.","When Caesar says \"Do this,\" it is perform'd."],"id":33,"m":"I'll remember. When Caesar says \"Do this,\" it is done."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Set on; and leave no ceremony out."],"id":34,"m":"Go on, and leave out none of the ceremonies."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music","id":35},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Caesar!"],"id":36,"m":"Caesar!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Ha! Who calls?"],"id":37,"m":"Ha! Who's calling?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Bid every noise be still; peace yet again!"],"id":38,"m":"Make every noise stop. Quiet again!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Music ceases","id":39},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Who is it in the press that calls on me?","I hear a tongue shriller than all the music,","Cry \"Caesar\"! Speak. Caesar is turn'd to hear."],"id":40,"m":"Who in the crowd is calling me? I hear a voice shriller than all the music cry \"Caesar!\" Speak. Caesar has turned to listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Beware the Ides of March."],"id":41,"m":"Beware the fifteenth of March."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["What man is that?"],"id":42,"m":"Who is that man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["A soothsayer bids you beware the Ides of March."],"id":43,"m":"A fortune-teller tells you to beware the fifteenth of March."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Set him before me; let me see his face."],"id":44,"m":"Bring him in front of me. Let me see his face."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Fellow, come from the throng; look upon Caesar."],"id":45,"m":"You, come out of the crowd. Look at Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["What say'st thou to me now? Speak once again."],"id":46,"m":"What do you say to me now? Say it again."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Beware the Ides of March."],"id":47,"m":"Beware the fifteenth of March."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["He is a dreamer; let us leave him. Pass."],"id":48,"m":"He's a dreamer. Let's leave him. Move on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sennet. Exeunt all but Brutus and Cassius","id":49},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Will you go see the order of the course?"],"id":50,"m":"Are you going to watch the race?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Not I."],"id":51,"m":"Not me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I pray you, do."],"id":52,"m":"Please, do."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I am not gamesome: I do lack some part","Of that quick spirit that is in Antony.","Let me not hinder, Cassius, your desires;","I'll leave you."],"id":53,"m":"I'm not in the mood for games. I lack some of the lively spirit that Antony has. Don't let me stop you doing what you want, Cassius. I'll leave you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Brutus, I do observe you now of late:","I have not from your eyes that gentleness","And show of love as I was wont to have.","You bear too stubborn and too strange a hand","Over your friend that loves you."],"id":54,"m":"Brutus, I've been watching you lately. I don't see the gentleness and signs of affection in your eyes that I used to. You're too cold and distant with a friend who loves you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Cassius,","Be not deceived: if I have veil'd my look,","I turn the trouble of my countenance","Merely upon myself. Vexed I am","Of late with passions of some difference,","Conceptions only proper to myself,","Which give some soil perhaps to my behaviors;","But let not therefore my good friends be grieved","(Among which number, Cassius, be you one)","Nor construe any further my neglect,","Than that poor Brutus, with himself at war,","Forgets the shows of love to other men."],"id":55,"m":"Cassius, don't be misled. If I've looked troubled, my frown is aimed only at myself. Lately I have been troubled by conflicting feelings, thoughts that concern only me, which perhaps spoil my behaviour a little. But don't let my good friends be upset by it (and you are one of them, Cassius), or read anything more into my neglect than that poor Brutus, at war with himself, forgets to show his affection to other men."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Then, Brutus, I have much mistook your passion;","By means whereof this breast of mine hath buried","Thoughts of great value, worthy cogitations.","Tell me, good Brutus, can you see your face?"],"id":56,"m":"Then, Brutus, I have badly misread your feelings, and because of that I have kept to myself some thoughts of great importance, very serious ideas. Tell me, good Brutus, can you see your own face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No, Cassius, for the eye sees not itself","But by reflection, by some other thing."],"id":57,"m":"No, Cassius, because the eye can't see itself except by reflection, in something else."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["'Tis just:","And it is very much lamented, Brutus,","That you have no such mirrors as will turn","Your hidden worthiness into your eye,","That you might see your shadow. I have heard","Where many of the best respect in Rome,","(Except immortal Caesar) speaking of Brutus,","And groaning underneath this age's yoke,","Have wish'd that noble Brutus had his eyes."],"id":58,"m":"That's right. And it's a great pity, Brutus, that you have no mirrors that could show your hidden worth to your own eyes, so you could see your reflection. I have heard many of the most respected men in Rome (except immortal Caesar), speaking of Brutus and groaning under the oppression of these times, wish that noble Brutus could see himself as they do."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Into what dangers would you lead me, Cassius,","That you would have me seek into myself","For that which is not in me?"],"id":59,"m":"What danger are you leading me into, Cassius, that you want me to look inside myself for something that isn't there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Therefore, good Brutus, be prepared to hear;","And since you know you cannot see yourself","So well as by reflection, I, your glass,","Will modestly discover to yourself","That of yourself which you yet know not of.","And be not jealous on me, gentle Brutus:","Were I a common laugher, or did use","To stale with ordinary oaths my love","To every new protester; if you know","That I do fawn on men, and hug them hard,","And after scandal them; or if you know","That I profess myself in banqueting,","To all the rout, then hold me dangerous."],"id":60,"m":"So, good Brutus, get ready to listen. And since you know you can't see yourself as well as by reflection, I will be your mirror and modestly show you the part of yourself you don't yet know about. And don't be suspicious of me, gentle Brutus. If I were a common joker, or cheapened my friendship by swearing it to every new person who claimed to be my friend; if you know that I flatter men and hug them hard and then slander them behind their backs; or if you know that I declare friendship to all the riff-raff when I'm feasting, then consider me dangerous."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish and shout","id":61},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What means this shouting? I do fear the people","Choose Caesar for their king."],"id":62,"m":"What's this shouting for? I'm afraid the people are choosing Caesar as their king."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Ay, do you fear it?","Then must I think you would not have it so."],"id":63,"m":"Oh, are you afraid of it? Then I must think you wouldn't want it to happen."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I would not, Cassius; yet I love him well,","But wherefore do you hold me here so long?","What is it that you would impart to me?","If it be aught toward the general good,","Set honour in one eye and death i' the other,","And I will look on both indifferently;","For let the gods so speed me as I love","The name of honour more than I fear death."],"id":64,"m":"I wouldn't, Cassius. And yet I love him well. But why are you keeping me here so long? What is it you want to tell me? If it is anything for the public good, set honour in front of one eye and death in front of the other, and I will look at both without caring which. Let the gods only bless me as much as I love the name of honour more than I fear death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I know that virtue to be in you, Brutus,","As well as I do know your outward favour.","Well, honour is the subject of my story.","I cannot tell what you and other men","Think of this life; but, for my single self,","I had as lief not be as live to be","In awe of such a thing as I myself.","I was born free as Caesar; so were you;","We both have fed as well, and we can both","Endure the winter's cold as well as he:","For once, upon a raw and gusty day,","The troubled Tiber chafing with her shores,","Caesar said to me, \"Dar'st thou, Cassius, now","Leap in with me into this angry flood,","And swim to yonder point?\" Upon the word,","Accoutred as I was, I plunged in,","And bade him follow: so indeed he did.","The torrent roar'd, and we did buffet it","With lusty sinews, throwing it aside","And stemming it with hearts of controversy.","But ere we could arrive the point propos'd,","Caesar cried, \"Help me, Cassius, or I sink!\"","I, as Aeneas, our great ancestor,","Did from the flames of Troy upon his shoulder","The old Anchises bear, so from the waves of Tiber","Did I the tired Caesar. And this man","Is now become a god; and Cassius is","A wretched creature, and must bend his body,","If Caesar carelessly but nod on him.","He had a fever when he was in Spain,","And when the fit was on him I did mark","How he did shake: 'tis true, this god did shake:","His coward lips did from their colour fly,","And that same eye whose bend doth awe the world","Did lose his lustre. I did hear him groan:","Ay, and that tongue of his, that bade the Romans","Mark him, and write his speeches in their books,","Alas, it cried, \"Give me some drink, Titinius,\"","As a sick girl. Ye gods, it doth amaze me,","A man of such a feeble temper should","So get the start of the majestic world,","And bear the palm alone."],"id":65,"m":"I know you have that virtue, Brutus, as well as I know your face. Well, honour is the subject of my story. I can't say what you and other men think of this life, but for my part I would rather not exist than live in awe of a man no better than I am. I was born as free as Caesar, and so were you. We have both eaten as well as he has, and we can both stand the winter cold as well as he can. Once, on a raw, windy day, when the stormy Tiber was crashing against its banks, Caesar said to me, \"Do you dare, Cassius, to leap into this angry river with me now and swim to that point over there?\" At that, dressed as I was, I plunged in and told him to follow, and so he did. The torrent roared, and we fought it with strong muscles, throwing it aside and pushing against it with competitive hearts. But before we could reach the point, Caesar cried, \"Help me, Cassius, or I'll sink!\" Just as Aeneas, our great ancestor, carried old Anchises out of the flames of Troy on his shoulder, so I carried the tired Caesar out of the waves of the Tiber. And this man has now become a god, and Cassius is a wretched creature who must bow down if Caesar even carelessly nods at him. He had a fever when he was in Spain, and when the fit was on him I saw how he shook. It's true: this god shook. His cowardly lips lost their colour, and that same eye whose glance terrifies the world lost its shine. I heard him groan. Yes, and that tongue of his, which ordered the Romans to listen to him and write his speeches down in their books, cried, \"Give me a drink, Titinius,\" like a sick girl. You gods, it amazes me that a man with such a weak constitution should get ahead of the whole majestic world and carry off the victor's prize alone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Shout. Flourish","id":66},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Another general shout?","I do believe that these applauses are","For some new honours that are heap'd on Caesar."],"id":67,"m":"Another shout from everyone? I believe this applause is for some new honours being heaped on Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Why, man, he doth bestride the narrow world","Like a Colossus, and we petty men","Walk under his huge legs, and peep about","To find ourselves dishonourable graves.","Men at some time are masters of their fates:","The fault, dear Brutus, is not in our stars,","But in ourselves, that we are underlings.","\"Brutus\" and \"Caesar\": what should be in that \"Caesar\"?","Why should that name be sounded more than yours?","Write them together, yours is as fair a name;","Sound them, it doth become the mouth as well;","Weigh them, it is as heavy; conjure with 'em,","\"Brutus\" will start a spirit as soon as \"Caesar.\"","Now in the names of all the gods at once,","Upon what meat doth this our Caesar feed,","That he is grown so great? Age, thou art sham'd!","Rome, thou hast lost the breed of noble bloods!","When went there by an age since the great flood,","But it was fam'd with more than with one man?","When could they say, till now, that talk'd of Rome,","That her wide walls encompass'd but one man?","Now is it Rome indeed, and room enough,","When there is in it but one only man.","O, you and I have heard our fathers say,","There was a Brutus once that would have brook'd","Th' eternal devil to keep his state in Rome,","As easily as a king!"],"id":68,"m":"Why, man, he stands over the narrow world like a giant statue, and we little men walk under his huge legs and peer about to find ourselves shameful graves. At some point men are masters of their own fates. The fault, dear Brutus, is not in our stars but in ourselves, that we are underlings. \"Brutus\" and \"Caesar\": what is so special about \"Caesar\"? Why should that name be spoken more than yours? Write them side by side: yours is just as fine a name. Say them: yours sounds as good. Weigh them: it's as heavy. Use them in spells: \"Brutus\" will call up a spirit as fast as \"Caesar.\" Now, in the names of all the gods at once, what food does this Caesar of ours eat that makes him grow so great? Our age should be ashamed! Rome, you have lost the breed of noble men! Since the great flood, was there ever an age that wasn't famous for more than one man? Until now, when could anyone talking of Rome say that her wide walls held only one man? Now it really is Rome, and room enough, when there's only one man in it. Oh, you and I have heard our fathers say that there was once a Brutus who would have let the devil himself rule in Rome as easily as he would have allowed a king."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["That you do love me, I am nothing jealous;","What you would work me to, I have some aim:","How I have thought of this, and of these times,","I shall recount hereafter. For this present,","I would not, so with love I might entreat you,","Be any further mov'd. What you have said,","I will consider; what you have to say","I will with patience hear; and find a time","Both meet to hear and answer such high things.","Till then, my noble friend, chew upon this:","Brutus had rather be a villager","Than to repute himself a son of Rome","Under these hard conditions as this time","Is like to lay upon us."],"id":69,"m":"I don't doubt at all that you love me. I have some idea what you want to persuade me to do. What I think about this, and about these times, I'll tell you later. For now, I ask you as a friend, don't push me any further. I will think about what you have said; I will listen patiently to whatever else you have to say, and find a suitable time both to hear and answer such serious matters. Until then, my noble friend, think about this: Brutus would rather be a villager than call himself a son of Rome under the hard conditions these times are likely to put on us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I am glad that my weak words","Have struck but thus much show of fire from Brutus."],"id":70,"m":"I'm glad my weak words have struck even this much fire from Brutus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Caesar and his Train.","id":71},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The games are done, and Caesar is returning."],"id":72,"m":"The games are over, and Caesar is coming back."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["As they pass by, pluck Casca by the sleeve,","And he will, after his sour fashion, tell you","What hath proceeded worthy note today."],"id":73,"m":"As they pass by, tug Casca's sleeve, and in his sour way he'll tell you what happened today that's worth knowing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I will do so. But, look you, Cassius,","The angry spot doth glow on Caesar's brow,","And all the rest look like a chidden train:","Calphurnia's cheek is pale; and Cicero","Looks with such ferret and such fiery eyes","As we have seen him in the Capitol,","Being cross'd in conference by some senators."],"id":74,"m":"I will. But look, Cassius, an angry red spot glows on Caesar's forehead, and all the rest look like servants who have been told off. Calphurnia's cheek is pale, and Cicero has the red, fiery eyes of a ferret, as we've seen him in the Capitol when senators disagree with him in debate."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Casca will tell us what the matter is."],"id":75,"m":"Casca will tell us what's wrong."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Antonius."],"id":76,"m":"Antonius."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Caesar?"],"id":77,"m":"Caesar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Let me have men about me that are fat,","Sleek-headed men, and such as sleep a-nights:","Yond Cassius has a lean and hungry look;","He thinks too much: such men are dangerous."],"id":78,"m":"I want men around me who are fat, men with smooth, well-groomed heads who sleep at night. That Cassius over there has a lean and hungry look. He thinks too much. Men like that are dangerous."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Fear him not, Caesar; he's not dangerous;","He is a noble Roman and well given."],"id":79,"m":"Don't be afraid of him, Caesar, he's not dangerous. He's a noble Roman and well disposed to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Would he were fatter! But I fear him not:","Yet if my name were liable to fear,","I do not know the man I should avoid","So soon as that spare Cassius. He reads much,","He is a great observer, and he looks","Quite through the deeds of men. He loves no plays,","As thou dost, Antony; he hears no music.","Seldom he smiles; and smiles in such a sort","As if he mock'd himself and scorn'd his spirit","That could be mov'd to smile at anything.","Such men as he be never at heart's ease","Whiles they behold a greater than themselves,","And therefore are they very dangerous.","I rather tell thee what is to be fear'd","Than what I fear; for always I am Caesar.","Come on my right hand, for this ear is deaf,","And tell me truly what thou think'st of him."],"id":80,"m":"I wish he were fatter! But I'm not afraid of him. Yet if Caesar could feel fear, I don't know any man I would avoid sooner than that thin Cassius. He reads a lot, he watches everything, and he sees right through what men do to their motives. He doesn't like plays, as you do, Antony; he doesn't listen to music. He rarely smiles, and when he does, it's as if he were mocking himself and despising his own spirit for being moved to smile at anything. Men like him are never comfortable while they see someone greater than themselves, and that's why they are very dangerous. I'm telling you what ought to be feared, not what I fear, because I am always Caesar. Come round to my right side, because this ear is deaf, and tell me honestly what you think of him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Caesar and his Train. Casca stays","id":81},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["You pull'd me by the cloak; would you speak with me?"],"id":82,"m":"You pulled my cloak. Do you want to talk to me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Ay, Casca, tell us what hath chanc'd today,","That Caesar looks so sad."],"id":83,"m":"Yes, Casca. Tell us what happened today to make Caesar look so serious."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Why, you were with him, were you not?"],"id":84,"m":"Why, you were with him, weren't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I should not then ask Casca what had chanc'd."],"id":85,"m":"Then I wouldn't be asking Casca what happened."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Why, there was a crown offer'd him; and being offer'd him, he put it by","with the back of his hand, thus; and then the people fell a-shouting."],"id":86,"m":"Well, he was offered a crown, and when it was offered he pushed it away with the back of his hand, like this, and then the people started shouting."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What was the second noise for?"],"id":87,"m":"What was the second shout for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Why, for that too."],"id":88,"m":"The same thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["They shouted thrice: what was the last cry for?"],"id":89,"m":"They shouted three times. What was the last cheer for?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Why, for that too."],"id":90,"m":"The same thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Was the crown offer'd him thrice?"],"id":91,"m":"Was he offered the crown three times?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Ay, marry, was't, and he put it by thrice, every time gentler than","other; and at every putting-by mine honest neighbours shouted."],"id":92,"m":"Yes, by God, he was, and he pushed it away three times, each time more gently than the last, and every time he pushed it away my honest neighbours shouted."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Who offer'd him the crown?"],"id":93,"m":"Who offered him the crown?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Why, Antony."],"id":94,"m":"Why, Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Tell us the manner of it, gentle Casca."],"id":95,"m":"Tell us how it happened, gentle Casca."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["I can as well be hang'd, as tell the manner of it: it was mere foolery;","I did not mark it. I saw Mark Antony offer him a crown; yet 'twas not a","crown neither, 'twas one of these coronets; and, as I told you, he put","it by once: but, for all that, to my thinking, he would fain have had","it. Then he offered it to him again: then he put it by again: but, to","my thinking, he was very loath to lay his fingers off it. And then he","offered it the third time; he put it the third time by; and still, as","he refus'd it, the rabblement hooted, and clapp'd their chopt hands,","and threw up their sweaty night-caps, and uttered such a deal of","stinking breath because Caesar refus'd the crown, that it had, almost,","choked Caesar, for he swooned, and fell down at it. And for mine own","part, I durst not laugh, for fear of opening my lips and receiving the","bad air."],"id":96,"m":"I might as well be hanged as tell you how it happened. It was pure nonsense; I didn't pay attention. I saw Mark Antony offer him a crown, though it wasn't really a crown, it was one of those little coronets, and, as I told you, he pushed it away once. But all the same, I think he'd have liked to have it. Then Antony offered it to him again, and he pushed it away again, but I think he was very unwilling to take his fingers off it. And then Antony offered it the third time, and he pushed it away the third time. And each time he refused it, the mob hooted and clapped their chapped hands and threw up their sweaty nightcaps, and let out such a quantity of stinking breath because Caesar refused the crown that it almost choked Caesar, because he fainted and fell down from it. And for my part, I didn't dare laugh, for fear of opening my lips and breathing in the bad air."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["But, soft! I pray you. What, did Caesar swoon?"],"id":97,"m":"Wait, please. What, did Caesar faint?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["He fell down in the market-place, and foam'd at mouth, and was","speechless."],"id":98,"m":"He fell down in the market-place, foamed at the mouth and couldn't speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["'Tis very like: he hath the falling-sickness."],"id":99,"m":"That's very likely. He has epilepsy, the falling sickness."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["No, Caesar hath it not; but you, and I,","And honest Casca, we have the falling-sickness."],"id":100,"m":"No, Caesar doesn't have it. But you and I and honest Casca, we have the falling sickness."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["I know not what you mean by that; but I am sure Caesar fell down. If","the tag-rag people did not clap him and hiss him, according as he","pleased and displeased them, as they use to do the players in the","theatre, I am no true man."],"id":101,"m":"I don't know what you mean by that, but I'm sure Caesar fell down. If the ragged crowd didn't clap and hiss him according to whether he pleased or displeased them, just as they do to actors in the theatre, I'm no honest man."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What said he when he came unto himself?"],"id":102,"m":"What did he say when he came round?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Marry, before he fell down, when he perceived the common herd was glad","he refused the crown, he pluck'd me ope his doublet, and offer'd them","his throat to cut. And I had been a man of any occupation, if I would","not have taken him at a word, I would I might go to hell among the","rogues. And so he fell. When he came to himself again, he said, if he","had done or said anything amiss, he desir'd their worships to think it","was his infirmity. Three or four wenches where I stood cried, \"Alas,","good soul!\" and forgave him with all their hearts. But there's no heed","to be taken of them: if Caesar had stabb'd their mothers, they would","have done no less."],"id":103,"m":"Well, before he fell down, when he saw the common herd was glad he refused the crown, he pulled open his jacket and offered them his throat to cut. If I had been a working man with a tool in my hand, I'd have taken him at his word, or I hope I go to hell with the scoundrels. And so he fell. When he came round again, he said that if he had done or said anything wrong, he wanted their worships to put it down to his illness. Three or four women near me cried, \"Oh, the poor man!\" and forgave him with all their hearts. But you can't take any notice of them: if Caesar had stabbed their mothers, they would have done the same."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["And, after that, he came thus sad away?"],"id":104,"m":"And after that he came away this serious?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Ay."],"id":105,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Did Cicero say anything?"],"id":106,"m":"Did Cicero say anything?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Ay, he spoke Greek."],"id":107,"m":"Yes, he spoke Greek."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["To what effect?"],"id":108,"m":"What did he mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Nay, and I tell you that, I'll ne'er look you i' the face again. But","those that understood him smil'd at one another and shook their heads;","but for mine own part, it was Greek to me. I could tell you more news","too: Marullus and Flavius, for pulling scarfs off Caesar's images, are","put to silence. Fare you well. There was more foolery yet, if I could","remember it."],"id":109,"m":"No, if I told you that, I'd never be able to look you in the face again. But those who understood him smiled at one another and shook their heads. As for me, it was Greek to me. I could tell you more news, too: Marullus and Flavius have been silenced for pulling the scarves off Caesar's statues. Goodbye. There was more foolery still, if I could remember it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Will you sup with me tonight, Casca?"],"id":110,"m":"Will you have supper with me tonight, Casca?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["No, I am promis'd forth."],"id":111,"m":"No, I've already promised to go out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Will you dine with me tomorrow?"],"id":112,"m":"Will you have dinner with me tomorrow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Ay, if I be alive, and your mind hold, and your dinner worth the","eating."],"id":113,"m":"Yes, if I'm alive, and you don't change your mind, and your dinner is worth eating."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Good. I will expect you."],"id":114,"m":"Good. I'll expect you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Do so; farewell both."],"id":115,"m":"Do. Goodbye to you both."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Casca","id":116},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What a blunt fellow is this grown to be!","He was quick mettle when he went to school."],"id":117,"m":"What a blunt fellow he has turned into! He was so sharp when he was at school."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["So is he now in execution","Of any bold or noble enterprise,","However he puts on this tardy form.","This rudeness is a sauce to his good wit,","Which gives men stomach to digest his words","With better appetite."],"id":118,"m":"He still is when it comes to carrying out any bold or noble plan, though he puts on this slow manner. His rudeness is a sauce for his good sense: it gives people the appetite to swallow what he says with more pleasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["And so it is. For this time I will leave you:","Tomorrow, if you please to speak with me,","I will come home to you; or, if you will,","Come home to me, and I will wait for you."],"id":119,"m":"That's true. I'll leave you for now. Tomorrow, if you want to talk to me, I'll come to your house; or, if you like, come to mine and I'll wait for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I will do so: till then, think of the world."],"id":120,"m":"I will. Until then, think about the state of the world."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Brutus","id":121},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Well, Brutus, thou art noble; yet I see,","Thy honourable metal may be wrought","From that it is dispos'd: therefore 'tis meet","That noble minds keep ever with their likes;","For who so firm that cannot be seduc'd?","Caesar doth bear me hard, but he loves Brutus.","If I were Brutus now, and he were Cassius,","He should not humour me. I will this night,","In several hands, in at his windows throw,","As if they came from several citizens,","Writings, all tending to the great opinion","That Rome holds of his name; wherein obscurely","Caesar's ambition shall be glanced at.","And after this, let Caesar seat him sure,","For we will shake him, or worse days endure."],"id":122,"m":"Well, Brutus, you are noble. And yet I see your honourable metal can be bent away from its natural shape. So it's right that noble minds should always keep company with their own kind, because who is so firm that he can't be led astray? Caesar holds a grudge against me, but he loves Brutus. If I were Brutus now and he were Cassius, he wouldn't win me round. Tonight I will throw letters in through his windows in different handwriting, as if they came from different citizens, all about the high opinion Rome has of him, and hinting at Caesar's ambition. And after this, let Caesar sit tight, because we will shake him off, or suffer worse times."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":123},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A street.","id":124},{"t":"sd","text":"Thunder and lightning. Enter, from opposite sides, Casca with his sword drawn, and Cicero.","id":125},{"t":"sp","who":"CICERO","lines":["Good even, Casca: brought you Caesar home?","Why are you breathless, and why stare you so?"],"id":126,"m":"Good evening, Casca. Did you bring Caesar home? Why are you out of breath, and why are you staring like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Are not you moved, when all the sway of earth","Shakes like a thing unfirm? O Cicero,","I have seen tempests, when the scolding winds","Have riv'd the knotty oaks; and I have seen","Th' ambitious ocean swell and rage and foam,","To be exalted with the threatening clouds:","But never till tonight, never till now,","Did I go through a tempest dropping fire.","Either there is a civil strife in heaven,","Or else the world too saucy with the gods,","Incenses them to send destruction."],"id":127,"m":"Aren't you disturbed when the whole earth shakes as if it were unsteady? Oh, Cicero, I have seen storms when the howling winds have split the gnarled oaks, and I have seen the ambitious ocean swell and rage and foam to reach as high as the threatening clouds. But never until tonight, never until now, have I walked through a storm raining fire. Either there is civil war in heaven, or else the world has been so insolent to the gods that it has provoked them to destroy it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CICERO","lines":["Why, saw you anything more wonderful?"],"id":128,"m":"Why, did you see anything more amazing?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["A common slave, you'd know him well by sight,","Held up his left hand, which did flame and burn","Like twenty torches join'd, and yet his hand,","Not sensible of fire remain'd unscorch'd.","Besides, I ha' not since put up my sword,","Against the Capitol I met a lion,","Who glared upon me, and went surly by,","Without annoying me. And there were drawn","Upon a heap a hundred ghastly women,","Transformed with their fear; who swore they saw","Men, all in fire, walk up and down the streets.","And yesterday the bird of night did sit,","Even at noonday upon the marketplace,","Hooting and shrieking. When these prodigies","Do so conjointly meet, let not men say,","\"These are their reasons; they are natural\";","For I believe, they are portentous things","Unto the climate that they point upon."],"id":129,"m":"A common slave, you'd know him by sight, held up his left hand, which flamed and burned like twenty torches put together, and yet his hand felt no fire and stayed unburnt. Besides that (I haven't put my sword away since), by the Capitol I met a lion, which glared at me and walked sullenly past without attacking me. And a hundred terrified women were huddled in a heap, changed out of all recognition by fear, who swore they saw men all on fire walking up and down the streets. And yesterday the owl, the bird of night, sat in the market-place even at midday, hooting and shrieking. When these unnatural signs all happen together, don't let anyone say, \"There are reasons for them, they're natural,\" because I believe they are omens of disaster for the country they point to."},{"t":"sp","who":"CICERO","lines":["Indeed, it is a strange-disposed time.","But men may construe things after their fashion,","Clean from the purpose of the things themselves.","Comes Caesar to the Capitol tomorrow?"],"id":130,"m":"It certainly is a strange time. But men can interpret things in their own way, quite differently from what the things really mean. Is Caesar coming to the Capitol tomorrow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["He doth, for he did bid Antonius","Send word to you he would be there tomorrow."],"id":131,"m":"He is. He told Antonius to send you word that he would be there tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"CICERO","lines":["Goodnight then, Casca: this disturbed sky","Is not to walk in."],"id":132,"m":"Good night then, Casca. This stormy sky is no weather to walk in."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Farewell, Cicero."],"id":133,"m":"Goodbye, Cicero."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Cicero","id":134},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cassius.","id":135},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Who's there?"],"id":136,"m":"Who's there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["A Roman."],"id":137,"m":"A Roman."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Casca, by your voice."],"id":138,"m":"Casca, by your voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Your ear is good. Cassius, what night is this!"],"id":139,"m":"You've got a good ear. Cassius, what a night this is!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["A very pleasing night to honest men."],"id":140,"m":"A very pleasant night for honest men."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Who ever knew the heavens menace so?"],"id":141,"m":"Whoever saw the heavens so threatening?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Those that have known the earth so full of faults.","For my part, I have walk'd about the streets,","Submitting me unto the perilous night;","And, thus unbraced, Casca, as you see,","Have bar'd my bosom to the thunder-stone;","And when the cross blue lightning seem'd to open","The breast of heaven, I did present myself","Even in the aim and very flash of it."],"id":142,"m":"Those who have known the earth to be so full of wrongs. For my part, I have walked about the streets exposing myself to the dangerous night, and, with my shirt undone like this, Casca, as you see, I have bared my chest to the thunderbolts. And when the jagged blue lightning seemed to split open the sky, I put myself right where it was aimed, in its very flash."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["But wherefore did you so much tempt the Heavens?","It is the part of men to fear and tremble,","When the most mighty gods by tokens send","Such dreadful heralds to astonish us."],"id":143,"m":"But why did you tempt the heavens like that? It is right for men to fear and tremble when the mighty gods send such dreadful messengers as signs to terrify us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["You are dull, Casca; and those sparks of life","That should be in a Roman you do want,","Or else you use not. You look pale and gaze,","And put on fear and cast yourself in wonder,","To see the strange impatience of the Heavens:","But if you would consider the true cause","Why all these fires, why all these gliding ghosts,","Why birds and beasts, from quality and kind;","Why old men, fools, and children calculate,","Why all these things change from their ordinance,","Their natures, and pre-formed faculties,","To monstrous quality; why, you shall find","That Heaven hath infus'd them with these spirits,","To make them instruments of fear and warning","Unto some monstrous state.","Now could I, Casca, name to thee a man","Most like this dreadful night,","That thunders, lightens, opens graves, and roars,","As doth the lion in the Capitol;","A man no mightier than thyself, or me,","In personal action; yet prodigious grown,","And fearful, as these strange eruptions are."],"id":144,"m":"You are slow, Casca, and you either lack the spark of life a Roman should have or you don't use it. You look pale and stare, and you are afraid and amazed to see the heavens so strangely angry. But if you thought about the real reason why there are all these fires, all these gliding ghosts, why birds and animals act against their nature, why old men, fools and children make prophecies, why all these things change from their normal ways and their natural abilities to become monstrous, you would find that heaven has filled them with these spirits to make them instruments of fear, warning of a monstrous state of affairs. Now, Casca, I could name a man to you who is very like this dreadful night, who thunders, flashes, opens graves and roars like the lion in the Capitol: a man no stronger than you or me in himself, and yet grown huge and frightening, like these strange outbreaks."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["'Tis Caesar that you mean; is it not, Cassius?"],"id":145,"m":"It's Caesar you mean, isn't it, Cassius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Let it be who it is: for Romans now","Have thews and limbs like to their ancestors;","But, woe the while! our fathers' minds are dead,","And we are govern'd with our mothers' spirits;","Our yoke and sufferance show us womanish."],"id":146,"m":"Let it be whoever it is. Romans today have muscles and limbs like their ancestors, but, alas for our times, our fathers' minds are dead, and we are governed by our mothers' spirits. The way we put up with our yoke shows we are like women."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Indeed, they say the senators tomorrow","Mean to establish Caesar as a king;","And he shall wear his crown by sea and land,","In every place, save here in Italy."],"id":147,"m":"In fact, they say the senators mean to make Caesar king tomorrow, and he will wear his crown on sea and land, everywhere except here in Italy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I know where I will wear this dagger then;","Cassius from bondage will deliver Cassius:","Therein, ye gods, you make the weak most strong;","Therein, ye gods, you tyrants do defeat.","Nor stony tower, nor walls of beaten brass,","Nor airless dungeon, nor strong links of iron,","Can be retentive to the strength of spirit;","But life, being weary of these worldly bars,","Never lacks power to dismiss itself.","If I know this, know all the world besides,","That part of tyranny that I do bear","I can shake off at pleasure."],"id":148,"m":"Then I know where I'll put this dagger: Cassius will free Cassius from slavery. That is how, you gods, you make the weak strongest; that is how, you gods, you defeat tyrants. No stone tower, no walls of hammered brass, no airless dungeon, no strong iron chains can imprison a determined spirit. Life, when it is tired of these earthly prison bars, always has the power to set itself free. If I know this, let the rest of the world know too: the share of tyranny I suffer I can throw off whenever I like."},{"t":"sd","text":"Thunder still","id":149},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["So can I:","So every bondman in his own hand bears","The power to cancel his captivity."],"id":150,"m":"So can I. Every slave holds in his own hand the power to end his captivity."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["And why should Caesar be a tyrant then?","Poor man! I know he would not be a wolf,","But that he sees the Romans are but sheep:","He were no lion, were not Romans hinds.","Those that with haste will make a mighty fire","Begin it with weak straws. What trash is Rome,","What rubbish, and what offal, when it serves","For the base matter to illuminate","So vile a thing as Caesar! But, O grief,","Where hast thou led me? I, perhaps, speak this","Before a willing bondman: then I know","My answer must be made; but I am arm'd,","And dangers are to me indifferent."],"id":151,"m":"Then why should Caesar be a tyrant? Poor man! I know he wouldn't be a wolf if he didn't see that the Romans are only sheep. He wouldn't be a lion if the Romans weren't deer. People who want to make a great fire in a hurry start it with feeble straws. What trash Rome is, what rubbish, what garbage, when it serves as the worthless fuel to light up something as vile as Caesar! But, oh grief, where have you led me? Perhaps I am saying this in front of a willing slave. Then I know I'll have to answer for it. But I am armed, and dangers mean nothing to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["You speak to Casca, and to such a man","That is no fleering tell-tale. Hold, my hand:","Be factious for redress of all these griefs,","And I will set this foot of mine as far","As who goes farthest."],"id":152,"m":"You are speaking to Casca, and he is no sneering tell-tale. Here, take my hand. Form a party to put right all these wrongs, and I will go as far as anyone goes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["There's a bargain made.","Now know you, Casca, I have mov'd already","Some certain of the noblest-minded Romans","To undergo with me an enterprise","Of honourable-dangerous consequence;","And I do know by this, they stay for me","In Pompey's Porch: for now, this fearful night,","There is no stir or walking in the streets;","And the complexion of the element","In favour's like the work we have in hand,","Most bloody, fiery, and most terrible."],"id":153,"m":"That's a bargain. Now you should know, Casca, that I have already persuaded some of the noblest Romans to undertake with me an enterprise that is both honourable and dangerous. And I know that by now they are waiting for me in Pompey's Porch, because on this frightening night no one is moving or walking in the streets, and the look of the sky is like the work we have in hand: bloody, fiery and terrible."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cinna.","id":154},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Stand close awhile, for here comes one in haste."],"id":155,"m":"Keep out of sight for a moment. Here comes someone in a hurry."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["'Tis Cinna; I do know him by his gait;","He is a friend. Cinna, where haste you so?"],"id":156,"m":"It's Cinna. I know him by the way he walks. He's a friend. Cinna, where are you rushing to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["To find out you. Who's that? Metellus Cimber?"],"id":157,"m":"To find you. Who's that? Metellus Cimber?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["No, it is Casca, one incorporate","To our attempts. Am I not stay'd for, Cinna?"],"id":158,"m":"No, it's Casca, who has joined our plans. Aren't they waiting for me, Cinna?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["I am glad on't. What a fearful night is this!","There's two or three of us have seen strange sights."],"id":159,"m":"I'm glad of it. What a frightening night this is! Two or three of us have seen strange sights."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Am I not stay'd for? tell me."],"id":160,"m":"Aren't they waiting for me? Tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["Yes, you are. O Cassius, if you could","But win the noble Brutus to our party—"],"id":161,"m":"Yes, they are. Oh, Cassius, if you could only win noble Brutus over to our side—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Be you content. Good Cinna, take this paper,","And look you lay it in the praetor's chair,","Where Brutus may but find it; and throw this","In at his window; set this up with wax","Upon old Brutus' statue: all this done,","Repair to Pompey's Porch, where you shall find us.","Is Decius Brutus and Trebonius there?"],"id":162,"m":"Don't worry. Good Cinna, take this paper and make sure you put it on the judge's chair, where Brutus is sure to find it. Throw this one in at his window, and stick this one with wax on the statue of old Brutus. When all this is done, come back to Pompey's Porch, where you will find us. Are Decius Brutus and Trebonius there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["All but Metellus Cimber, and he's gone","To seek you at your house. Well, I will hie,","And so bestow these papers as you bade me."],"id":163,"m":"Everyone except Metellus Cimber, and he's gone to look for you at your house. Well, I'll hurry and put these papers where you told me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["That done, repair to Pompey's theatre."],"id":164,"m":"When that's done, come back to Pompey's theatre."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Cinna","id":165},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Come, Casca, you and I will yet, ere day,","See Brutus at his house: three parts of him","Is ours already, and the man entire","Upon the next encounter, yields him ours."],"id":166,"m":"Come, Casca, you and I will go and see Brutus at his house before morning. Three quarters of him is ours already, and at the next meeting the whole man will give himself to us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["O, he sits high in all the people's hearts!","And that which would appear offence in us,","His countenance, like richest alchemy,","Will change to virtue and to worthiness."],"id":167,"m":"Oh, he sits high in all the people's hearts! And what would look like a crime if we did it, his reputation, like the finest alchemy, will turn into virtue and worth."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Him, and his worth, and our great need of him,","You have right well conceited. Let us go,","For it is after midnight; and ere day,","We will awake him, and be sure of him."],"id":168,"m":"You have understood very well how much he is worth and how much we need him. Let's go, because it's past midnight, and before dawn we will wake him and make sure of him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":169},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. Brutus' orchard.","id":170},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Brutus.","id":171},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What, Lucius, ho!","I cannot, by the progress of the stars,","Give guess how near to day.—Lucius, I say!","I would it were my fault to sleep so soundly.","When, Lucius, when? Awake, I say! What, Lucius!"],"id":172,"m":"Lucius! Hey! I can't tell from the position of the stars how close it is to morning. Lucius, I say! I wish I had the fault of sleeping so soundly. Come on, Lucius, come on! Wake up, I say! Lucius!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Call'd you, my lord?"],"id":174,"m":"Did you call, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Get me a taper in my study, Lucius:","When it is lighted, come and call me here."],"id":175,"m":"Put a candle in my study, Lucius. When it's lit, come and call me here."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I will, my lord."],"id":176,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":177},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["It must be by his death: and for my part,","I know no personal cause to spurn at him,","But for the general. He would be crown'd:","How that might change his nature, there's the question.","It is the bright day that brings forth the adder,","And that craves wary walking. Crown him?—that;","And then, I grant, we put a sting in him,","That at his will he may do danger with.","Th' abuse of greatness is, when it disjoins","Remorse from power; and, to speak truth of Caesar,","I have not known when his affections sway'd","More than his reason. But 'tis a common proof,","That lowliness is young ambition's ladder,","Whereto the climber-upward turns his face;","But when he once attains the upmost round,","He then unto the ladder turns his back,","Looks in the clouds, scorning the base degrees","By which he did ascend. So Caesar may;","Then lest he may, prevent. And since the quarrel","Will bear no colour for the thing he is,","Fashion it thus: that what he is, augmented,","Would run to these and these extremities:","And therefore think him as a serpent's egg","Which hatch'd, would, as his kind grow mischievous;","And kill him in the shell."],"id":178,"m":"It will have to be by his death. And for my own part, I have no personal reason to strike at him, only the good of the people. He wants to be crowned. How that might change his nature, that's the question. It's the bright sunshine that brings the snake out, and that calls for careful walking. Crown him? That's it. And then, I admit, we put a sting in him that he can use to do harm whenever he likes. Greatness is abused when it separates power from mercy. And to tell the truth about Caesar, I have never known his feelings rule his reason. But it's common experience that humility is the ladder young ambition climbs, with his face turned towards it as he goes up. But once he reaches the top rung, he turns his back on the ladder and looks into the clouds, scorning the lower steps he climbed by. Caesar might do that. So, in case he might, we must prevent it. And since our quarrel can't be justified by what he is now, put it like this: what he is, if increased, would run to these and these extremes. So think of him as a serpent's egg which, once hatched, would grow harmful like the rest of its kind, and kill him in the shell."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius.","id":179},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["The taper burneth in your closet, sir.","Searching the window for a flint, I found","This paper, thus seal'd up, and I am sure","It did not lie there when I went to bed."],"id":180,"m":"The candle is burning in your study, sir. While I was searching the window ledge for a flint, I found this paper, sealed like this, and I'm sure it wasn't there when I went to bed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Gives him the letter","id":181},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Get you to bed again; it is not day.","Is not tomorrow, boy, the Ides of March?"],"id":182,"m":"Go back to bed. It isn't day yet. Isn't tomorrow, boy, the fifteenth of March?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I know not, sir."],"id":183,"m":"I don't know, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Look in the calendar, and bring me word."],"id":184,"m":"Look in the calendar and tell me."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I will, sir."],"id":185,"m":"I will, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":186},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The exhalations, whizzing in the air","Give so much light that I may read by them."],"id":187,"m":"The meteors whizzing through the air give so much light that I can read by them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Opens the letter and reads","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Brutus, thou sleep'st: awake and see thyself.","Shall Rome, &c. Speak, strike, redress!","\"Brutus, thou sleep'st: awake!\"","Such instigations have been often dropp'd","Where I have took them up.","\"Shall Rome, &c.\" Thus must I piece it out:","Shall Rome stand under one man's awe? What, Rome?","My ancestors did from the streets of Rome","The Tarquin drive, when he was call'd a king.","\"Speak, strike, redress!\" Am I entreated","To speak and strike? O Rome, I make thee promise,","If the redress will follow, thou receivest","Thy full petition at the hand of Brutus."],"id":189,"m":"\"Brutus, you are asleep. Wake up and see yourself. Shall Rome, etc. Speak, strike, put things right!\" \"Brutus, you are asleep. Wake up!\" Messages urging me like this have often been dropped where I have picked them up. \"Shall Rome, etc.\" I must fill in the rest like this: shall Rome stand in awe of one man? What, Rome? My ancestors drove Tarquin out of the streets of Rome when he was called a king. \"Speak, strike, put things right!\" Am I being begged to speak and strike? Oh, Rome, I promise you, if putting things right will follow, you will get everything you ask for from the hand of Brutus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius.","id":190},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sir, March is wasted fifteen days."],"id":191,"m":"Sir, fifteen days of March have passed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knock within","id":192},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["'Tis good. Go to the gate, somebody knocks."],"id":193,"m":"Good. Go to the gate, someone is knocking."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lucius","id":194},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Since Cassius first did whet me against Caesar,","I have not slept.","Between the acting of a dreadful thing","And the first motion, all the interim is","Like a phantasma, or a hideous dream:","The genius and the mortal instruments","Are then in council; and the state of man,","Like to a little kingdom, suffers then","The nature of an insurrection."],"id":195,"m":"Since Cassius first sharpened me against Caesar, I haven't slept. Between doing a dreadful thing and first thinking of it, all the time in between is like a nightmare or a hideous dream. The mind and the body's powers are then arguing it out, and a man, like a little kingdom, goes through a kind of rebellion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius.","id":196},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sir, 'tis your brother Cassius at the door,","Who doth desire to see you."],"id":197,"m":"Sir, it's your brother-in-law Cassius at the door, and he wants to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Is he alone?"],"id":198,"m":"Is he alone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["No, sir, there are moe with him."],"id":199,"m":"No, sir, there are more with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Do you know them?"],"id":200,"m":"Do you know them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["No, sir, their hats are pluck'd about their ears,","And half their faces buried in their cloaks,","That by no means I may discover them","By any mark of favour."],"id":201,"m":"No, sir. Their hats are pulled down over their ears, and half their faces are buried in their cloaks, so that I can't make them out by any feature at all."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Let 'em enter."],"id":202,"m":"Let them in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lucius","id":203},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["They are the faction. O conspiracy,","Sham'st thou to show thy dangerous brow by night,","When evils are most free? O, then, by day","Where wilt thou find a cavern dark enough","To mask thy monstrous visage? Seek none, conspiracy;","Hide it in smiles and affability:","For if thou path, thy native semblance on,","Not Erebus itself were dim enough","To hide thee from prevention."],"id":204,"m":"They are the conspirators. Oh, conspiracy, are you ashamed to show your dangerous face even at night, when evil is most free? Oh, then, by day where will you find a cave dark enough to hide your monstrous face? Don't look for one, conspiracy. Hide it in smiles and friendliness. Because if you walk about in your true appearance, not even the darkness of the underworld would be dark enough to keep you from being stopped."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cassius, Casca, Decius, Cinna, Metellus Cimber and Trebonius.","id":205},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I think we are too bold upon your rest:","Good morrow, Brutus; do we trouble you?"],"id":206,"m":"I think we are disturbing your rest too boldly. Good morning, Brutus. Are we troubling you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I have been up this hour, awake all night.","Know I these men that come along with you?"],"id":207,"m":"I've been up this past hour, awake all night. Do I know these men who have come with you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Yes, every man of them; and no man here","But honours you; and everyone doth wish","You had but that opinion of yourself","Which every noble Roman bears of you.","This is Trebonius."],"id":208,"m":"Yes, every one of them. And there is no man here who doesn't honour you, and every one wishes you had the opinion of yourself that every noble Roman has of you. This is Trebonius."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He is welcome hither."],"id":209,"m":"He is welcome here."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["This Decius Brutus."],"id":210,"m":"This is Decius Brutus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He is welcome too."],"id":211,"m":"He is welcome too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["This, Casca; this, Cinna; and this, Metellus Cimber."],"id":212,"m":"This is Casca; this is Cinna; and this is Metellus Cimber."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["They are all welcome.","What watchful cares do interpose themselves","Betwixt your eyes and night?"],"id":213,"m":"They are all welcome. What cares keep you awake and come between your eyes and the night?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Shall I entreat a word?"],"id":214,"m":"May I have a word with you?"},{"t":"sd","text":"They whisper","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Here lies the east: doth not the day break here?"],"id":216,"m":"This is east. Isn't the day breaking here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["No."],"id":217,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["O, pardon, sir, it doth; and yon grey lines","That fret the clouds are messengers of day."],"id":218,"m":"Oh, excuse me, sir, it is. And those grey streaks lining the clouds over there are messengers of the day."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["You shall confess that you are both deceiv'd.","Here, as I point my sword, the Sun arises;","Which is a great way growing on the South,","Weighing the youthful season of the year.","Some two months hence, up higher toward the North","He first presents his fire; and the high East","Stands, as the Capitol, directly here."],"id":219,"m":"You'll have to admit you're both wrong. The sun rises here, where I'm pointing my sword, a long way to the south, considering it's still early in the year. In about two months it will first appear higher up towards the north, and due east is here, in line with the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Give me your hands all over, one by one."],"id":220,"m":"Give me your hands, all of you, one by one."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["And let us swear our resolution."],"id":221,"m":"And let's swear an oath to our decision."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No, not an oath. If not the face of men,","The sufferance of our souls, the time's abuse—","If these be motives weak, break off betimes,","And every man hence to his idle bed.","So let high-sighted tyranny range on,","Till each man drop by lottery. But if these,","As I am sure they do, bear fire enough","To kindle cowards, and to steel with valour","The melting spirits of women; then, countrymen,","What need we any spur but our own cause","To prick us to redress? what other bond","Than secret Romans, that have spoke the word,","And will not palter? and what other oath","Than honesty to honesty engag'd,","That this shall be, or we will fall for it?","Swear priests and cowards, and men cautelous,","Old feeble carrions, and such suffering souls","That welcome wrongs; unto bad causes swear","Such creatures as men doubt; but do not stain","The even virtue of our enterprise,","Nor th' insuppressive mettle of our spirits,","To think that or our cause or our performance","Did need an oath; when every drop of blood","That every Roman bears, and nobly bears,","Is guilty of a several bastardy,","If he do break the smallest particle","Of any promise that hath pass'd from him."],"id":222,"m":"No, not an oath. If the look on men's faces, the suffering of our souls and the corruption of the times are weak motives, let's give up now and every man go back to his lazy bed. Then let ambitious tyranny run wild until each of us dies at the tyrant's whim. But if these motives, as I am sure they do, have fire enough to set cowards alight and to harden with courage the soft spirits of women, then, countrymen, what spur do we need but our own cause to drive us to put things right? What other bond than that we are Romans who can keep a secret, who have given our word and won't go back on it? And what other oath than honest men pledged to each other that this will happen or we will die for it? Let priests and cowards and cautious men swear, and feeble old wrecks and those meek souls who welcome being wronged. Let creatures that no one trusts swear to bad causes. But don't stain the pure virtue of our enterprise or the unconquerable courage of our spirits by thinking that either our cause or our action needs an oath, when every drop of blood in every Roman, nobly carried, would prove itself not truly his, if he broke the smallest part of any promise he had made."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["But what of Cicero? Shall we sound him?","I think he will stand very strong with us."],"id":223,"m":"But what about Cicero? Shall we sound him out? I think he will stand firmly with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Let us not leave him out."],"id":224,"m":"Let's not leave him out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["No, by no means."],"id":225,"m":"No, certainly not."},{"t":"sp","who":"METELLUS","lines":["O, let us have him, for his silver hairs","Will purchase us a good opinion,","And buy men's voices to commend our deeds.","It shall be said, his judgement rul'd our hands;","Our youths and wildness shall no whit appear,","But all be buried in his gravity."],"id":226,"m":"Oh, let's have him, because his silver hair will win us a good reputation and buy men's voices to praise what we do. People will say his judgement guided our hands. Our youth and wildness won't show at all, but will be buried under his seriousness."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O, name him not; let us not break with him;","For he will never follow anything","That other men begin."],"id":227,"m":"Oh, don't mention him. Let's not tell him our plans, because he will never follow anything that other men have started."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Then leave him out."],"id":228,"m":"Then leave him out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Indeed, he is not fit."],"id":229,"m":"Yes, he's not suitable."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Shall no man else be touch'd but only Caesar?"],"id":230,"m":"Is no one else to be touched except Caesar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Decius, well urg'd. I think it is not meet,","Mark Antony, so well belov'd of Caesar,","Should outlive Caesar: we shall find of him","A shrewd contriver; and you know, his means,","If he improve them, may well stretch so far","As to annoy us all; which to prevent,","Let Antony and Caesar fall together."],"id":231,"m":"Decius, good point. I don't think it's right that Mark Antony, whom Caesar loves so much, should outlive Caesar. We'll find him a cunning schemer. And you know his resources, if he makes the most of them, could stretch far enough to harm us all. To prevent that, let Antony and Caesar fall together."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Our course will seem too bloody, Caius Cassius,","To cut the head off, and then hack the limbs,","Like wrath in death, and envy afterwards;","For Antony is but a limb of Caesar.","Let us be sacrificers, but not butchers, Caius.","We all stand up against the spirit of Caesar,","And in the spirit of men there is no blood.","O, that we then could come by Caesar's spirit,","And not dismember Caesar! But, alas,","Caesar must bleed for it! And, gentle friends,","Let's kill him boldly, but not wrathfully;","Let's carve him as a dish fit for the gods,","Not hew him as a carcass fit for hounds.","And let our hearts, as subtle masters do,","Stir up their servants to an act of rage,","And after seem to chide 'em. This shall mark","Our purpose necessary, and not envious;","Which so appearing to the common eyes,","We shall be call'd purgers, not murderers.","And for Mark Antony, think not of him;","For he can do no more than Caesar's arm","When Caesar's head is off."],"id":232,"m":"Our action will seem too bloody, Caius Cassius, to cut the head off and then hack at the limbs, as if we killed in anger and kept up the hatred afterwards, because Antony is only a limb of Caesar. Let us be sacrificers, not butchers, Caius. We are all standing up against the spirit of Caesar, and in men's spirits there is no blood. Oh, if only we could get at Caesar's spirit and not cut up Caesar! But, alas, Caesar must bleed for it. And, gentle friends, let's kill him boldly but not angrily. Let's carve him like a dish fit for the gods, not hack him like a carcass fit for dogs. And let our hearts, like cunning masters, stir up their servants, our hands, to a violent act, and then seem to scold them for it. This will make our purpose look necessary and not spiteful. And if it looks like that to ordinary people, we'll be called healers who purge the state, not murderers. As for Mark Antony, don't think about him, because he can do no more than Caesar's arm can once Caesar's head is off."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Yet I fear him;","For in the ingrafted love he bears to Caesar—"],"id":233,"m":"Still, I fear him, because of the deep-rooted love he has for Caesar—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Alas, good Cassius, do not think of him:","If he love Caesar, all that he can do","Is to himself; take thought and die for Caesar.","And that were much he should; for he is given","To sports, to wildness, and much company."],"id":234,"m":"Oh, good Cassius, don't think about him. If he loves Caesar, all he can do is harm himself: grieve and die for Caesar. And that would be a lot to expect of him, because he loves sport, wild living and plenty of company."},{"t":"sp","who":"TREBONIUS","lines":["There is no fear in him; let him not die;","For he will live, and laugh at this hereafter."],"id":235,"m":"There's nothing to fear in him. Don't let him die, because he'll live and laugh about this afterwards."},{"t":"sd","text":"Clock strikes","id":236},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Peace! count the clock."],"id":237,"m":"Quiet! Count the strokes of the clock."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["The clock hath stricken three."],"id":238,"m":"The clock has struck three."},{"t":"sp","who":"TREBONIUS","lines":["'Tis time to part."],"id":239,"m":"It's time to go."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["But it is doubtful yet","Whether Caesar will come forth today or no;","For he is superstitious grown of late,","Quite from the main opinion he held once","Of fantasy, of dreams, and ceremonies.","It may be these apparent prodigies,","The unaccustom'd terror of this night,","And the persuasion of his augurers,","May hold him from the Capitol today."],"id":240,"m":"But it's still uncertain whether Caesar will come out today or not, because he has grown superstitious lately, quite unlike the strong opinion he once held about fancies, dreams and omens. It may be that these obvious signs, the unusual terrors of this night and the persuasion of his fortune-tellers will keep him away from the Capitol today."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Never fear that: if he be so resolved,","I can o'ersway him, for he loves to hear","That unicorns may be betray'd with trees,","And bears with glasses, elephants with holes,","Lions with toils, and men with flatterers.","But when I tell him he hates flatterers,","He says he does, being then most flattered.","Let me work;","For I can give his humour the true bent,","And I will bring him to the Capitol."],"id":241,"m":"Don't worry about that. If he has made up his mind to stay, I can win him round, because he loves to hear that unicorns can be trapped by trees, bears with mirrors, elephants with pits, lions with nets, and men with flatterers. But when I tell him he hates flatterers, he says he does, and that's when he is most flattered. Let me work on him, because I can turn his mood the right way, and I will bring him to the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Nay, we will all of us be there to fetch him."],"id":242,"m":"No, we'll all be there to fetch him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["By the eighth hour: is that the uttermost?"],"id":243,"m":"By eight o'clock. Is that the latest?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["Be that the uttermost; and fail not then."],"id":244,"m":"Let that be the latest, and don't fail to be there."},{"t":"sp","who":"METELLUS","lines":["Caius Ligarius doth bear Caesar hard,","Who rated him for speaking well of Pompey;","I wonder none of you have thought of him."],"id":245,"m":"Caius Ligarius has a grudge against Caesar, who told him off for speaking well of Pompey. I'm surprised none of you have thought of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Now, good Metellus, go along by him:","He loves me well, and I have given him reason;","Send him but hither, and I'll fashion him."],"id":246,"m":"Now, good Metellus, go to his house. He loves me, and I have given him reason to. Just send him here and I'll shape him to our purpose."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["The morning comes upon's. We'll leave you, Brutus.","And, friends, disperse yourselves; but all remember","What you have said, and show yourselves true Romans."],"id":247,"m":"Morning is coming on us. We'll leave you, Brutus. And, friends, go your separate ways, but all of you remember what you have said and show yourselves true Romans."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Good gentlemen, look fresh and merrily;","Let not our looks put on our purposes,","But bear it as our Roman actors do,","With untired spirits and formal constancy.","And so, good morrow to you everyone."],"id":248,"m":"Good gentlemen, look fresh and cheerful. Don't let our faces show what we intend, but carry it off as our Roman actors do, with tireless spirits and steady composure. And so, good morning to every one of you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Brutus","id":249},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Boy! Lucius! Fast asleep? It is no matter;","Enjoy the honey-heavy dew of slumber:","Thou hast no figures nor no fantasies,","Which busy care draws in the brains of men;","Therefore thou sleep'st so sound."],"id":250,"m":"Boy! Lucius! Fast asleep? It doesn't matter. Enjoy the sweet, heavy dew of sleep. You have no imaginings or fantasies that busy worry draws in men's minds, and that's why you sleep so soundly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Portia.","id":251},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Brutus, my lord."],"id":252,"m":"Brutus, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Portia, what mean you? Wherefore rise you now?","It is not for your health thus to commit","Your weak condition to the raw cold morning."],"id":253,"m":"Portia, what are you doing? Why are you up now? It isn't good for your health to expose your delicate body to the raw, cold morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Nor for yours neither. Y' have ungently, Brutus,","Stole from my bed; and yesternight at supper,","You suddenly arose, and walk'd about,","Musing and sighing, with your arms across;","And when I ask'd you what the matter was,","You star'd upon me with ungentle looks.","I urg'd you further; then you scratch'd your head,","And too impatiently stamp'd with your foot;","Yet I insisted, yet you answer'd not,","But with an angry wafture of your hand","Gave sign for me to leave you. So I did,","Fearing to strengthen that impatience","Which seem'd too much enkindled; and withal","Hoping it was but an effect of humour,","Which sometime hath his hour with every man.","It will not let you eat, nor talk, nor sleep;","And could it work so much upon your shape","As it hath much prevail'd on your condition,","I should not know you, Brutus. Dear my lord,","Make me acquainted with your cause of grief."],"id":254,"m":"Nor for yours either. You crept unkindly from my bed, Brutus. And last night at supper you suddenly got up and walked about, brooding and sighing, with your arms folded. And when I asked you what was wrong, you stared at me unkindly. I pressed you further; then you scratched your head and stamped your foot too impatiently. Still I insisted, and still you didn't answer, but with an angry wave of your hand signalled me to leave you. So I did, afraid of making worse an impatience that seemed already too inflamed, and hoping too that it was only a passing mood, which every man has sometimes. It won't let you eat or talk or sleep, and if it could change your appearance as much as it has changed your temper, I wouldn't recognise you, Brutus. My dear lord, tell me the cause of your unhappiness."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I am not well in health, and that is all."],"id":255,"m":"I am not well, and that's all."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Brutus is wise, and, were he not in health,","He would embrace the means to come by it."],"id":256,"m":"Brutus is wise, and if he weren't well he would do what he needed to get better."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why, so I do. Good Portia, go to bed."],"id":257,"m":"Why, so I am. Good Portia, go to bed."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Is Brutus sick, and is it physical","To walk unbraced and suck up the humours","Of the dank morning? What, is Brutus sick,","And will he steal out of his wholesome bed","To dare the vile contagion of the night,","And tempt the rheumy and unpurged air","To add unto his sickness? No, my Brutus;","You have some sick offence within your mind,","Which, by the right and virtue of my place,","I ought to know of: and, upon my knees,","I charm you, by my once commended beauty,","By all your vows of love, and that great vow","Which did incorporate and make us one,","That you unfold to me, your self, your half,","Why you are heavy, and what men tonight","Have had resort to you; for here have been","Some six or seven, who did hide their faces","Even from darkness."],"id":258,"m":"Is Brutus sick, and is it healthy to walk about with his clothes undone, breathing in the damp morning air? What, is Brutus sick, and will he creep out of his healthy bed to risk the foul diseases of the night and tempt the damp, unclean air to make him sicker? No, my Brutus, you have some illness in your mind, which I have a right, as your wife, to know about. And on my knees I beg you, by my beauty which you once praised, by all your vows of love, and by that great vow which joined us into one, that you reveal to me, your self, your other half, why you are so sad, and what men have visited you tonight. Six or seven have been here, who hid their faces even from the darkness."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Kneel not, gentle Portia."],"id":259,"m":"Don't kneel, gentle Portia."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["I should not need, if you were gentle Brutus.","Within the bond of marriage, tell me, Brutus,","Is it excepted I should know no secrets","That appertain to you? Am I your self","But, as it were, in sort or limitation,","To keep with you at meals, comfort your bed,","And talk to you sometimes? Dwell I but in the suburbs","Of your good pleasure? If it be no more,","Portia is Brutus' harlot, not his wife."],"id":260,"m":"I wouldn't need to if you were gentle Brutus. Tell me, Brutus, is it a condition of our marriage that I shouldn't know any secrets that concern you? Am I part of you only in a limited way, to keep you company at meals, comfort you in bed and talk to you sometimes? Do I live only on the outskirts of your affection? If that's all, Portia is Brutus's whore, not his wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You are my true and honourable wife,","As dear to me as are the ruddy drops","That visit my sad heart."],"id":261,"m":"You are my true and honourable wife, as dear to me as the red drops of blood that flow through my sad heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["If this were true, then should I know this secret.","I grant I am a woman; but withal","A woman that Lord Brutus took to wife;","I grant I am a woman; but withal","A woman well reputed, Cato's daughter.","Think you I am no stronger than my sex,","Being so father'd and so husbanded?","Tell me your counsels, I will not disclose 'em.","I have made strong proof of my constancy,","Giving myself a voluntary wound","Here, in the thigh: can I bear that with patience","And not my husband's secrets?"],"id":262,"m":"If that were true, I would know this secret. I admit I am a woman, but a woman whom Lord Brutus took as his wife. I admit I am a woman, but a woman with a good name, Cato's daughter. Do you think I am no stronger than other women, with such a father and such a husband? Tell me your plans; I won't reveal them. I have given strong proof of my firmness by deliberately wounding myself here, in the thigh. Can I bear that patiently and not my husband's secrets?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O ye gods,","Render me worthy of this noble wife!"],"id":263,"m":"Oh, you gods, make me worthy of this noble wife!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Knock","id":264},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Hark, hark, one knocks. Portia, go in awhile;","And by and by thy bosom shall partake","The secrets of my heart.","All my engagements I will construe to thee,","All the charactery of my sad brows.","Leave me with haste."],"id":265,"m":"Listen, listen, someone is knocking. Portia, go inside for a while, and soon your heart will share the secrets of mine. I will explain all my commitments to you, and everything written in my sad face. Leave me, quickly."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Portia","id":266},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius with Ligarius.","id":267},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Lucius, who's that knocks?"],"id":268,"m":"Lucius, who's that knocking?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Here is a sick man that would speak with you."],"id":269,"m":"Here's a sick man who wants to speak to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Caius Ligarius, that Metellus spake of.","Boy, stand aside. Caius Ligarius, how?"],"id":270,"m":"It's Caius Ligarius, the one Metellus talked about. Boy, stand aside. Caius Ligarius, how are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LIGARIUS","lines":["Vouchsafe good-morrow from a feeble tongue."],"id":271,"m":"Accept a good morning from a weak voice."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O, what a time have you chose out, brave Caius,","To wear a kerchief! Would you were not sick!"],"id":272,"m":"Oh, what a time you have chosen, brave Caius, to wear a sick man's scarf! I wish you weren't ill!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LIGARIUS","lines":["I am not sick, if Brutus have in hand","Any exploit worthy the name of honour."],"id":273,"m":"I'm not ill, if Brutus has in hand any deed worthy to be called honourable."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Such an exploit have I in hand, Ligarius,","Had you a healthful ear to hear of it."],"id":274,"m":"I have such a deed in hand, Ligarius, if you had a healthy ear to hear about it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LIGARIUS","lines":["By all the gods that Romans bow before,","I here discard my sickness. Soul of Rome!","Brave son, derived from honourable loins!","Thou, like an exorcist, hast conjur'd up","My mortified spirit. Now bid me run,","And I will strive with things impossible,","Yea, get the better of them. What's to do?"],"id":275,"m":"By all the gods that Romans bow to, I throw off my sickness now. Soul of Rome! Brave son, born of an honourable family! Like a magician, you have called up my deadened spirit. Now tell me to run and I will struggle with impossible things, yes, and beat them. What is to be done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["A piece of work that will make sick men whole."],"id":276,"m":"A piece of work that will make sick men well."},{"t":"sp","who":"LIGARIUS","lines":["But are not some whole that we must make sick?"],"id":277,"m":"But aren't there some well men we must make sick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["That must we also. What it is, my Caius,","I shall unfold to thee, as we are going,","To whom it must be done."],"id":278,"m":"We must do that too. What it is, my Caius, and who it must be done to, I'll explain to you as we walk."},{"t":"sp","who":"LIGARIUS","lines":["Set on your foot,","And with a heart new-fir'd I follow you,","To do I know not what; but it sufficeth","That Brutus leads me on."],"id":279,"m":"Lead on, and with a heart set on fire again I'll follow you to do I don't know what. It's enough that Brutus leads me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Thunder","id":280},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Follow me then."],"id":281,"m":"Follow me, then."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":282},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"A room in Caesar's palace.","id":283},{"t":"sd","text":"Thunder and lightning. Enter Caesar, in his nightgown.","id":284},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Nor heaven nor earth have been at peace tonight:","Thrice hath Calphurnia in her sleep cried out,","\"Help, ho! They murder Caesar!\" Who's within?"],"id":285,"m":"Neither heaven nor earth has been at peace tonight. Three times Calphurnia has cried out in her sleep, \"Help! They are murdering Caesar!\" Who's there?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":286},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["My lord?"],"id":287,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Go bid the priests do present sacrifice,","And bring me their opinions of success."],"id":288,"m":"Go and tell the priests to make a sacrifice at once, and bring me their opinion of how things will go."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I will, my lord."],"id":289,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":290},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Calphurnia.","id":291},{"t":"sp","who":"CALPHURNIA","lines":["What mean you, Caesar? Think you to walk forth?","You shall not stir out of your house today."],"id":292,"m":"What are you doing, Caesar? Are you thinking of going out? You shall not set foot out of the house today."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Caesar shall forth. The things that threaten'd me","Ne'er look'd but on my back; when they shall see","The face of Caesar, they are vanished."],"id":293,"m":"Caesar will go out. The things that threatened me have only ever seen my back. When they see Caesar's face, they vanish."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALPHURNIA","lines":["Caesar, I never stood on ceremonies,","Yet now they fright me. There is one within,","Besides the things that we have heard and seen,","Recounts most horrid sights seen by the watch.","A lioness hath whelped in the streets,","And graves have yawn'd, and yielded up their dead;","Fierce fiery warriors fight upon the clouds","In ranks and squadrons and right form of war,","Which drizzled blood upon the Capitol;","The noise of battle hurtled in the air,","Horses did neigh, and dying men did groan,","And ghosts did shriek and squeal about the streets.","O Caesar, these things are beyond all use,","And I do fear them!"],"id":294,"m":"Caesar, I never paid attention to omens, but now they frighten me. There is someone in the house who, besides the things we have heard and seen, tells of the most horrible sights seen by the night watch. A lioness has given birth in the streets, and graves have opened and given up their dead. Fierce, fiery warriors fight on the clouds in ranks and squadrons and proper battle formation, and they drizzled blood on the Capitol. The noise of battle clashed in the air, horses neighed, dying men groaned, and ghosts shrieked and squealed in the streets. Oh, Caesar, these things are beyond all experience, and I'm afraid of them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["What can be avoided","Whose end is purpos'd by the mighty gods?","Yet Caesar shall go forth; for these predictions","Are to the world in general as to Caesar."],"id":295,"m":"What can be avoided if the mighty gods have decided it? Still, Caesar will go out, because these predictions apply to the world in general as much as to Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALPHURNIA","lines":["When beggars die, there are no comets seen;","The heavens themselves blaze forth the death of princes."],"id":296,"m":"When beggars die, no comets are seen. The heavens themselves blaze out the death of princes."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Cowards die many times before their deaths;","The valiant never taste of death but once.","Of all the wonders that I yet have heard,","It seems to me most strange that men should fear,","Seeing that death, a necessary end,","Will come when it will come."],"id":297,"m":"Cowards die many times before they actually die. The brave only taste death once. Of all the wonders I have heard of, the strangest to me is that men should be afraid, since death, which no one can escape, will come when it will come."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Servant.","id":298},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","cont":1,"lines":["What say the augurers?"],"id":299,"m":"What do the fortune-tellers say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["They would not have you to stir forth today.","Plucking the entrails of an offering forth,","They could not find a heart within the beast."],"id":300,"m":"They don't want you to go out today. When they pulled out the insides of the sacrificed animal, they couldn't find a heart in the beast."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["The gods do this in shame of cowardice:","Caesar should be a beast without a heart","If he should stay at home today for fear.","No, Caesar shall not. Danger knows full well","That Caesar is more dangerous than he.","We are two lions litter'd in one day,","And I the elder and more terrible,","And Caesar shall go forth."],"id":301,"m":"The gods do this to shame cowardice. Caesar would be a beast without a heart if he stayed at home today out of fear. No, Caesar will not. Danger knows very well that Caesar is more dangerous than he is. We are two lions born on the same day, and I am the elder and more terrible. And Caesar will go out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALPHURNIA","lines":["Alas, my lord,","Your wisdom is consum'd in confidence.","Do not go forth today: call it my fear","That keeps you in the house, and not your own.","We'll send Mark Antony to the Senate-house,","And he shall say you are not well today.","Let me upon my knee prevail in this."],"id":302,"m":"Oh, my lord, your wisdom is eaten up by overconfidence. Don't go out today. Say it's my fear that keeps you in the house, not your own. We'll send Mark Antony to the Senate house, and he will say you aren't well today. On my knees, let me win this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Mark Antony shall say I am not well,","And for thy humour, I will stay at home."],"id":303,"m":"Mark Antony will say I'm not well, and to please you I'll stay at home."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Decius.","id":304},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","cont":1,"lines":["Here's Decius Brutus, he shall tell them so."],"id":305,"m":"Here's Decius Brutus. He'll tell them so."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Caesar, all hail! Good morrow, worthy Caesar.","I come to fetch you to the Senate-house."],"id":306,"m":"Hail, Caesar! Good morning, worthy Caesar. I've come to bring you to the Senate house."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["And you are come in very happy time","To bear my greeting to the Senators,","And tell them that I will not come today.","Cannot, is false; and that I dare not, falser:","I will not come today. Tell them so, Decius."],"id":307,"m":"And you have come at just the right time to take my greetings to the senators and tell them I won't come today. That I cannot is false, and that I dare not is falser. I will not come today. Tell them that, Decius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CALPHURNIA","lines":["Say he is sick."],"id":308,"m":"Say he is sick."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Shall Caesar send a lie?","Have I in conquest stretch'd mine arm so far,","To be afeard to tell grey-beards the truth?","Decius, go tell them Caesar will not come."],"id":309,"m":"Shall Caesar send a lie? Have I stretched my arm so far in conquest only to be afraid of telling the truth to some old greybeards? Decius, go and tell them Caesar will not come."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Most mighty Caesar, let me know some cause,","Lest I be laugh'd at when I tell them so."],"id":310,"m":"Most mighty Caesar, give me some reason, so that I'm not laughed at when I tell them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["The cause is in my will; I will not come.","That is enough to satisfy the Senate.","But for your private satisfaction,","Because I love you, I will let you know:","Calphurnia here, my wife, stays me at home.","She dreamt tonight she saw my statue,","Which like a fountain with an hundred spouts","Did run pure blood; and many lusty Romans","Came smiling, and did bathe their hands in it.","And these does she apply for warnings and portents","And evils imminent; and on her knee","Hath begg'd that I will stay at home today."],"id":311,"m":"The reason is my will: I will not come. That is enough to satisfy the Senate. But for your private satisfaction, because I love you, I'll tell you. Calphurnia here, my wife, is keeping me at home. Last night she dreamt she saw my statue, which ran with pure blood like a fountain with a hundred spouts, and many strong, cheerful Romans came smiling and bathed their hands in it. She interprets these as warnings and omens of evils about to happen, and on her knees she has begged me to stay at home today."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["This dream is all amiss interpreted:","It was a vision fair and fortunate.","Your statue spouting blood in many pipes,","In which so many smiling Romans bath'd,","Signifies that from you great Rome shall suck","Reviving blood, and that great men shall press","For tinctures, stains, relics, and cognizance.","This by Calphurnia's dream is signified."],"id":312,"m":"This dream has been completely misinterpreted. It was a fair and lucky vision. Your statue spouting blood from many pipes, in which so many smiling Romans bathed, means that great Rome will suck reviving blood from you, and that great men will crowd round for cloths dipped in it, for stains, relics and badges to remember you by. That is what Calphurnia's dream means."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["And this way have you well expounded it."],"id":313,"m":"And you have explained it well that way."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["I have, when you have heard what I can say;","And know it now. The Senate have concluded","To give this day a crown to mighty Caesar.","If you shall send them word you will not come,","Their minds may change. Besides, it were a mock","Apt to be render'd, for someone to say,","\"Break up the Senate till another time,","When Caesar's wife shall meet with better dreams.\"","If Caesar hide himself, shall they not whisper","\"Lo, Caesar is afraid\"?","Pardon me, Caesar; for my dear dear love","To your proceeding bids me tell you this,","And reason to my love is liable."],"id":314,"m":"I have, as you'll see when you've heard what I have to say. And know this now: the Senate has decided to give a crown to mighty Caesar today. If you send word that you won't come, they may change their minds. Besides, someone would be likely to make a joke of it and say, \"Close the Senate until another time, when Caesar's wife has better dreams.\" If Caesar hides, won't they whisper, \"Look, Caesar is afraid\"? Forgive me, Caesar, because my deep, deep love for your advancement makes me tell you this, and my love overrules my sense of what is proper."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["How foolish do your fears seem now, Calphurnia!","I am ashamed I did yield to them.","Give me my robe, for I will go."],"id":315,"m":"How foolish your fears seem now, Calphurnia! I'm ashamed I gave in to them. Give me my robe, because I will go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Brutus, Ligarius, Metellus, Casca, Trebonius, Cinna and Publius.","id":316},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","cont":1,"lines":["And look where Publius is come to fetch me."],"id":317,"m":"And look, here's Publius come to fetch me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["Good morrow, Caesar."],"id":318,"m":"Good morning, Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Welcome, Publius.","What, Brutus, are you stirr'd so early too?","Good morrow, Casca. Caius Ligarius,","Caesar was ne'er so much your enemy","As that same ague which hath made you lean.","What is't o'clock?"],"id":319,"m":"Welcome, Publius. What, Brutus, are you up so early too? Good morning, Casca. Caius Ligarius, Caesar was never as much your enemy as that fever which has made you so thin. What time is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Caesar, 'tis strucken eight."],"id":320,"m":"Caesar, it has struck eight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["I thank you for your pains and courtesy."],"id":321,"m":"Thank you for your trouble and courtesy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antony.","id":322},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","cont":1,"lines":["See! Antony, that revels long a-nights,","Is notwithstanding up. Good morrow, Antony."],"id":323,"m":"Look! Antony, who stays up partying late at night, is up all the same. Good morning, Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["So to most noble Caesar."],"id":324,"m":"And to you, most noble Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Bid them prepare within.","I am to blame to be thus waited for.","Now, Cinna; now, Metellus; what, Trebonius!","I have an hour's talk in store for you:","Remember that you call on me today;","Be near me, that I may remember you."],"id":325,"m":"Tell them to get ready inside. I am at fault for keeping you waiting like this. Now, Cinna; now, Metellus; what, Trebonius! I have an hour's talk saved up for you. Remember to come and see me today. Stay near me so that I remember you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TREBONIUS","lines":["Caesar, I will. [Aside] and so near will I be,","That your best friends shall wish I had been further."],"id":326,"m":"Caesar, I will. [Aside] And I'll be so near that your best friends will wish I had been further away."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Good friends, go in, and taste some wine with me;","And we, like friends, will straightway go together."],"id":327,"m":"Good friends, come in and have some wine with me, and then, like friends, we'll go along together at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["[Aside] That every like is not the same, O Caesar,","The heart of Brutus yearns to think upon."],"id":328,"m":"[Aside] Brutus's heart aches to think that being like friends is not the same as being friends, Caesar."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":329},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"III","loc":"A street near the Capitol.","id":330},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Artemidorus, reading a paper.","id":331},{"t":"sp","who":"ARTEMIDORUS","lines":["\"Caesar, beware of Brutus; take heed of Cassius; come not near Casca;","have an eye to Cinna; trust not Trebonius; mark well Metellus Cimber;","Decius Brutus loves thee not; thou hast wrong'd Caius Ligarius. There","is but one mind in all these men, and it is bent against Caesar. If","thou be'st not immortal, look about you: security gives way to","conspiracy. The mighty gods defend thee!","Thy lover, Artemidorus.\"","Here will I stand till Caesar pass along,","And as a suitor will I give him this.","My heart laments that virtue cannot live","Out of the teeth of emulation.","If thou read this, O Caesar, thou mayest live;","If not, the Fates with traitors do contrive."],"id":332,"m":"\"Caesar, beware of Brutus; watch out for Cassius; don't go near Casca; keep an eye on Cinna; don't trust Trebonius; watch Metellus Cimber closely; Decius Brutus doesn't love you; you have wronged Caius Ligarius. All these men have one purpose, and it is aimed at Caesar. If you are not immortal, look about you: overconfidence opens the way to conspiracy. May the mighty gods defend you! Your friend, Artemidorus.\" I'll stand here until Caesar passes, and give him this as if it were a petition. My heart grieves that goodness cannot live beyond the reach of envy's teeth. If you read this, Caesar, you may live. If not, the Fates are plotting with the traitors."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":333},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another part of the same street, before the house of Brutus.","id":334},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Portia and Lucius.","id":335},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["I pr'ythee, boy, run to the Senate-house;","Stay not to answer me, but get thee gone.","Why dost thou stay?"],"id":336,"m":"Please, boy, run to the Senate house. Don't stop to answer me, just go. Why are you waiting?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["To know my errand, madam."],"id":337,"m":"To find out what I'm to do there, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["I would have had thee there and here again,","Ere I can tell thee what thou shouldst do there.","[Aside] O constancy, be strong upon my side,","Set a huge mountain 'tween my heart and tongue!","I have a man's mind, but a woman's might.","How hard it is for women to keep counsel!","Art thou here yet?"],"id":338,"m":"I'd have liked you to be there and back again before I could tell you what to do there. [Aside] Oh, self-control, stay firmly on my side, and put a huge mountain between my heart and my tongue! I have a man's mind but a woman's strength. How hard it is for women to keep a secret! Are you still here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Madam, what should I do?","Run to the Capitol, and nothing else?","And so return to you, and nothing else?"],"id":339,"m":"Madam, what should I do? Run to the Capitol, and nothing else? And then come back to you, and nothing else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Yes, bring me word, boy, if thy lord look well,","For he went sickly forth: and take good note","What Caesar doth, what suitors press to him.","Hark, boy, what noise is that?"],"id":340,"m":"Yes, come back and tell me, boy, if your master looks well, because he was unwell when he went out. And take careful note of what Caesar does, and which petitioners crowd round him. Listen, boy, what's that noise?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I hear none, madam."],"id":341,"m":"I can't hear anything, madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Pr'ythee, listen well.","I heard a bustling rumour, like a fray,","And the wind brings it from the Capitol."],"id":342,"m":"Please, listen carefully. I heard a confused noise, like a fight, and the wind is bringing it from the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Sooth, madam, I hear nothing."],"id":343,"m":"Honestly, madam, I can't hear anything."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Soothsayer.","id":344},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Come hither, fellow:","Which way hast thou been?"],"id":345,"m":"Come here, fellow. Where have you come from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["At mine own house, good lady."],"id":346,"m":"From my own house, good lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["What is't o'clock?"],"id":347,"m":"What time is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["About the ninth hour, lady."],"id":348,"m":"About nine o'clock, lady."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Is Caesar yet gone to the Capitol?"],"id":349,"m":"Has Caesar gone to the Capitol yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Madam, not yet. I go to take my stand,","To see him pass on to the Capitol."],"id":350,"m":"Not yet, madam. I'm going to find a place to watch him go by on his way to the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Thou hast some suit to Caesar, hast thou not?"],"id":351,"m":"You have a petition for Caesar, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["That I have, lady, if it will please Caesar","To be so good to Caesar as to hear me,","I shall beseech him to befriend himself."],"id":352,"m":"I have, lady. If Caesar will be good enough to himself to listen to me, I will beg him to look after himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["Why, know'st thou any harm's intended towards him?"],"id":353,"m":"Why, do you know of any harm intended against him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["None that I know will be, much that I fear may chance.","Good morrow to you. Here the street is narrow.","The throng that follows Caesar at the heels,","Of Senators, of Praetors, common suitors,","Will crowd a feeble man almost to death:","I'll get me to a place more void, and there","Speak to great Caesar as he comes along."],"id":354,"m":"None that I know will happen, but much that I fear may. Good morning to you. The street is narrow here. The crowd of senators, magistrates and ordinary petitioners at Caesar's heels will squeeze a weak man almost to death. I'll find a less crowded place and speak to great Caesar there as he comes by."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":355},{"t":"sp","who":"PORTIA","lines":["I must go in.","[Aside] Ay me, how weak a thing","The heart of woman is! O Brutus,","The heavens speed thee in thine enterprise!","Sure, the boy heard me. Brutus hath a suit","That Caesar will not grant. O, I grow faint.","Run, Lucius, and commend me to my lord;","Say I am merry; come to me again,","And bring me word what he doth say to thee."],"id":356,"m":"I must go in. [Aside] Oh dear, what a weak thing a woman's heart is! Oh, Brutus, may the heavens help you in your enterprise! I'm sure the boy heard me. Brutus has a petition that Caesar won't grant. Oh, I feel faint. Run, Lucius, and give my love to my husband. Tell him I'm cheerful, then come back to me and tell me what he says to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":357},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. Before the Capitol; the Senate sitting.","id":358},{"t":"sd","text":"A crowd of people in the street leading to the Capitol. Flourish. Enter Caesar, Brutus, Cassius, Casca, Decius, Metellus, Trebonius, Cinna, Antony, Lepidus, Artemidorus, Publius, Popilius and the Soothsayer.","id":359},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["The Ides of March are come."],"id":360,"m":"The fifteenth of March has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"SOOTHSAYER","lines":["Ay, Caesar; but not gone."],"id":361,"m":"Yes, Caesar, but it hasn't gone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARTEMIDORUS","lines":["Hail, Caesar! Read this schedule."],"id":362,"m":"Hail, Caesar! Read this paper."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Trebonius doth desire you to o'er-read,","At your best leisure, this his humble suit."],"id":363,"m":"Trebonius asks you to read through his humble petition when you have time."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARTEMIDORUS","lines":["O Caesar, read mine first; for mine's a suit","That touches Caesar nearer. Read it, great Caesar."],"id":364,"m":"Oh, Caesar, read mine first, because my petition concerns Caesar more closely. Read it, great Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["What touches us ourself shall be last serv'd."],"id":365,"m":"What concerns me personally will be dealt with last."},{"t":"sp","who":"ARTEMIDORUS","lines":["Delay not, Caesar. Read it instantly."],"id":366,"m":"Don't delay, Caesar. Read it at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["What, is the fellow mad?"],"id":367,"m":"What, is the fellow mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PUBLIUS","lines":["Sirrah, give place."],"id":368,"m":"You there, get out of the way."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["What, urge you your petitions in the street?","Come to the Capitol."],"id":369,"m":"What, are you pushing your petitions in the street? Come to the Capitol."},{"t":"sd","text":"Caesar enters the Capitol, the rest following. All the Senators rise.","id":370},{"t":"sp","who":"POPILIUS","lines":["I wish your enterprise today may thrive."],"id":371,"m":"I hope your enterprise succeeds today."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["What enterprise, Popilius?"],"id":372,"m":"What enterprise, Popilius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POPILIUS","lines":["Fare you well."],"id":373,"m":"Goodbye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Advances to Caesar","id":374},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What said Popilius Lena?"],"id":375,"m":"What did Popilius Lena say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["He wish'd today our enterprise might thrive.","I fear our purpose is discovered."],"id":376,"m":"He hoped our enterprise would succeed today. I'm afraid our plan has been discovered."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Look how he makes to Caesar: mark him."],"id":377,"m":"Look how he's going up to Caesar. Watch him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Casca, be sudden, for we fear prevention.","Brutus, what shall be done? If this be known,","Cassius or Caesar never shall turn back,","For I will slay myself."],"id":378,"m":"Casca, be quick, because we're afraid of being stopped. Brutus, what shall we do? If this is known, either Cassius or Caesar will never go home, because I'll kill myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Cassius, be constant:","Popilius Lena speaks not of our purposes;","For look, he smiles, and Caesar doth not change."],"id":379,"m":"Cassius, stay calm. Popilius Lena isn't talking about our plans. Look, he's smiling, and Caesar's face doesn't change."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Trebonius knows his time, for look you, Brutus,","He draws Mark Antony out of the way."],"id":380,"m":"Trebonius knows his moment. Look, Brutus, he's drawing Mark Antony out of the way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Antony and Trebonius. Caesar and the Senators take their seats","id":381},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Where is Metellus Cimber? Let him go,","And presently prefer his suit to Caesar."],"id":382,"m":"Where is Metellus Cimber? Let him go and present his petition to Caesar now."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He is address'd; press near and second him."],"id":383,"m":"He's ready. Move in close and back him up."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["Casca, you are the first that rears your hand."],"id":384,"m":"Casca, you are the first to raise your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Are we all ready? What is now amiss","That Caesar and his Senate must redress?"],"id":385,"m":"Are we all ready? What is wrong now that Caesar and his Senate must put right?"},{"t":"sp","who":"METELLUS","lines":["Most high, most mighty, and most puissant Caesar,","Metellus Cimber throws before thy seat","An humble heart."],"id":386,"m":"Most high, most mighty and most powerful Caesar, Metellus Cimber throws down a humble heart before your seat."},{"t":"sd","text":"Kneeling","id":387},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["I must prevent thee, Cimber.","These couchings and these lowly courtesies","Might fire the blood of ordinary men,","And turn pre-ordinance and first decree","Into the law of children. Be not fond,","To think that Caesar bears such rebel blood","That will be thaw'd from the true quality","With that which melteth fools; I mean sweet words,","Low-crooked curtsies, and base spaniel fawning.","Thy brother by decree is banished:","If thou dost bend, and pray, and fawn for him,","I spurn thee like a cur out of my way.","Know, Caesar dost not wrong, nor without cause","Will he be satisfied."],"id":388,"m":"I must stop you, Cimber. This crouching and humble bowing might fire up the blood of ordinary men and turn what has already been decided into a child's game. Don't be so foolish as to think that Caesar's blood is so unruly that it can be melted from its true nature by what melts fools: I mean sweet words, low bows and the cringing of a spaniel. Your brother has been banished by law. If you bow and beg and fawn for him, I'll kick you out of my way like a dog. Know that Caesar does no wrong, and he will not be persuaded without good reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"METELLUS","lines":["Is there no voice more worthy than my own,","To sound more sweetly in great Caesar's ear","For the repealing of my banish'd brother?"],"id":389,"m":"Is there no voice worthier than mine to sound sweeter in great Caesar's ear, to call back my banished brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I kiss thy hand, but not in flattery, Caesar;","Desiring thee that Publius Cimber may","Have an immediate freedom of repeal."],"id":390,"m":"I kiss your hand, but not to flatter you, Caesar, asking you to let Publius Cimber be recalled at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["What, Brutus?"],"id":391,"m":"What, Brutus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Pardon, Caesar; Caesar, pardon:","As low as to thy foot doth Cassius fall,","To beg enfranchisement for Publius Cimber."],"id":392,"m":"Pardon, Caesar! Caesar, pardon! Cassius falls as low as your foot to beg freedom for Publius Cimber."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["I could be well mov'd, if I were as you;","If I could pray to move, prayers would move me:","But I am constant as the northern star,","Of whose true-fix'd and resting quality","There is no fellow in the firmament.","The skies are painted with unnumber'd sparks,","They are all fire, and every one doth shine;","But there's but one in all doth hold his place.","So in the world; 'tis furnish'd well with men,","And men are flesh and blood, and apprehensive;","Yet in the number I do know but one","That unassailable holds on his rank,","Unshak'd of motion: and that I am he,","Let me a little show it, even in this,","That I was constant Cimber should be banish'd,","And constant do remain to keep him so."],"id":393,"m":"I could easily be moved if I were like you. If I could beg others to change their minds, other people's begging would move me. But I am as constant as the northern star, which has no equal in the sky for staying fixed and still. The skies are painted with countless sparks. They are all fire, and every one shines, but only one of them all keeps its place. It's the same on earth. It is well supplied with men, and men are flesh and blood and can think. Yet out of all of them I know only one who keeps his position without being shaken or moved, and that I am he, let me show a little, even in this: I was firm that Cimber should be banished, and I remain firm in keeping him banished."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["O Caesar,—"],"id":394,"m":"Oh, Caesar—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Hence! wilt thou lift up Olympus?"],"id":395,"m":"Get away! Would you try to lift up Mount Olympus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["Great Caesar,—"],"id":396,"m":"Great Caesar—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Doth not Brutus bootless kneel?"],"id":397,"m":"Even Brutus kneels in vain, doesn't he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Speak, hands, for me!"],"id":398,"m":"Hands, speak for me!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Casca stabs Caesar in the neck. Caesar catches hold of his arm. He is then stabbed by several other Conspirators, and at last by Marcus Brutus","id":399},{"t":"sp","who":"CAESAR","lines":["Et tu, Brute?—Then fall, Caesar!"],"id":400,"m":"You too, Brutus?—Then fall, Caesar!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies. The Senators and People retire in confusion","id":401},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["Liberty! Freedom! Tyranny is dead!","Run hence, proclaim, cry it about the streets."],"id":402,"m":"Liberty! Freedom! Tyranny is dead! Run from here, announce it, shout it through the streets."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Some to the common pulpits and cry out,","\"Liberty, freedom, and enfranchisement!\""],"id":403,"m":"Some of you go to the public platforms and cry, \"Liberty, freedom and release from slavery!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["People and Senators, be not affrighted.","Fly not; stand still; ambition's debt is paid."],"id":404,"m":"People and senators, don't be frightened. Don't run; stand still. Ambition has paid its debt."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Go to the pulpit, Brutus."],"id":405,"m":"Go up to the platform, Brutus."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["And Cassius too."],"id":406,"m":"And Cassius too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Where's Publius?"],"id":407,"m":"Where's Publius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA","lines":["Here, quite confounded with this mutiny."],"id":408,"m":"Here, completely stunned by this uprising."},{"t":"sp","who":"METELLUS","lines":["Stand fast together, lest some friend of Caesar's","Should chance—"],"id":409,"m":"Stand firm together, in case some friend of Caesar's should happen—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Talk not of standing. Publius, good cheer!","There is no harm intended to your person,","Nor to no Roman else. So tell them, Publius."],"id":410,"m":"Don't talk about standing firm. Publius, cheer up! No harm is meant to you or to any other Roman. Tell them so, Publius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["And leave us, Publius; lest that the people","Rushing on us, should do your age some mischief."],"id":411,"m":"And leave us, Publius, in case the people rush at us and hurt you, an old man."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Do so; and let no man abide this deed","But we the doers."],"id":412,"m":"Do that. And let no one answer for this deed except us who did it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Trebonius.","id":413},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Where's Antony?"],"id":414,"m":"Where's Antony?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TREBONIUS","lines":["Fled to his house amaz'd.","Men, wives, and children stare, cry out, and run,","As it were doomsday."],"id":415,"m":"He ran to his house in shock. Men, wives and children are staring, crying out and running, as if it were the end of the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Fates, we will know your pleasures.","That we shall die, we know; 'tis but the time","And drawing days out, that men stand upon."],"id":416,"m":"Fates, we will soon know what you have in store for us. We know we will die; it's only the time, and how long they can spin their days out, that men care about."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASCA","lines":["Why, he that cuts off twenty years of life","Cuts off so many years of fearing death."],"id":417,"m":"Why, whoever cuts off twenty years of a man's life cuts off that many years of fearing death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Grant that, and then is death a benefit:","So are we Caesar's friends, that have abridg'd","His time of fearing death. Stoop, Romans, stoop,","And let us bathe our hands in Caesar's blood","Up to the elbows, and besmear our swords:","Then walk we forth, even to the market-place,","And waving our red weapons o'er our heads,","Let's all cry, \"Peace, freedom, and liberty!\""],"id":418,"m":"Accept that, and then death is a favour. So we are Caesar's friends, who have shortened his time of fearing death. Bend down, Romans, bend down, and let us bathe our hands in Caesar's blood up to the elbows and smear our swords with it. Then let's walk out, right to the market-place, and, waving our red weapons over our heads, all shout, \"Peace, freedom and liberty!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Stoop then, and wash. How many ages hence","Shall this our lofty scene be acted over","In States unborn, and accents yet unknown!"],"id":419,"m":"Bend down, then, and wash. How many ages from now will this noble scene of ours be acted again, in countries not yet born and in languages not yet known!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["How many times shall Caesar bleed in sport,","That now on Pompey's basis lies along,","No worthier than the dust!"],"id":420,"m":"How many times will Caesar bleed in plays, who now lies stretched out at the foot of Pompey's statue, worth no more than dust!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["So oft as that shall be,","So often shall the knot of us be call'd","The men that gave their country liberty."],"id":421,"m":"As often as that happens, our group will be called the men who gave their country liberty."},{"t":"sp","who":"DECIUS","lines":["What, shall we forth?"],"id":422,"m":"Well, shall we go out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Ay, every man away.","Brutus shall lead; and we will grace his heels","With the most boldest and best hearts of Rome."],"id":423,"m":"Yes, every man go. Brutus will lead, and we will follow at his heels in honour, the boldest and best hearts in Rome."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":424},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Soft, who comes here? A friend of Antony's."],"id":425,"m":"Wait, who's this coming? A friend of Antony's."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Thus, Brutus, did my master bid me kneel;","Thus did Mark Antony bid me fall down;","And, being prostrate, thus he bade me say:","Brutus is noble, wise, valiant, and honest;","Caesar was mighty, bold, royal, and loving;","Say I love Brutus and I honour him;","Say I fear'd Caesar, honour'd him, and lov'd him.","If Brutus will vouchsafe that Antony","May safely come to him, and be resolv'd","How Caesar hath deserv'd to lie in death,","Mark Antony shall not love Caesar dead","So well as Brutus living; but will follow","The fortunes and affairs of noble Brutus","Thorough the hazards of this untrod state,","With all true faith. So says my master Antony."],"id":426,"m":"Brutus, my master told me to kneel like this. Mark Antony told me to fall down like this, and when I lay at your feet, he told me to say this: Brutus is noble, wise, brave and honest; Caesar was mighty, bold, royal and loving. Say I love Brutus and honour him. Say I feared Caesar, honoured him and loved him. If Brutus will allow Antony to come to him safely and have it explained to him how Caesar deserved to die, Mark Antony will not love Caesar dead as much as Brutus living, but will follow noble Brutus's fortunes and affairs through all the dangers of this new state of things, with complete loyalty. That is what my master Antony says."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Thy master is a wise and valiant Roman;","I never thought him worse.","Tell him, so please him come unto this place,","He shall be satisfied and, by my honour,","Depart untouch'd."],"id":427,"m":"Your master is a wise and brave Roman. I never thought him anything less. Tell him that if he cares to come here, his questions will be answered, and, on my honour, he will leave unharmed."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I'll fetch him presently."],"id":428,"m":"I'll bring him at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":429},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I know that we shall have him well to friend."],"id":430,"m":"I know we will have him as a good friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I wish we may: but yet have I a mind","That fears him much; and my misgiving still","Falls shrewdly to the purpose."],"id":431,"m":"I hope so. But I still have a feeling that fears him a lot, and my misgivings always turn out to be uncomfortably close to the mark."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antony.","id":432},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["But here comes Antony. Welcome, Mark Antony."],"id":433,"m":"But here comes Antony. Welcome, Mark Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["O mighty Caesar! Dost thou lie so low?","Are all thy conquests, glories, triumphs, spoils,","Shrunk to this little measure? Fare thee well.","I know not, gentlemen, what you intend,","Who else must be let blood, who else is rank:","If I myself, there is no hour so fit","As Caesar's death's hour; nor no instrument","Of half that worth as those your swords, made rich","With the most noble blood of all this world.","I do beseech ye, if you bear me hard,","Now, whilst your purpled hands do reek and smoke,","Fulfill your pleasure. Live a thousand years,","I shall not find myself so apt to die.","No place will please me so, no means of death,","As here by Caesar, and by you cut off,","The choice and master spirits of this age."],"id":434,"m":"Oh, mighty Caesar! Are you lying so low? Have all your conquests, glories, triumphs and spoils shrunk to this small space? Goodbye. I don't know, gentlemen, what you intend, who else must have blood let, who else is diseased. If it's me, there is no hour as fitting as the hour of Caesar's death, and no weapon half as worthy as your swords, made rich with the noblest blood in the whole world. I beg you, if you have something against me, do what you want now, while your bloodstained hands are still steaming. If I live a thousand years, I won't find myself so ready to die. No place will please me as much, and no way of dying, as here beside Caesar, cut down by you, the finest and most powerful spirits of this age."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O Antony, beg not your death of us.","Though now we must appear bloody and cruel,","As by our hands and this our present act","You see we do; yet see you but our hands","And this the bleeding business they have done.","Our hearts you see not; they are pitiful;","And pity to the general wrong of Rome—","As fire drives out fire, so pity pity—","Hath done this deed on Caesar. For your part,","To you our swords have leaden points, Mark Antony;","Our arms in strength of malice, and our hearts","Of brothers' temper, do receive you in","With all kind love, good thoughts, and reverence."],"id":435,"m":"Oh, Antony, don't beg us for your death. Though we must look bloody and cruel now, as you can see from our hands and what we have just done, you only see our hands and the bleeding work they have done. You don't see our hearts. They are full of pity, and pity for the wrong done to all of Rome (as fire drives out fire, so pity drives out pity) has done this to Caesar. As for you, Mark Antony, our swords have blunt lead points. Our arms, though strong enough to seem hostile, and our hearts, full of brotherly feeling, welcome you in with all kind love, good thoughts and respect."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Your voice shall be as strong as any man's","In the disposing of new dignities."],"id":436,"m":"Your voice will be as strong as anyone's in handing out the new offices of state."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Only be patient till we have appeas'd","The multitude, beside themselves with fear,","And then we will deliver you the cause","Why I, that did love Caesar when I struck him,","Have thus proceeded."],"id":437,"m":"Only be patient until we have calmed the crowd, who are beside themselves with fear, and then we will explain to you why I, who loved Caesar even as I struck him, have acted as I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["I doubt not of your wisdom.","Let each man render me his bloody hand.","First, Marcus Brutus, will I shake with you;","Next, Caius Cassius, do I take your hand.","Now, Decius Brutus, yours; now yours, Metellus;","Yours, Cinna; and, my valiant Casca, yours;","Though last, not least in love, yours, good Trebonius.","Gentlemen all—alas, what shall I say?","My credit now stands on such slippery ground,","That one of two bad ways you must conceit me,","Either a coward or a flatterer.","That I did love thee, Caesar, O, 'tis true:","If then thy spirit look upon us now,","Shall it not grieve thee dearer than thy death,","To see thy Antony making his peace,","Shaking the bloody fingers of thy foes,","Most noble, in the presence of thy corse?","Had I as many eyes as thou hast wounds,","Weeping as fast as they stream forth thy blood,","It would become me better than to close","In terms of friendship with thine enemies.","Pardon me, Julius! Here wast thou bay'd, brave hart;","Here didst thou fall; and here thy hunters stand,","Sign'd in thy spoil, and crimson'd in thy lethe.","O world, thou wast the forest to this hart;","And this indeed, O world, the heart of thee.","How like a deer strucken by many princes,","Dost thou here lie!"],"id":438,"m":"I don't doubt your wisdom. Let each man give me his bloody hand. First, Marcus Brutus, I'll shake yours. Next, Caius Cassius, I take your hand. Now, Decius Brutus, yours; now yours, Metellus; yours, Cinna; and, my brave Casca, yours. Last, but not least in my love, yours, good Trebonius. Gentlemen all—oh, what can I say? My reputation now stands on such slippery ground that you must think of me in one of two bad ways, either as a coward or as a flatterer. That I loved you, Caesar, oh, it's true! If your spirit is looking down on us now, won't it grieve you more than your death to see your Antony making his peace, shaking the bloody fingers of your enemies, most noble Caesar, in the presence of your body? If I had as many eyes as you have wounds, weeping as fast as they pour out your blood, it would suit me better than making friends with your enemies. Forgive me, Julius! Here you were cornered, brave stag. Here you fell, and here your hunters stand, marked with your slaughter and red with your lifeblood. Oh, world, you were the forest for this stag, and this, oh world, was truly your heart. How like a deer struck down by many princes you lie here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Mark Antony,—"],"id":439,"m":"Mark Antony—"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Pardon me, Caius Cassius:","The enemies of Caesar shall say this;","Then, in a friend, it is cold modesty."],"id":440,"m":"Forgive me, Caius Cassius. Even Caesar's enemies will say this, so from a friend it is only cool restraint."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I blame you not for praising Caesar so;","But what compact mean you to have with us?","Will you be prick'd in number of our friends,","Or shall we on, and not depend on you?"],"id":441,"m":"I don't blame you for praising Caesar like this. But what agreement do you mean to make with us? Will you be counted as one of our friends, or shall we go on without relying on you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Therefore I took your hands; but was indeed","Sway'd from the point, by looking down on Caesar.","Friends am I with you all, and love you all,","Upon this hope, that you shall give me reasons","Why, and wherein, Caesar was dangerous."],"id":442,"m":"That's why I took your hands, but I was distracted from the point by looking down at Caesar. I am friends with you all and love you all, in the hope that you will give me reasons why and in what way Caesar was dangerous."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Or else were this a savage spectacle.","Our reasons are so full of good regard","That were you, Antony, the son of Caesar,","You should be satisfied."],"id":443,"m":"Otherwise this would be a savage sight. Our reasons are so well considered that even if you were Caesar's son, Antony, you would be satisfied."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["That's all I seek,","And am moreover suitor that I may","Produce his body to the market-place;","And in the pulpit, as becomes a friend,","Speak in the order of his funeral."],"id":444,"m":"That's all I ask. And I also ask that I may carry his body to the market-place and, in the pulpit, as a friend should, speak at his funeral."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You shall, Mark Antony."],"id":445,"m":"You may, Mark Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Brutus, a word with you.","[Aside to Brutus] You know not what you do. Do not consent","That Antony speak in his funeral.","Know you how much the people may be mov'd","By that which he will utter?"],"id":446,"m":"Brutus, a word with you. [Aside to Brutus] You don't know what you're doing. Don't agree to let Antony speak at his funeral. Do you know how much the people may be stirred up by what he will say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["[Aside to Cassius] By your pardon:","I will myself into the pulpit first,","And show the reason of our Caesar's death.","What Antony shall speak, I will protest","He speaks by leave and by permission;","And that we are contented Caesar shall","Have all true rights and lawful ceremonies.","It shall advantage more than do us wrong."],"id":447,"m":"[Aside to Cassius] Excuse me. I will go up into the pulpit first and explain the reason for Caesar's death. I'll announce that whatever Antony says, he says with our permission, and that we are happy for Caesar to have all the proper rites and lawful ceremonies. It will do us more good than harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["[Aside to Brutus] I know not what may fall; I like it not."],"id":448,"m":"[Aside to Brutus] I don't know what may happen. I don't like it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Mark Antony, here, take you Caesar's body.","You shall not in your funeral speech blame us,","But speak all good you can devise of Caesar,","And say you do't by our permission;","Else shall you not have any hand at all","About his funeral. And you shall speak","In the same pulpit whereto I am going,","After my speech is ended."],"id":449,"m":"Mark Antony, here, take Caesar's body. In your funeral speech you will not blame us, but say all the good you can think of about Caesar, and say you do it by our permission. Otherwise you won't have any part at all in his funeral. And you will speak in the same pulpit I am going to, after my speech is over."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Be it so;","I do desire no more."],"id":450,"m":"So be it. I want nothing more."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Prepare the body, then, and follow us."],"id":451,"m":"Prepare the body, then, and follow us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Antony","id":452},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["O, pardon me, thou bleeding piece of earth,","That I am meek and gentle with these butchers.","Thou art the ruins of the noblest man","That ever lived in the tide of times.","Woe to the hand that shed this costly blood!","Over thy wounds now do I prophesy,","Which, like dumb mouths do ope their ruby lips","To beg the voice and utterance of my tongue,","A curse shall light upon the limbs of men;","Domestic fury and fierce civil strife","Shall cumber all the parts of Italy;","Blood and destruction shall be so in use,","And dreadful objects so familiar,","That mothers shall but smile when they behold","Their infants quartered with the hands of war;","All pity chok'd with custom of fell deeds:","And Caesar's spirit, ranging for revenge,","With Ate by his side come hot from Hell,","Shall in these confines with a monarch's voice","Cry havoc and let slip the dogs of war,","That this foul deed shall smell above the earth","With carrion men, groaning for burial."],"id":453,"m":"Oh, forgive me, you bleeding piece of earth, for being meek and gentle with these butchers. You are the ruins of the noblest man who ever lived in the course of time. Woe to the hand that shed this precious blood! Over your wounds, which open their red lips like mouths that cannot speak, begging my tongue to speak for them, I now prophesy: a curse will fall on men's bodies. Fighting within families and fierce civil war will overrun every part of Italy. Blood and destruction will be so common, and dreadful sights so familiar, that mothers will only smile when they see their babies cut to pieces by the hands of war, all pity choked by the habit of cruel deeds. And Caesar's spirit, roaming for revenge, with Ate, goddess of ruin, hot from hell at his side, will cry \"Havoc!\" throughout this land in a king's voice and let loose the dogs of war, so that this foul deed will stink above the earth with rotting men groaning to be buried."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":454},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","cont":1,"lines":["You serve Octavius Caesar, do you not?"],"id":455,"m":"You serve Octavius Caesar, don't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I do, Mark Antony."],"id":456,"m":"I do, Mark Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Caesar did write for him to come to Rome."],"id":457,"m":"Caesar wrote for him to come to Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He did receive his letters, and is coming,","And bid me say to you by word of mouth,—","[Seeing the body] O Caesar!"],"id":458,"m":"He received the letters and is on his way, and he told me to say to you in person— [Seeing the body] Oh, Caesar!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Thy heart is big, get thee apart and weep.","Passion, I see, is catching; for mine eyes,","Seeing those beads of sorrow stand in thine,","Began to water. Is thy master coming?"],"id":459,"m":"Your heart is full. Go aside and weep. Grief is catching, I see, because my eyes, seeing those drops of sorrow in yours, began to water. Is your master coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He lies tonight within seven leagues of Rome."],"id":460,"m":"He is staying tonight within twenty miles of Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Post back with speed, and tell him what hath chanc'd.","Here is a mourning Rome, a dangerous Rome,","No Rome of safety for Octavius yet.","Hie hence, and tell him so. Yet stay awhile;","Thou shalt not back till I have borne this corse","Into the market-place: there shall I try,","In my oration, how the people take","The cruel issue of these bloody men;","According to the which thou shalt discourse","To young Octavius of the state of things.","Lend me your hand."],"id":461,"m":"Ride back fast and tell him what has happened. This is a mourning Rome, a dangerous Rome, not yet a safe Rome for Octavius. Hurry and tell him so. But wait a moment. Don't go back until I have carried this body into the market-place. There, in my speech, I will test how the people take the cruel act of these bloody men, and according to that you will report to young Octavius how things stand. Give me a hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt with Caesar's body","id":462},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. The Forum.","id":463},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Brutus and goes into the pulpit, and Cassius, with a throng of Citizens.","id":464},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["We will be satisfied; let us be satisfied."],"id":465,"m":"We want an explanation. Give us an explanation."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Then follow me, and give me audience, friends.","Cassius, go you into the other street","And part the numbers.","Those that will hear me speak, let 'em stay here;","Those that will follow Cassius, go with him;","And public reasons shall be rendered","Of Caesar's death."],"id":466,"m":"Then follow me and listen to me, friends. Cassius, you go into the other street and divide the crowd. Those who want to hear me speak, stay here. Those who want to follow Cassius, go with him. And the reasons for Caesar's death will be given publicly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["I will hear Brutus speak."],"id":467,"m":"I'll hear Brutus speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["I will hear Cassius; and compare their reasons,","When severally we hear them rendered."],"id":468,"m":"I'll hear Cassius, and we can compare their reasons when we've heard them separately."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Cassius, with some of the Citizens. Brutus goes into the rostrum","id":469},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["The noble Brutus is ascended: silence!"],"id":470,"m":"Noble Brutus has gone up. Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Be patient till the last.","Romans, countrymen, and lovers, hear me for my cause; and be silent,","that you may hear. Believe me for mine honour, and have respect to mine","honour, that you may believe. Censure me in your wisdom, and awake your","senses, that you may the better judge. If there be any in this","assembly, any dear friend of Caesar's, to him I say that Brutus' love","to Caesar was no less than his. If then that friend demand why Brutus","rose against Caesar, this is my answer: Not that I loved Caesar less,","but that I loved Rome more. Had you rather Caesar were living, and die","all slaves, than that Caesar were dead, to live all free men? As Caesar","loved me, I weep for him; as he was fortunate, I rejoice at it; as he","was valiant, I honour him; but, as he was ambitious, I slew him. There","is tears, for his love; joy for his fortune; honour for his valour; and","death, for his ambition. Who is here so base, that would be a bondman?","If any, speak; for him have I offended. Who is here so rude, that would","not be a Roman? If any, speak; for him have I offended. Who is here so","vile, that will not love his country? If any, speak; for him have I","offended. I pause for a reply."],"id":471,"m":"Be patient until I've finished. Romans, countrymen and friends, hear me for the sake of my cause, and be silent so that you can hear. Believe me because of my honour, and respect my honour so that you can believe me. Judge me with your wisdom, and wake up your minds so that you can judge better. If there is anyone in this crowd, any dear friend of Caesar's, I say to him that Brutus's love for Caesar was no less than his. If that friend then asks why Brutus rose against Caesar, this is my answer: not that I loved Caesar less, but that I loved Rome more. Would you rather Caesar were alive and all of you died slaves, than that Caesar were dead and all of you lived as free men? Because Caesar loved me, I weep for him; because he was fortunate, I rejoice at it; because he was brave, I honour him; but because he was ambitious, I killed him. There are tears for his love, joy for his good fortune, honour for his courage and death for his ambition. Who here is so low that he would be a slave? If anyone, speak, because I have offended him. Who here is so uncivilised that he would not be a Roman? If anyone, speak, because I have offended him. Who here is so vile that he will not love his country? If anyone, speak, because I have offended him. I pause for a reply."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["None, Brutus, none."],"id":472,"m":"No one, Brutus, no one."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Then none have I offended. I have done no more to Caesar than you shall","do to Brutus. The question of his death is enroll'd in the Capitol, his","glory not extenuated, wherein he was worthy; nor his offences enforc'd,","for which he suffered death."],"id":473,"m":"Then I have offended no one. I have done no more to Caesar than you would do to Brutus. The reasons for his death are recorded in the Capitol. His glory has not been played down where he deserved it, nor his offences exaggerated, for which he suffered death."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antony and others, with Caesar's body.","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes his body, mourned by Mark Antony, who, though he had no hand","in his death, shall receive the benefit of his dying, a place in the","commonwealth; as which of you shall not? With this I depart, that, as I","slew my best lover for the good of Rome, I have the same dagger for","myself, when it shall please my country to need my death."],"id":475,"m":"Here comes his body, mourned by Mark Antony, who, though he had no part in his death, will receive the benefit of his dying, a place in the free state, as will every one of you. With this I leave you: as I killed my best friend for the good of Rome, I have the same dagger for myself whenever my country needs my death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Live, Brutus! live, live!"],"id":476,"m":"Long live Brutus! Live, live!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Bring him with triumph home unto his house."],"id":477,"m":"Carry him home to his house in triumph."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Give him a statue with his ancestors."],"id":478,"m":"Give him a statue alongside his ancestors."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Let him be Caesar."],"id":479,"m":"Let him be Caesar."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Caesar's better parts","Shall be crown'd in Brutus."],"id":480,"m":"The best parts of Caesar will be crowned in Brutus."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["We'll bring him to his house with shouts and clamours."],"id":481,"m":"We'll take him to his house with shouting and cheering."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["My countrymen,—"],"id":482,"m":"My countrymen—"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Peace! Silence! Brutus speaks."],"id":483,"m":"Quiet! Silence! Brutus is speaking."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Peace, ho!"],"id":484,"m":"Quiet there!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Good countrymen, let me depart alone,","And, for my sake, stay here with Antony.","Do grace to Caesar's corpse, and grace his speech","Tending to Caesar's glories, which Mark Antony,","By our permission, is allow'd to make.","I do entreat you, not a man depart,","Save I alone, till Antony have spoke."],"id":485,"m":"Good countrymen, let me go alone, and, for my sake, stay here with Antony. Honour Caesar's body, and honour his speech about Caesar's glories, which Mark Antony is allowed to make by our permission. I beg you, let no one leave except me until Antony has spoken."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":486},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Stay, ho! and let us hear Mark Antony."],"id":487,"m":"Wait! Let's hear Mark Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Let him go up into the public chair.","We'll hear him. Noble Antony, go up."],"id":488,"m":"Let him go up to the public platform. We'll hear him. Noble Antony, go up."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["For Brutus' sake, I am beholding to you."],"id":489,"m":"For Brutus's sake, I am grateful to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Goes up","id":490},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["What does he say of Brutus?"],"id":491,"m":"What's he saying about Brutus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["He says, for Brutus' sake","He finds himself beholding to us all."],"id":492,"m":"He says that for Brutus's sake he is grateful to all of us."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["'Twere best he speak no harm of Brutus here!"],"id":493,"m":"He'd better not say anything bad about Brutus here!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["This Caesar was a tyrant."],"id":494,"m":"This Caesar was a tyrant."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Nay, that's certain.","We are blest that Rome is rid of him."],"id":495,"m":"Yes, that's certain. We are lucky Rome is rid of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Peace! let us hear what Antony can say."],"id":496,"m":"Quiet! Let's hear what Antony has to say."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["You gentle Romans,—"],"id":497,"m":"You gentle Romans—"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Peace, ho! let us hear him."],"id":498,"m":"Quiet! Let's hear him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears;","I come to bury Caesar, not to praise him.","The evil that men do lives after them,","The good is oft interred with their bones;","So let it be with Caesar. The noble Brutus","Hath told you Caesar was ambitious.","If it were so, it was a grievous fault,","And grievously hath Caesar answer'd it.","Here, under leave of Brutus and the rest,","For Brutus is an honourable man,","So are they all, all honourable men,","Come I to speak in Caesar's funeral.","He was my friend, faithful and just to me;","But Brutus says he was ambitious,","And Brutus is an honourable man.","He hath brought many captives home to Rome,","Whose ransoms did the general coffers fill:","Did this in Caesar seem ambitious?","When that the poor have cried, Caesar hath wept;","Ambition should be made of sterner stuff:","Yet Brutus says he was ambitious;","And Brutus is an honourable man.","You all did see that on the Lupercal","I thrice presented him a kingly crown,","Which he did thrice refuse. Was this ambition?","Yet Brutus says he was ambitious;","And sure he is an honourable man.","I speak not to disprove what Brutus spoke,","But here I am to speak what I do know.","You all did love him once, not without cause;","What cause withholds you then to mourn for him?","O judgement, thou art fled to brutish beasts,","And men have lost their reason. Bear with me.","My heart is in the coffin there with Caesar,","And I must pause till it come back to me."],"id":499,"m":"Friends, Romans, countrymen, listen to me. I have come to bury Caesar, not to praise him. The evil that men do lives on after them; the good is often buried with their bones. Let it be so with Caesar. The noble Brutus has told you that Caesar was ambitious. If that was so, it was a serious fault, and Caesar has paid seriously for it. Here, with the permission of Brutus and the rest (for Brutus is an honourable man; so are they all, all honourable men), I have come to speak at Caesar's funeral. He was my friend, faithful and fair to me. But Brutus says he was ambitious, and Brutus is an honourable man. He brought many captives home to Rome, whose ransoms filled the public treasury. Did this seem ambitious in Caesar? When the poor cried, Caesar wept. Ambition should be made of harder stuff. Yet Brutus says he was ambitious, and Brutus is an honourable man. You all saw that at the Lupercal I offered him a king's crown three times, and three times he refused it. Was this ambition? Yet Brutus says he was ambitious, and he is certainly an honourable man. I'm not speaking to disprove what Brutus said, but I am here to say what I know. You all loved him once, and not without reason. What reason stops you mourning for him, then? Oh, judgement, you have run away to the brute beasts, and men have lost their reason. Bear with me. My heart is in the coffin there with Caesar, and I must pause until it comes back to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Methinks there is much reason in his sayings."],"id":500,"m":"I think there's a lot of sense in what he says."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["If thou consider rightly of the matter,","Caesar has had great wrong."],"id":501,"m":"If you think about it properly, Caesar has been greatly wronged."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Has he, masters?","I fear there will a worse come in his place."],"id":502,"m":"Has he, friends? I'm afraid someone worse will come in his place."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Mark'd ye his words? He would not take the crown;","Therefore 'tis certain he was not ambitious."],"id":503,"m":"Did you notice what he said? He wouldn't take the crown. So it's certain he wasn't ambitious."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["If it be found so, some will dear abide it."],"id":504,"m":"If that turns out to be true, someone will pay dearly for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Poor soul, his eyes are red as fire with weeping."],"id":505,"m":"Poor man, his eyes are red as fire from weeping."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["There's not a nobler man in Rome than Antony."],"id":506,"m":"There isn't a nobler man in Rome than Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Now mark him; he begins again to speak."],"id":507,"m":"Now listen to him. He's starting to speak again."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["But yesterday the word of Caesar might","Have stood against the world; now lies he there,","And none so poor to do him reverence.","O masters! If I were dispos'd to stir","Your hearts and minds to mutiny and rage,","I should do Brutus wrong and Cassius wrong,","Who, you all know, are honourable men.","I will not do them wrong; I rather choose","To wrong the dead, to wrong myself and you,","Than I will wrong such honourable men.","But here's a parchment with the seal of Caesar,","I found it in his closet; 'tis his will:","Let but the commons hear this testament,","Which, pardon me, I do not mean to read,","And they would go and kiss dead Caesar's wounds,","And dip their napkins in his sacred blood;","Yea, beg a hair of him for memory,","And, dying, mention it within their wills,","Bequeathing it as a rich legacy","Unto their issue."],"id":508,"m":"Only yesterday the word of Caesar could have stood against the whole world. Now he lies there, and no one is so low as to show him respect. Oh, friends! If I wanted to stir your hearts and minds to rebellion and rage, I would wrong Brutus and wrong Cassius, who, you all know, are honourable men. I will not wrong them. I would rather wrong the dead, wrong myself and you, than wrong such honourable men. But here's a document with Caesar's seal. I found it in his study. It's his will. If the common people could only hear this will (which, forgive me, I don't intend to read), they would go and kiss dead Caesar's wounds and dip their handkerchiefs in his sacred blood, yes, beg for a hair of his to remember him by, and when they died they would mention it in their own wills, leaving it as a precious inheritance to their children."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["We'll hear the will. Read it, Mark Antony."],"id":509,"m":"We'll hear the will. Read it, Mark Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["The will, the will! We will hear Caesar's will."],"id":510,"m":"The will, the will! We want to hear Caesar's will."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Have patience, gentle friends, I must not read it.","It is not meet you know how Caesar loved you.","You are not wood, you are not stones, but men;","And being men, hearing the will of Caesar,","It will inflame you, it will make you mad.","'Tis good you know not that you are his heirs;","For if you should, O, what would come of it?"],"id":511,"m":"Be patient, gentle friends. I mustn't read it. It isn't right for you to know how much Caesar loved you. You are not wood, you are not stones, but men, and being men, hearing Caesar's will would inflame you, it would drive you mad. It's better you don't know you are his heirs, because if you did, oh, what would come of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Read the will! We'll hear it, Antony;","You shall read us the will, Caesar's will!"],"id":512,"m":"Read the will! We'll hear it, Antony. You must read us the will, Caesar's will!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Will you be patient? Will you stay awhile?","I have o'ershot myself to tell you of it.","I fear I wrong the honourable men","Whose daggers have stabb'd Caesar; I do fear it."],"id":513,"m":"Will you be patient? Will you wait a little? I have gone too far in telling you about it. I'm afraid I'm wronging the honourable men whose daggers stabbed Caesar. I really am afraid of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["They were traitors. Honourable men!"],"id":514,"m":"They were traitors. Honourable men!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["The will! The testament!"],"id":515,"m":"The will! The will!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["They were villains, murderers. The will! Read the will!"],"id":516,"m":"They were villains, murderers. The will! Read the will!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["You will compel me then to read the will?","Then make a ring about the corpse of Caesar,","And let me show you him that made the will.","Shall I descend? and will you give me leave?"],"id":517,"m":"So you will force me to read the will? Then make a ring around Caesar's body, and let me show you the man who made the will. Shall I come down? Will you allow me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Come down."],"id":518,"m":"Come down."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Descend."],"id":519,"m":"Come down."},{"t":"sd","text":"He comes down","id":520},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["You shall have leave."],"id":521,"m":"You have our permission."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["A ring! Stand round."],"id":522,"m":"A ring! Stand round."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Stand from the hearse, stand from the body."],"id":523,"m":"Stand back from the coffin, stand back from the body."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Room for Antony, most noble Antony!"],"id":524,"m":"Make room for Antony, most noble Antony!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Nay, press not so upon me; stand far off."],"id":525,"m":"No, don't crowd me like that. Stand further off."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Stand back; room! bear back."],"id":526,"m":"Stand back! Make room! Get back."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["If you have tears, prepare to shed them now.","You all do know this mantle. I remember","The first time ever Caesar put it on;","'Twas on a Summer's evening, in his tent,","That day he overcame the Nervii.","Look, in this place ran Cassius' dagger through:","See what a rent the envious Casca made:","Through this the well-beloved Brutus stabb'd;","And as he pluck'd his cursed steel away,","Mark how the blood of Caesar follow'd it,","As rushing out of doors, to be resolv'd","If Brutus so unkindly knock'd, or no;","For Brutus, as you know, was Caesar's angel.","Judge, O you gods, how dearly Caesar lov'd him.","This was the most unkindest cut of all;","For when the noble Caesar saw him stab,","Ingratitude, more strong than traitors' arms,","Quite vanquish'd him: then burst his mighty heart;","And in his mantle muffling up his face,","Even at the base of Pompey's statue","Which all the while ran blood, great Caesar fell.","O, what a fall was there, my countrymen!","Then I, and you, and all of us fell down,","Whilst bloody treason flourish'd over us.","O, now you weep; and I perceive you feel","The dint of pity. These are gracious drops.","Kind souls, what weep you when you but behold","Our Caesar's vesture wounded? Look you here,","Here is himself, marr'd, as you see, with traitors."],"id":527,"m":"If you have tears, get ready to shed them now. You all know this cloak. I remember the first time Caesar ever put it on. It was a summer evening in his tent, the day he defeated the Nervii. Look, here is where Cassius's dagger went through. See what a tear spiteful Casca made. Through this one the much-loved Brutus stabbed, and as he pulled his cursed steel away, see how Caesar's blood followed it, as if rushing out of doors to find out for certain whether it really was Brutus knocking so unkindly or not. Because Brutus, as you know, was Caesar's darling. Judge, you gods, how dearly Caesar loved him! This was the most unkind cut of all. Because when noble Caesar saw him stab, ingratitude, stronger than traitors' weapons, completely defeated him. Then his mighty heart burst, and, covering his face with his cloak, great Caesar fell at the foot of Pompey's statue, which ran with blood all the while. Oh, what a fall that was, my countrymen! Then I and you and all of us fell down, while bloody treason triumphed over us. Oh, now you are weeping, and I see you feel the force of pity. These are gracious tears. Kind souls, why do you weep when you only see our Caesar's clothes wounded? Look here, here is the man himself, mutilated, as you see, by traitors."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["O piteous spectacle!"],"id":528,"m":"Oh, pitiful sight!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["O noble Caesar!"],"id":529,"m":"Oh, noble Caesar!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["O woeful day!"],"id":530,"m":"Oh, terrible day!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["O traitors, villains!"],"id":531,"m":"Oh, traitors, villains!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["O most bloody sight!"],"id":532,"m":"Oh, most bloody sight!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["We will be revenged."],"id":533,"m":"We will have revenge."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Revenge,—about,—seek,—burn,—fire,—kill,—slay,—let not a traitor live!"],"id":534,"m":"Revenge! Go! Search! Burn! Fire! Kill! Slay! Don't let a traitor live!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Stay, countrymen."],"id":535,"m":"Wait, countrymen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Peace there! Hear the noble Antony."],"id":536,"m":"Quiet there! Listen to noble Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["We'll hear him, we'll follow him, we'll die with him."],"id":537,"m":"We'll listen to him, we'll follow him, we'll die with him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Good friends, sweet friends, let me not stir you up","To such a sudden flood of mutiny.","They that have done this deed are honourable.","What private griefs they have, alas, I know not,","That made them do it. They're wise and honourable,","And will, no doubt, with reasons answer you.","I come not, friends, to steal away your hearts.","I am no orator, as Brutus is;","But, as you know me all, a plain blunt man,","That love my friend; and that they know full well","That gave me public leave to speak of him.","For I have neither wit, nor words, nor worth,","Action, nor utterance, nor the power of speech,","To stir men's blood. I only speak right on.","I tell you that which you yourselves do know,","Show you sweet Caesar's wounds, poor poor dumb mouths,","And bid them speak for me. But were I Brutus,","And Brutus Antony, there were an Antony","Would ruffle up your spirits, and put a tongue","In every wound of Caesar, that should move","The stones of Rome to rise and mutiny."],"id":538,"m":"Good friends, sweet friends, don't let me stir you up into such a sudden flood of rebellion. The men who did this deed are honourable. What private grudges they had that made them do it, alas, I don't know. They are wise and honourable, and no doubt they will give you reasons. I haven't come, friends, to steal your hearts. I am no speaker, as Brutus is, but, as you all know, a plain, blunt man who loves his friend, and the men who gave me public permission to speak about him know that very well. Because I have neither cleverness, nor words, nor standing, nor gestures, nor delivery, nor the power of speech to stir men's blood. I just speak straight out. I tell you what you already know, show you sweet Caesar's wounds, poor, poor speechless mouths, and tell them to speak for me. But if I were Brutus, and Brutus were Antony, then there would be an Antony who would stir up your spirits and put a tongue in every one of Caesar's wounds that would move the very stones of Rome to rise up and rebel."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["We'll mutiny."],"id":539,"m":"We'll rebel."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["We'll burn the house of Brutus."],"id":540,"m":"We'll burn Brutus's house."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Away, then! come, seek the conspirators."],"id":541,"m":"Let's go, then! Come on, find the conspirators."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Yet hear me, countrymen; yet hear me speak."],"id":542,"m":"Still, listen to me, countrymen. Still, hear me speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Peace, ho! Hear Antony; most noble Antony."],"id":543,"m":"Quiet! Listen to Antony, most noble Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Why, friends, you go to do you know not what.","Wherein hath Caesar thus deserved your loves?","Alas, you know not; I must tell you then.","You have forgot the will I told you of."],"id":544,"m":"Why, friends, you're going off to do you don't know what. How has Caesar earned your love like this? Oh, you don't know. Then I must tell you. You have forgotten the will I told you about."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Most true; the will!—let's stay, and hear the will."],"id":545,"m":"Very true. The will! Let's stay and hear the will."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Here is the will, and under Caesar's seal.","To every Roman citizen he gives,","To every several man, seventy-five drachmas."],"id":546,"m":"Here is the will, under Caesar's seal. To every Roman citizen, to every single man, he gives seventy-five drachmas."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Most noble Caesar! We'll revenge his death."],"id":547,"m":"Most noble Caesar! We'll avenge his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["O, royal Caesar!"],"id":548,"m":"Oh, royal Caesar!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Hear me with patience."],"id":549,"m":"Listen to me patiently."},{"t":"sp","who":"CITIZENS","lines":["Peace, ho!"],"id":550,"m":"Quiet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Moreover, he hath left you all his walks,","His private arbors, and new-planted orchards,","On this side Tiber; he hath left them you,","And to your heirs forever; common pleasures,","To walk abroad, and recreate yourselves.","Here was a Caesar! when comes such another?"],"id":551,"m":"What's more, he has left you all his walks, his private gardens and his newly planted orchards on this side of the Tiber. He has left them to you and your heirs for ever, as public parks to walk in and enjoy yourselves. That was a Caesar! When will there be another like him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Never, never. Come, away, away!","We'll burn his body in the holy place,","And with the brands fire the traitors' houses.","Take up the body."],"id":552,"m":"Never, never! Come on, away, away! We'll burn his body in the holy place and use the burning wood to set fire to the traitors' houses. Pick up the body."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Go, fetch fire."],"id":553,"m":"Go and get fire."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Pluck down benches."],"id":554,"m":"Tear down benches."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Pluck down forms, windows, anything."],"id":555,"m":"Tear down seats, shutters, anything."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Citizens, with the body","id":556},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Now let it work. Mischief, thou art afoot,","Take thou what course thou wilt!"],"id":557,"m":"Now let it work. Trouble, you are on the move. Take whatever course you like!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":558},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","cont":1,"lines":["How now, fellow?"],"id":559,"m":"What is it, fellow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Sir, Octavius is already come to Rome."],"id":560,"m":"Sir, Octavius has already arrived in Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Where is he?"],"id":561,"m":"Where is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["He and Lepidus are at Caesar's house."],"id":562,"m":"He and Lepidus are at Caesar's house."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["And thither will I straight to visit him.","He comes upon a wish. Fortune is merry,","And in this mood will give us anything."],"id":563,"m":"And I'll go straight there to see him. He comes just when I wanted him. Fortune is in a good mood, and while she's like this she'll give us anything."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I heard him say Brutus and Cassius","Are rid like madmen through the gates of Rome."],"id":564,"m":"I heard him say that Brutus and Cassius have ridden like madmen out through the gates of Rome."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Belike they had some notice of the people,","How I had moved them. Bring me to Octavius."],"id":565,"m":"Perhaps they heard how I had stirred up the people. Take me to Octavius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":566},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"The same. A street.","id":567},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cinna, the poet, and after him the citizens.","id":568},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["I dreamt tonight that I did feast with Caesar,","And things unluckily charge my fantasy.","I have no will to wander forth of doors,","Yet something leads me forth."],"id":569,"m":"Last night I dreamt I was feasting with Caesar, and things weigh on my imagination like a bad omen. I have no wish to go out of doors, and yet something leads me out."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["What is your name?"],"id":570,"m":"What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Whither are you going?"],"id":571,"m":"Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Where do you dwell?"],"id":572,"m":"Where do you live?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Are you a married man or a bachelor?"],"id":573,"m":"Are you a married man or a bachelor?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["Answer every man directly."],"id":574,"m":"Answer every man straight."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Ay, and briefly."],"id":575,"m":"Yes, and briefly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Ay, and wisely."],"id":576,"m":"Yes, and wisely."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Ay, and truly, you were best."],"id":577,"m":"Yes, and truthfully, if you know what's good for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["What is my name? Whither am I going? Where do I dwell? Am I a married","man or a bachelor? Then, to answer every man directly and briefly,","wisely and truly. Wisely I say I am a bachelor."],"id":578,"m":"What's my name? Where am I going? Where do I live? Am I a married man or a bachelor? Then, to answer every man straight and briefly, wisely and truthfully: wisely, I say I'm a bachelor."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["That's as much as to say they are fools that marry; you'll bear me a","bang for that, I fear. Proceed, directly."],"id":579,"m":"That's as good as saying people who marry are fools. You'll get a punch from me for that, I'm afraid. Go on, straight."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["Directly, I am going to Caesar's funeral."],"id":580,"m":"Straight, I'm going to Caesar's funeral."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["As a friend, or an enemy?"],"id":581,"m":"As a friend or an enemy?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["As a friend."],"id":582,"m":"As a friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND CITIZEN","lines":["That matter is answered directly."],"id":583,"m":"That question's answered straight."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["For your dwelling, briefly."],"id":584,"m":"Where you live, briefly."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["Briefly, I dwell by the Capitol."],"id":585,"m":"Briefly, I live near the Capitol."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Your name, sir, truly."],"id":586,"m":"Your name, sir, truthfully."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["Truly, my name is Cinna."],"id":587,"m":"Truthfully, my name is Cinna."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST CITIZEN","lines":["Tear him to pieces! He's a conspirator."],"id":588,"m":"Tear him to pieces! He's a conspirator."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["I am Cinna the poet, I am Cinna the poet."],"id":589,"m":"I'm Cinna the poet, I'm Cinna the poet."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["Tear him for his bad verses, tear him for his bad verses."],"id":590,"m":"Tear him for his bad poems, tear him for his bad poems."},{"t":"sp","who":"CINNA THE POET","lines":["I am not Cinna the conspirator."],"id":591,"m":"I'm not Cinna the conspirator."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","lines":["It is no matter, his name's Cinna; pluck but his name out of his heart,","and turn him going."],"id":592,"m":"It doesn't matter. His name's Cinna. Just tear his name out of his heart and send him on his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","lines":["Tear him, tear him! Come; brands, ho! firebrands. To Brutus', to","Cassius'; burn all. Some to Decius' house, and some to Casca's, some to","Ligarius'. Away, go!"],"id":593,"m":"Tear him, tear him! Come on, torches! Burning torches! To Brutus's, to Cassius's, burn them all. Some to Decius's house, some to Casca's, some to Ligarius's. Away, go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":594},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Rome. A room in Antony's house.","id":595},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antony, Octavius and Lepidus, seated at a table.","id":596},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["These many then shall die; their names are prick'd."],"id":597,"m":"So these men will die. Their names are marked."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Your brother too must die; consent you, Lepidus?"],"id":598,"m":"Your brother must die too. Do you agree, Lepidus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LEPIDUS","lines":["I do consent,—"],"id":599,"m":"I agree—"},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Prick him down, Antony."],"id":600,"m":"Mark him down, Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEPIDUS","lines":["Upon condition Publius shall not live,","Who is your sister's son, Mark Antony."],"id":601,"m":"On condition that Publius doesn't live either, who is your sister's son, Mark Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["He shall not live; look, with a spot I damn him.","But, Lepidus, go you to Caesar's house;","Fetch the will hither, and we shall determine","How to cut off some charge in legacies."],"id":602,"m":"He won't live. Look, with a mark I condemn him. But, Lepidus, go to Caesar's house and bring the will here, and we'll work out how to cut some of the costs of his bequests."},{"t":"sp","who":"LEPIDUS","lines":["What, shall I find you here?"],"id":603,"m":"What, will I find you here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Or here, or at the Capitol."],"id":604,"m":"Either here or at the Capitol."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lepidus","id":605},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["This is a slight unmeritable man,","Meet to be sent on errands. Is it fit,","The three-fold world divided, he should stand","One of the three to share it?"],"id":606,"m":"He is an insignificant, worthless man, fit to be sent on errands. Is it right, when the world is divided into three, that he should be one of the three to share it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["So you thought him,","And took his voice who should be prick'd to die","In our black sentence and proscription."],"id":607,"m":"That's what you thought of him, and you took his opinion on who should be marked down to die in our black list of death sentences."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Octavius, I have seen more days than you;","And though we lay these honours on this man,","To ease ourselves of divers sland'rous loads,","He shall but bear them as the ass bears gold,","To groan and sweat under the business,","Either led or driven, as we point the way;","And having brought our treasure where we will,","Then take we down his load, and turn him off,","Like to the empty ass, to shake his ears,","And graze in commons."],"id":608,"m":"Octavius, I have lived longer than you. And though we give these honours to this man to take some of the blame off ourselves, he will only carry them as a donkey carries gold, groaning and sweating under the work, either led or driven, as we point the way. And when he has carried our treasure where we want it, then we take off his load and turn him out, like an unloaded donkey, to shake his ears and graze on the common."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["You may do your will;","But he's a tried and valiant soldier."],"id":609,"m":"You can do what you like, but he's an experienced and brave soldier."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["So is my horse, Octavius; and for that","I do appoint him store of provender.","It is a creature that I teach to fight,","To wind, to stop, to run directly on,","His corporal motion govern'd by my spirit.","And, in some taste, is Lepidus but so:","He must be taught, and train'd, and bid go forth:","A barren-spirited fellow; one that feeds","On objects, arts, and imitations,","Which, out of use and stal'd by other men,","Begin his fashion. Do not talk of him","But as a property. And now, Octavius,","Listen great things. Brutus and Cassius","Are levying powers; we must straight make head.","Therefore let our alliance be combin'd,","Our best friends made, our means stretch'd;","And let us presently go sit in council,","How covert matters may be best disclos'd,","And open perils surest answered."],"id":610,"m":"So is my horse, Octavius, and for that I give him a supply of food. It is a creature I teach to fight, to turn, to stop, to run straight on, its bodily movements controlled by my mind. And in some ways Lepidus is just like that. He has to be taught and trained and told to go. He's a man with no ideas of his own, who lives on curiosities, skills and fashions that have already been used up and grown stale with other men before he takes them up. Don't talk of him except as a tool. And now, Octavius, listen to important things. Brutus and Cassius are raising armies. We must raise a force at once. So let's join our alliance together, gather our best friends and stretch our resources as far as they will go, and let's sit down in council at once to decide how hidden dangers can best be discovered and open dangers most safely met."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Let us do so: for we are at the stake,","And bay'd about with many enemies;","And some that smile have in their hearts, I fear,","Millions of mischiefs."],"id":611,"m":"Let's do that, because we are like a bear tied to the stake, surrounded by many enemies barking at us. And some who smile have millions of evil plans in their hearts, I fear."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":612},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Before Brutus' tent, in the camp near Sardis.","id":613},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum. Enter Brutus, Lucilius, Titinius and Soldiers; Pindarus meeting them; Lucius at some distance.","id":614},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Stand, ho!"],"id":615,"m":"Halt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["Give the word, ho! and stand."],"id":616,"m":"Pass on the order! Halt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What now, Lucilius! is Cassius near?"],"id":617,"m":"What news, Lucilius? Is Cassius near?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["He is at hand, and Pindarus is come","To do you salutation from his master."],"id":618,"m":"He's close by, and Pindarus has come to greet you from his master."},{"t":"sd","text":"Pindarus gives a letter to Brutus","id":619},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He greets me well. Your master, Pindarus,","In his own change, or by ill officers,","Hath given me some worthy cause to wish","Things done, undone: but, if he be at hand,","I shall be satisfied."],"id":620,"m":"He sends his greetings by a good man. Your master, Pindarus, either through his own change of heart or through bad officers, has given me good reason to wish some things undone that have been done. But if he's nearby, I'll get an explanation."},{"t":"sp","who":"PINDARUS","lines":["I do not doubt","But that my noble master will appear","Such as he is, full of regard and honour."],"id":621,"m":"I'm sure my noble master will show himself as he is, worthy of respect and honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He is not doubted. A word, Lucilius;","How he received you, let me be resolv'd."],"id":622,"m":"I don't doubt him. A word, Lucilius. Tell me how he received you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["With courtesy and with respect enough,","But not with such familiar instances,","Nor with such free and friendly conference,","As he hath us'd of old."],"id":623,"m":"With courtesy and with enough respect, but not with the friendly gestures or such free and open talk as he used to."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Thou hast describ'd","A hot friend cooling. Ever note, Lucilius,","When love begins to sicken and decay","It useth an enforced ceremony.","There are no tricks in plain and simple faith;","But hollow men, like horses hot at hand,","Make gallant show and promise of their mettle;"],"id":624,"m":"You have described a warm friend cooling. Always notice, Lucilius, that when love begins to sicken and decay, it uses forced politeness. There are no tricks in plain and simple loyalty. But hollow men, like horses that are lively when held, make a fine show and promise of their spirit,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Low march within","id":625},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["But when they should endure the bloody spur,","They fall their crests, and like deceitful jades","Sink in the trial. Comes his army on?"],"id":626,"m":"but when they should endure the bloody spur, they drop their heads and, like worthless old nags, fail when tested. Is his army coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["They meant this night in Sardis to be quarter'd;","The greater part, the horse in general,","Are come with Cassius."],"id":627,"m":"They meant to camp tonight in Sardis. Most of them, all the cavalry, have come with Cassius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Cassius and Soldiers.","id":628},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Hark! he is arriv'd.","March gently on to meet him."],"id":629,"m":"Listen! He has arrived. March slowly forward to meet him."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Stand, ho!"],"id":630,"m":"Halt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Stand, ho! Speak the word along."],"id":631,"m":"Halt! Pass the order along."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Stand!"],"id":632,"m":"Halt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SOLDIER","lines":["Stand!"],"id":633,"m":"Halt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SOLDIER","lines":["Stand!"],"id":634,"m":"Halt!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Most noble brother, you have done me wrong."],"id":635,"m":"Most noble brother, you have wronged me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Judge me, you gods; wrong I mine enemies?","And if not so, how should I wrong a brother?"],"id":636,"m":"Judge me, you gods! Do I wrong even my enemies? And if not, how would I wrong a brother?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Brutus, this sober form of yours hides wrongs;","And when you do them—"],"id":637,"m":"Brutus, this serious manner of yours hides wrongs, and when you do them—"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Cassius, be content.","Speak your griefs softly, I do know you well.","Before the eyes of both our armies here,","Which should perceive nothing but love from us,","Let us not wrangle. Bid them move away;","Then in my tent, Cassius, enlarge your griefs,","And I will give you audience."],"id":638,"m":"Cassius, calm down. Speak your complaints quietly. I know you well. In front of both our armies here, who should see nothing but love from us, let's not quarrel. Tell them to move away. Then in my tent, Cassius, set out your complaints in full, and I will listen."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Pindarus,","Bid our commanders lead their charges off","A little from this ground."],"id":639,"m":"Pindarus, tell our commanders to lead their troops a little way off from here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Lucilius, do you the like; and let no man","Come to our tent till we have done our conference.","Lucius and Titinius, guard our door."],"id":640,"m":"Lucilius, you do the same, and let no one come to our tent until we've finished our talk. Lucius and Titinius, guard our door."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":641},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Within the tent of Brutus.","id":642},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Brutus and Cassius.","id":643},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["That you have wrong'd me doth appear in this:","You have condemn'd and noted Lucius Pella","For taking bribes here of the Sardians;","Wherein my letters, praying on his side","Because I knew the man, were slighted off."],"id":644,"m":"That you have wronged me is clear from this: you have condemned and disgraced Lucius Pella for taking bribes here from the people of Sardis, and my letters pleading for him, because I knew the man, were brushed aside."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You wrong'd yourself to write in such a case."],"id":645,"m":"You wronged yourself by writing in such a case."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["In such a time as this it is not meet","That every nice offence should bear his comment."],"id":646,"m":"At a time like this it isn't right that every petty offence should be criticised."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Let me tell you, Cassius, you yourself","Are much condemn'd to have an itching palm,","To sell and mart your offices for gold","To undeservers."],"id":647,"m":"Let me tell you, Cassius, that you yourself are much blamed for having an itching palm, for selling and trading your offices for gold to people who don't deserve them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I an itching palm!","You know that you are Brutus that speak this,","Or, by the gods, this speech were else your last."],"id":648,"m":"I have an itching palm! You know that you are Brutus, saying this, or, by the gods, this speech would be your last."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The name of Cassius honours this corruption,","And chastisement doth therefore hide his head."],"id":649,"m":"The name of Cassius makes this corruption look respectable, so punishment hides its head."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Chastisement!"],"id":650,"m":"Punishment!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Remember March, the Ides of March remember:","Did not great Julius bleed for justice' sake?","What villain touch'd his body, that did stab,","And not for justice? What! Shall one of us,","That struck the foremost man of all this world","But for supporting robbers, shall we now","Contaminate our fingers with base bribes,","And sell the mighty space of our large honours","For so much trash as may be grasped thus?","I had rather be a dog, and bay the moon,","Than such a Roman."],"id":651,"m":"Remember March, remember the fifteenth of March. Didn't great Julius bleed for the sake of justice? What villain touched his body and stabbed him, except for justice? What, shall one of us who struck down the greatest man in all this world just for protecting robbers, shall we now dirty our fingers with low bribes and sell the great range of our high honours for as much trash as can be grabbed like this? I would rather be a dog and howl at the moon than be a Roman like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Brutus, bait not me,","I'll not endure it. You forget yourself,","To hedge me in. I am a soldier, I,","Older in practice, abler than yourself","To make conditions."],"id":652,"m":"Brutus, don't bait me. I won't stand for it. You forget yourself when you try to restrict me. I am a soldier, I am, more experienced and better able than you to manage affairs."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Go to; you are not, Cassius."],"id":653,"m":"Come on, you're not, Cassius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I am."],"id":654,"m":"I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I say you are not."],"id":655,"m":"I say you're not."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Urge me no more, I shall forget myself;","Have mind upon your health, tempt me no farther."],"id":656,"m":"Don't push me any further, or I'll forget myself. Think of your safety. Don't provoke me any more."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Away, slight man!"],"id":657,"m":"Go away, you worthless man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Is't possible?"],"id":658,"m":"Is this possible?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Hear me, for I will speak.","Must I give way and room to your rash choler?","Shall I be frighted when a madman stares?"],"id":659,"m":"Listen to me, because I will speak. Must I give way to your hot temper? Shall I be frightened when a madman stares at me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["O ye gods, ye gods! Must I endure all this?"],"id":660,"m":"Oh, you gods, you gods! Must I put up with all this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["All this? ay, more: fret till your proud heart break;","Go show your slaves how choleric you are,","And make your bondmen tremble. Must I budge?","Must I observe you? Must I stand and crouch","Under your testy humour? By the gods,","You shall digest the venom of your spleen,","Though it do split you; for, from this day forth,","I'll use you for my mirth, yea, for my laughter,","When you are waspish."],"id":661,"m":"All this? Yes, more. Fume until your proud heart breaks. Go and show your slaves how bad-tempered you are, and make your servants tremble. Must I flinch? Must I treat you with respect? Must I stand and cower under your irritable mood? By the gods, you will swallow the poison of your own anger, even if it splits you open, because from today on I'll use you for my amusement, yes, for my laughter, when you are being waspish."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Is it come to this?"],"id":662,"m":"Has it come to this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You say you are a better soldier:","Let it appear so; make your vaunting true,","And it shall please me well. For mine own part,","I shall be glad to learn of noble men."],"id":663,"m":"You say you are a better soldier. Let it be seen. Prove your boasting true, and I'll be very pleased. For my part, I'll be glad to learn from noble men."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["You wrong me every way, you wrong me, Brutus.","I said, an elder soldier, not a better:","Did I say better?"],"id":664,"m":"You wrong me in every way. You wrong me, Brutus. I said an older soldier, not a better one. Did I say better?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["If you did, I care not."],"id":665,"m":"If you did, I don't care."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["When Caesar liv'd, he durst not thus have mov'd me."],"id":666,"m":"When Caesar was alive, he wouldn't have dared to provoke me like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Peace, peace! you durst not so have tempted him."],"id":667,"m":"Quiet, quiet! You wouldn't have dared to provoke him like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I durst not?"],"id":668,"m":"I wouldn't have dared?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No."],"id":669,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["What? durst not tempt him?"],"id":670,"m":"What? Wouldn't have dared to provoke him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["For your life you durst not."],"id":671,"m":"Not for your life."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Do not presume too much upon my love.","I may do that I shall be sorry for."],"id":672,"m":"Don't count too much on my love. I may do something I'll be sorry for."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You have done that you should be sorry for.","There is no terror, Cassius, in your threats,","For I am arm'd so strong in honesty,","That they pass by me as the idle wind,","Which I respect not. I did send to you","For certain sums of gold, which you denied me;","For I can raise no money by vile means:","By Heaven, I had rather coin my heart,","And drop my blood for drachmas, than to wring","From the hard hands of peasants their vile trash","By any indirection. I did send","To you for gold to pay my legions,","Which you denied me: was that done like Cassius?","Should I have answer'd Caius Cassius so?","When Marcus Brutus grows so covetous,","To lock such rascal counters from his friends,","Be ready, gods, with all your thunderbolts,","Dash him to pieces!"],"id":673,"m":"You have done something you should be sorry for. There is nothing frightening in your threats, Cassius, because I am so strongly armed with honesty that they pass me by like the empty wind, which I ignore. I sent to you for certain sums of gold, which you refused me, because I can't raise money by dishonest means. By heaven, I would rather mint my heart into coins and drop my blood for drachmas than squeeze their wretched pennies out of the hard hands of peasants by crooked means. I sent to you for gold to pay my legions, which you refused me. Was that done like Cassius? Would I have answered Caius Cassius like that? When Marcus Brutus grows so greedy that he locks such worthless coins away from his friends, be ready, gods, with all your thunderbolts, and smash him to pieces!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I denied you not."],"id":674,"m":"I didn't refuse you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["You did."],"id":675,"m":"You did."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I did not. He was but a fool","That brought my answer back. Brutus hath riv'd my heart.","A friend should bear his friend's infirmities;","But Brutus makes mine greater than they are."],"id":676,"m":"I did not. The man who brought back my answer was a fool. Brutus has split my heart. A friend should put up with his friend's weaknesses, but Brutus makes mine bigger than they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I do not, till you practise them on me."],"id":677,"m":"I don't, until you use them against me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["You love me not."],"id":678,"m":"You don't love me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I do not like your faults."],"id":679,"m":"I don't like your faults."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["A friendly eye could never see such faults."],"id":680,"m":"A friendly eye could never see such faults."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["A flatterer's would not, though they do appear","As huge as high Olympus."],"id":681,"m":"A flatterer's eye wouldn't, even if they looked as huge as high Mount Olympus."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Come, Antony, and young Octavius, come,","Revenge yourselves alone on Cassius,","For Cassius is a-weary of the world:","Hated by one he loves; brav'd by his brother;","Check'd like a bondman; all his faults observ'd,","Set in a note-book, learn'd and conn'd by rote,","To cast into my teeth. O, I could weep","My spirit from mine eyes! There is my dagger,","And here my naked breast; within, a heart","Dearer than Plutus' mine, richer than gold:","If that thou be'st a Roman, take it forth.","I, that denied thee gold, will give my heart:","Strike as thou didst at Caesar; for I know,","When thou didst hate him worst, thou lovedst him better","Than ever thou lovedst Cassius."],"id":682,"m":"Come, Antony, and young Octavius, come, take your revenge on Cassius alone, because Cassius is tired of the world: hated by someone he loves, defied by his brother, scolded like a slave, all his faults watched, written in a notebook, studied and learned by heart to throw in my face. Oh, I could weep my spirit out through my eyes! There is my dagger, and here is my bare chest, and inside it a heart more precious than the mines of Plutus, god of riches, richer than gold. If you are a Roman, take it out. I, who refused you gold, will give you my heart. Strike as you did at Caesar, because I know that when you hated him most, you loved him more than you ever loved Cassius."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Sheathe your dagger.","Be angry when you will, it shall have scope;","Do what you will, dishonour shall be humour.","O Cassius, you are yoked with a lamb","That carries anger as the flint bears fire,","Who, much enforced, shows a hasty spark,","And straight is cold again."],"id":683,"m":"Put your dagger away. Be angry when you like, it will be allowed. Do what you like, your insults will be put down to your mood. Oh, Cassius, you are yoked together with a lamb who carries anger as flint carries fire: struck hard, it gives off a quick spark and is cold again at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Hath Cassius liv'd","To be but mirth and laughter to his Brutus,","When grief and blood ill-temper'd vexeth him?"],"id":684,"m":"Has Cassius lived only to be a joke and a laughing stock to his Brutus, when grief and bad temper trouble him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["When I spoke that, I was ill-temper'd too."],"id":685,"m":"When I said that, I was bad-tempered too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Do you confess so much? Give me your hand."],"id":686,"m":"Do you admit that much? Give me your hand."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["And my heart too."],"id":687,"m":"And my heart too."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["O Brutus!"],"id":688,"m":"Oh, Brutus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What's the matter?"],"id":689,"m":"What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Have not you love enough to bear with me,","When that rash humour which my mother gave me","Makes me forgetful?"],"id":690,"m":"Don't you love me enough to be patient with me when the hot temper my mother gave me makes me forget myself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Yes, Cassius; and from henceforth,","When you are over-earnest with your Brutus,","He'll think your mother chides, and leave you so."],"id":691,"m":"Yes, Cassius. And from now on, when you are too harsh with your Brutus, he'll think it's your mother scolding and leave it at that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Poet, followed by Lucilius, Titinius and Lucius.","id":692},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["[Within] Let me go in to see the generals,","There is some grudge between 'em; 'tis not meet","They be alone."],"id":693,"m":"[Within] Let me in to see the generals. There is some quarrel between them. It isn't right for them to be alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["[Within] You shall not come to them."],"id":694,"m":"[Within] You shall not go to them."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["[Within] Nothing but death shall stay me."],"id":695,"m":"[Within] Nothing but death will stop me."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["How now! What's the matter?"],"id":696,"m":"What's this? What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["For shame, you generals! What do you mean?","Love, and be friends, as two such men should be;","For I have seen more years, I'm sure, than ye."],"id":697,"m":"Shame on you, generals! What do you think you're doing? Love each other and be friends, as two such men should be, because I've seen more years than you, I'm sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Ha, ha! How vilely doth this cynic rhyme!"],"id":698,"m":"Ha, ha! How badly this rude philosopher rhymes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Get you hence, sirrah. Saucy fellow, hence!"],"id":699,"m":"Get out of here, fellow. Cheeky man, get out!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Bear with him, Brutus; 'tis his fashion."],"id":700,"m":"Put up with him, Brutus. It's his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I'll know his humour when he knows his time.","What should the wars do with these jigging fools?","Companion, hence!"],"id":701,"m":"I'll put up with his whims when he knows the right time for them. What use are these jingling fools in a war? Get out, fellow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Away, away, be gone!"],"id":702,"m":"Away, away, go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Poet","id":703},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Lucilius and Titinius, bid the commanders","Prepare to lodge their companies tonight."],"id":704,"m":"Lucilius and Titinius, tell the commanders to get their troops ready to camp for the night."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["And come yourselves and bring Messala with you","Immediately to us."],"id":705,"m":"And come back yourselves, and bring Messala with you to us at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucilius and Titinius","id":706},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Lucius, a bowl of wine."],"id":707,"m":"Lucius, a bowl of wine."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lucius","id":708},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I did not think you could have been so angry."],"id":709,"m":"I didn't think you could be so angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O Cassius, I am sick of many griefs."],"id":710,"m":"Oh, Cassius, I am sick with many sorrows."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Of your philosophy you make no use,","If you give place to accidental evils."],"id":711,"m":"You're not making any use of your philosophy if you give in to the evils that chance brings."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No man bears sorrow better. Portia is dead."],"id":712,"m":"No man bears sorrow better. Portia is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Ha? Portia?"],"id":713,"m":"What? Portia?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["She is dead."],"id":714,"m":"She is dead."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["How 'scap'd I killing, when I cross'd you so?","O insupportable and touching loss!","Upon what sickness?"],"id":715,"m":"How did I escape being killed when I crossed you like that? Oh, unbearable and painful loss! What illness was it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Impatient of my absence,","And grief that young Octavius with Mark Antony","Have made themselves so strong; for with her death","That tidings came. With this she fell distract,","And, her attendants absent, swallow'd fire."],"id":716,"m":"She couldn't bear my absence, and she grieved that young Octavius and Mark Antony had made themselves so strong, because that news came with the news of her death. At this she went out of her mind and, while her servants were away, swallowed burning coals."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["And died so?"],"id":717,"m":"And died like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Even so."],"id":718,"m":"Just like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["O ye immortal gods!"],"id":719,"m":"Oh, you immortal gods!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius, with wine and a taper.","id":720},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Speak no more of her. Give me a bowl of wine.","In this I bury all unkindness, Cassius."],"id":721,"m":"Don't talk about her any more. Give me a bowl of wine. In this I bury all our unkindness, Cassius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drinks","id":722},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["My heart is thirsty for that noble pledge.","Fill, Lucius, till the wine o'erswell the cup.","I cannot drink too much of Brutus' love."],"id":723,"m":"My heart is thirsty for that noble toast. Fill it, Lucius, until the wine overflows the cup. I can't drink too much of Brutus's love."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drinks","id":724},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lucius","id":725},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titinius and Messala.","id":726},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come in, Titinius!","Welcome, good Messala.","Now sit we close about this taper here,","And call in question our necessities."],"id":727,"m":"Come in, Titinius! Welcome, good Messala. Now let's sit close round this candle here and discuss what we need to do."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Portia, art thou gone?"],"id":728,"m":"Portia, are you gone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No more, I pray you.","Messala, I have here received letters,","That young Octavius and Mark Antony","Come down upon us with a mighty power,","Bending their expedition toward Philippi."],"id":729,"m":"No more, please. Messala, I have received letters here saying that young Octavius and Mark Antony are coming down on us with a mighty army, heading their expedition towards Philippi."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Myself have letters of the selfsame tenor."],"id":730,"m":"I have letters saying the very same."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["With what addition?"],"id":731,"m":"With what else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["That by proscription and bills of outlawry","Octavius, Antony, and Lepidus","Have put to death an hundred Senators."],"id":732,"m":"That by death lists and decrees of outlawry Octavius, Antony and Lepidus have put a hundred senators to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Therein our letters do not well agree.","Mine speak of seventy Senators that died","By their proscriptions, Cicero being one."],"id":733,"m":"Our letters don't agree there. Mine speak of seventy senators who died on their death lists, Cicero among them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Cicero one!"],"id":734,"m":"Cicero one of them!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Cicero is dead,","And by that order of proscription.","Had you your letters from your wife, my lord?"],"id":735,"m":"Cicero is dead, and by that same death list. Did you have letters from your wife, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No, Messala."],"id":736,"m":"No, Messala."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Nor nothing in your letters writ of her?"],"id":737,"m":"And nothing written about her in your letters?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Nothing, Messala."],"id":738,"m":"Nothing, Messala."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["That, methinks, is strange."],"id":739,"m":"I think that's strange."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why ask you? Hear you aught of her in yours?"],"id":740,"m":"Why do you ask? Do you hear anything about her in yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["No, my lord."],"id":741,"m":"No, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Now as you are a Roman, tell me true."],"id":742,"m":"Now, as you are a Roman, tell me the truth."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Then like a Roman bear the truth I tell,","For certain she is dead, and by strange manner."],"id":743,"m":"Then bear the truth I tell you like a Roman: she is certainly dead, and in a strange way."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why, farewell, Portia. We must die, Messala.","With meditating that she must die once,","I have the patience to endure it now."],"id":744,"m":"Why, farewell, Portia. We must all die, Messala. By thinking that she had to die some time, I have the patience to bear it now."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Even so great men great losses should endure."],"id":745,"m":"That is how great men should bear great losses."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I have as much of this in art as you,","But yet my nature could not bear it so."],"id":746,"m":"I know as much of this philosophy as you do, but my nature couldn't bear it like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Well, to our work alive. What do you think","Of marching to Philippi presently?"],"id":747,"m":"Well, to our work among the living. What do you think of marching to Philippi at once?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I do not think it good."],"id":748,"m":"I don't think it's a good idea."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Your reason?"],"id":749,"m":"Your reason?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["This it is:","'Tis better that the enemy seek us;","So shall he waste his means, weary his soldiers,","Doing himself offence, whilst we, lying still,","Are full of rest, defence, and nimbleness."],"id":750,"m":"It's this. It's better that the enemy should come looking for us. That way he will use up his supplies and tire his soldiers, harming himself, while we stay put, well rested, ready to defend ourselves and quick to move."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Good reasons must of force give place to better.","The people 'twixt Philippi and this ground","Do stand but in a forced affection;","For they have grudg'd us contribution.","The enemy, marching along by them,","By them shall make a fuller number up,","Come on refresh'd, new-added, and encourag'd;","From which advantage shall we cut him off","If at Philippi we do face him there,","These people at our back."],"id":751,"m":"Good reasons must give way to better ones. The people between Philippi and here are only on our side because they have been forced to be, since they have resented giving us supplies. The enemy, marching through their country, will add to his numbers from them and come on refreshed, reinforced and encouraged. We will cut him off from that advantage if we face him at Philippi, with these people behind us."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Hear me, good brother."],"id":752,"m":"Listen to me, good brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Under your pardon. You must note besides,","That we have tried the utmost of our friends,","Our legions are brim-full, our cause is ripe.","The enemy increaseth every day;","We, at the height, are ready to decline.","There is a tide in the affairs of men,","Which, taken at the flood, leads on to fortune;","Omitted, all the voyage of their life","Is bound in shallows and in miseries.","On such a full sea are we now afloat,","And we must take the current when it serves,","Or lose our ventures."],"id":753,"m":"Excuse me. You must also bear in mind that we have got all we can from our friends. Our legions are full to the brim, our cause is ripe. The enemy grows stronger every day; we, at our peak, are about to decline. There is a tide in the affairs of men which, taken at high water, leads on to fortune. If it is missed, the whole voyage of their life is stuck in shallows and misery. We are now afloat on such a high sea, and we must take the current while it is with us or lose everything we have risked."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Then, with your will, go on:","We'll along ourselves, and meet them at Philippi."],"id":754,"m":"Then, as you wish, go on. We'll go ourselves and meet them at Philippi."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["The deep of night is crept upon our talk,","And nature must obey necessity,","Which we will niggard with a little rest.","There is no more to say?"],"id":755,"m":"It has grown late at night while we've been talking, and our bodies must give in to their needs, which we will grudgingly satisfy with a little rest. Is there anything more to say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["No more. Good night:","Early tomorrow will we rise, and hence."],"id":756,"m":"No more. Good night. We'll get up early tomorrow and go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius.","id":757},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Lucius! My gown."],"id":758,"m":"Lucius! My dressing gown."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Lucius","id":759},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Farewell now, good Messala.","Good night, Titinius. Noble, noble Cassius,","Good night, and good repose."],"id":760,"m":"Goodbye now, good Messala. Good night, Titinius. Noble, noble Cassius, good night, and rest well."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["O my dear brother!","This was an ill beginning of the night.","Never come such division 'tween our souls!","Let it not, Brutus."],"id":761,"m":"Oh, my dear brother! This was a bad start to the night. Let such a division never come between our souls again! Don't let it, Brutus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius with the gown.","id":762},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Everything is well."],"id":763,"m":"Everything is fine."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Good night, my lord."],"id":764,"m":"Good night, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Good night, good brother."],"id":765,"m":"Good night, good brother."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS AND MESSALA","lines":["Good night, Lord Brutus."],"id":766,"m":"Good night, Lord Brutus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Farewell, everyone."],"id":767,"m":"Goodbye, everyone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Cassius, Titinius and Messala","id":768},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Give me the gown. Where is thy instrument?"],"id":769,"m":"Give me the gown. Where is your instrument?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Here in the tent."],"id":770,"m":"Here in the tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["What, thou speak'st drowsily?","Poor knave, I blame thee not, thou art o'er-watch'd.","Call Claudius and some other of my men;","I'll have them sleep on cushions in my tent."],"id":771,"m":"What, you sound sleepy? Poor boy, I don't blame you. You've stayed awake too long. Call Claudius and some of my other men. I'll have them sleep on cushions in my tent."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Varro and Claudius!"],"id":772,"m":"Varro and Claudius!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Varro and Claudius.","id":773},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO","lines":["Calls my lord?"],"id":774,"m":"Did my lord call?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I pray you, sirs, lie in my tent and sleep;","It may be I shall raise you by-and-by","On business to my brother Cassius."],"id":775,"m":"Please, sirs, lie down in my tent and sleep. I may wake you soon to send you on business to my brother Cassius."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO","lines":["So please you, we will stand and watch your pleasure."],"id":776,"m":"If you like, we'll stand and stay awake ready for your orders."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I will not have it so; lie down, good sirs,","It may be I shall otherwise bethink me.","Look, Lucius, here's the book I sought for so;","I put it in the pocket of my gown."],"id":777,"m":"I won't have that. Lie down, good sirs. I may change my mind. Look, Lucius, here's the book I was searching for. I put it in the pocket of my gown."},{"t":"sd","text":"Servants lie down","id":778},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I was sure your lordship did not give it me."],"id":779,"m":"I was sure your lordship didn't give it to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Bear with me, good boy, I am much forgetful.","Canst thou hold up thy heavy eyes awhile,","And touch thy instrument a strain or two?"],"id":780,"m":"Be patient with me, good boy, I'm very forgetful. Can you keep your heavy eyes open a little longer and play a tune or two on your instrument?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Ay, my lord, an't please you."],"id":781,"m":"Yes, my lord, if you'd like."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["It does, my boy.","I trouble thee too much, but thou art willing."],"id":782,"m":"I would, my boy. I trouble you too much, but you are willing."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["It is my duty, sir."],"id":783,"m":"It's my duty, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["I should not urge thy duty past thy might;","I know young bloods look for a time of rest."],"id":784,"m":"I shouldn't push your duty beyond your strength. I know young people need their time to rest."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I have slept, my lord, already."],"id":785,"m":"I have slept already, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["It was well done, and thou shalt sleep again;","I will not hold thee long. If I do live,","I will be good to thee."],"id":786,"m":"That was well done, and you shall sleep again. I won't keep you long. If I live, I'll be good to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lucius plays and sings till he falls asleep","id":787},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["This is a sleepy tune. O murd'rous slumber,","Layest thou thy leaden mace upon my boy,","That plays thee music? Gentle knave, good night;","I will not do thee so much wrong to wake thee.","If thou dost nod, thou break'st thy instrument;","I'll take it from thee; and, good boy, good night.","Let me see, let me see; is not the leaf turn'd down","Where I left reading? Here it is, I think."],"id":788,"m":"This is a sleepy tune. Oh, murderous sleep, are you laying your heavy club on my boy, who plays music for you? Gentle boy, good night. I won't do you the wrong of waking you. If you nod, you'll break your instrument. I'll take it from you, and, good boy, good night. Let me see, let me see. Isn't the page turned down where I stopped reading? Here it is, I think."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Ghost of Caesar.","id":789},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["How ill this taper burns! Ha! who comes here?","I think it is the weakness of mine eyes","That shapes this monstrous apparition.","It comes upon me. Art thou anything?","Art thou some god, some angel, or some devil,","That mak'st my blood cold and my hair to stare?","Speak to me what thou art."],"id":790,"m":"How badly this candle burns! Ha! Who's coming here? I think it's the weakness of my eyes that creates this monstrous ghost. It's coming towards me. Are you anything real? Are you some god, some angel or some devil, that makes my blood run cold and my hair stand on end? Tell me what you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"GHOST","lines":["Thy evil spirit, Brutus."],"id":791,"m":"Your evil spirit, Brutus."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why com'st thou?"],"id":792,"m":"Why have you come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GHOST","lines":["To tell thee thou shalt see me at Philippi."],"id":793,"m":"To tell you that you will see me at Philippi."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Well; then I shall see thee again?"],"id":794,"m":"Well, then I'll see you again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GHOST","lines":["Ay, at Philippi."],"id":795,"m":"Yes, at Philippi."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why, I will see thee at Philippi then."],"id":796,"m":"Why, then I'll see you at Philippi."},{"t":"sd","text":"Ghost vanishes","id":797},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Now I have taken heart, thou vanishest.","Ill spirit, I would hold more talk with thee.","Boy! Lucius! Varro! Claudius! Sirs, awake! Claudius!"],"id":798,"m":"Now that I have got my courage back, you vanish. Evil spirit, I would like to talk with you more. Boy! Lucius! Varro! Claudius! Sirs, wake up! Claudius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["The strings, my lord, are false."],"id":799,"m":"The strings are out of tune, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["He thinks he still is at his instrument.","Lucius, awake!"],"id":800,"m":"He thinks he's still playing his instrument. Lucius, wake up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["My lord?"],"id":801,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Didst thou dream, Lucius, that thou so criedst out?"],"id":802,"m":"Were you dreaming, Lucius, that you cried out like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["My lord, I do not know that I did cry."],"id":803,"m":"My lord, I don't know that I did cry out."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Yes, that thou didst. Didst thou see anything?"],"id":804,"m":"Yes, you did. Did you see anything?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Nothing, my lord."],"id":805,"m":"Nothing, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Sleep again, Lucius. Sirrah Claudius!","Fellow thou, awake!"],"id":806,"m":"Go back to sleep, Lucius. Claudius, you there! You, fellow, wake up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO","lines":["My lord?"],"id":807,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIUS","lines":["My lord?"],"id":808,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why did you so cry out, sirs, in your sleep?"],"id":809,"m":"Why did you cry out like that in your sleep, sirs?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO AND CLAUDIUS","lines":["Did we, my lord?"],"id":810,"m":"Did we, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Ay. Saw you anything?"],"id":811,"m":"Yes. Did you see anything?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO","lines":["No, my lord, I saw nothing."],"id":812,"m":"No, my lord, I saw nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLAUDIUS","lines":["Nor I, my lord."],"id":813,"m":"Nor did I, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Go and commend me to my brother Cassius;","Bid him set on his powers betimes before,","And we will follow."],"id":814,"m":"Go and give my greetings to my brother Cassius. Tell him to set his forces moving early, ahead of us, and we'll follow."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO AND CLAUDIUS","lines":["It shall be done, my lord."],"id":815,"m":"It will be done, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":816},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"The plains of Philippi.","id":817},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Octavius, Antony and their Army.","id":818},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Now, Antony, our hopes are answered.","You said the enemy would not come down,","But keep the hills and upper regions.","It proves not so; their battles are at hand,","They mean to warn us at Philippi here,","Answering before we do demand of them."],"id":819,"m":"Now, Antony, our hopes have come true. You said the enemy wouldn't come down but would stay on the hills and the high ground. It's turned out otherwise. Their armies are close by. They mean to challenge us here at Philippi, responding before we have even challenged them."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Tut, I am in their bosoms, and I know","Wherefore they do it. They could be content","To visit other places, and come down","With fearful bravery, thinking by this face","To fasten in our thoughts that they have courage;","But 'tis not so."],"id":820,"m":"Bah, I know what they're thinking, and I know why they're doing it. They would be happy to be somewhere else, and they come down with a show of courage that hides their fear, thinking that by putting on this face they will convince us they are brave. But it isn't so."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":821},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Prepare you, generals.","The enemy comes on in gallant show;","Their bloody sign of battle is hung out,","And something to be done immediately."],"id":822,"m":"Get ready, generals. The enemy is coming on in a fine show. Their red flag of battle is hung out, and something must be done at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Octavius, lead your battle softly on","Upon the left hand of the even field."],"id":823,"m":"Octavius, lead your army slowly forward on the left side of the level field."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Upon the right hand I. Keep thou the left."],"id":824,"m":"I'll take the right. You keep the left."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Why do you cross me in this exigent?"],"id":825,"m":"Why do you contradict me at this critical moment?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["I do not cross you; but I will do so."],"id":826,"m":"I'm not contradicting you, but I will do it my way."},{"t":"sd","text":"March","id":827},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum. Enter Brutus, Cassius and their Army; Lucilius, Titinius, Messala and others.","id":828},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["They stand, and would have parley."],"id":829,"m":"They have stopped and want to talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Stand fast, Titinius; we must out and talk."],"id":830,"m":"Stand firm, Titinius. We must go out and talk."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Mark Antony, shall we give sign of battle?"],"id":831,"m":"Mark Antony, shall we give the signal for battle?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["No, Caesar, we will answer on their charge.","Make forth; the generals would have some words."],"id":832,"m":"No, Caesar, we'll respond when they charge. Go forward. The generals want a few words."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Stir not until the signal."],"id":833,"m":"Don't move until the signal."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Words before blows: is it so, countrymen?"],"id":834,"m":"Words before blows. Is that it, countrymen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Not that we love words better, as you do."],"id":835,"m":"Not that we love words more, as you do."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Good words are better than bad strokes, Octavius."],"id":836,"m":"Good words are better than bad blows, Octavius."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["In your bad strokes, Brutus, you give good words;","Witness the hole you made in Caesar's heart,","Crying, \"Long live! Hail, Caesar!\""],"id":837,"m":"With your bad blows, Brutus, you give good words. Look at the hole you made in Caesar's heart while crying, \"Long live Caesar! Hail, Caesar!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Antony,","The posture of your blows are yet unknown;","But for your words, they rob the Hybla bees,","And leave them honeyless."],"id":838,"m":"Antony, we don't yet know what kind of blows you strike. But as for your words, they rob the bees of Hybla and leave them without honey."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Not stingless too."],"id":839,"m":"And without their sting too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O yes, and soundless too,","For you have stol'n their buzzing, Antony,","And very wisely threat before you sting."],"id":840,"m":"Oh yes, and without sound too, because you have stolen their buzzing, Antony, and very wisely you threaten before you sting."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Villains, you did not so when your vile daggers","Hack'd one another in the sides of Caesar:","You show'd your teeth like apes, and fawn'd like hounds,","And bow'd like bondmen, kissing Caesar's feet;","Whilst damned Casca, like a cur, behind","Struck Caesar on the neck. O you flatterers!"],"id":841,"m":"Villains, you didn't do that when your vile daggers hacked into one another in Caesar's sides. You grinned like apes, fawned like dogs and bowed like slaves, kissing Caesar's feet, while damned Casca, like a mongrel, struck Caesar on the neck from behind. Oh, you flatterers!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Flatterers! Now, Brutus, thank yourself.","This tongue had not offended so today,","If Cassius might have rul'd."],"id":842,"m":"Flatterers! Now, Brutus, you can thank yourself. This tongue wouldn't be insulting us like this today if Cassius had had his way."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Come, come, the cause. If arguing makes us sweat,","The proof of it will turn to redder drops.","Look, I draw a sword against conspirators.","When think you that the sword goes up again?","Never, till Caesar's three and thirty wounds","Be well aveng'd; or till another Caesar","Have added slaughter to the sword of traitors."],"id":843,"m":"Come, come, to the point. If arguing makes us sweat, the test of it will turn into redder drops. Look, I draw my sword against conspirators. When do you think this sword will go back in its sheath? Never, until Caesar's thirty-three wounds are fully avenged, or until another Caesar has been slaughtered by the swords of traitors too."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Caesar, thou canst not die by traitors' hands,","Unless thou bring'st them with thee."],"id":844,"m":"Caesar, you can't die by traitors' hands unless you have brought them with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["So I hope.","I was not born to die on Brutus' sword."],"id":845,"m":"I hope so. I wasn't born to die on Brutus's sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O, if thou wert the noblest of thy strain,","Young man, thou couldst not die more honourable."],"id":846,"m":"Oh, if you were the noblest of your family, young man, you couldn't die more honourably."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["A peevish school-boy, worthless of such honour,","Join'd with a masker and a reveller."],"id":847,"m":"A sulky schoolboy, not worthy of such an honour, teamed up with a party-goer and a drunkard."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Old Cassius still!"],"id":848,"m":"The same old Cassius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Come, Antony; away!","Defiance, traitors, hurl we in your teeth.","If you dare fight today, come to the field;","If not, when you have stomachs."],"id":849,"m":"Come, Antony, away! Traitors, we throw our defiance in your teeth. If you dare to fight today, come to the battlefield. If not, come when you have the stomach for it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Octavius, Antony and their Army","id":850},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Why now, blow wind, swell billow, and swim bark!","The storm is up, and all is on the hazard."],"id":851,"m":"Well now, blow, wind! Swell, wave! Sail, ship! The storm has started, and everything is at stake."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Ho, Lucilius! Hark, a word with you."],"id":852,"m":"Hey, Lucilius! Listen, a word with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["My lord?"],"id":853,"m":"My lord?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Brutus and Lucilius talk apart","id":854},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Messala."],"id":855,"m":"Messala."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["What says my General?"],"id":856,"m":"What does my general say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Messala,","This is my birth-day; as this very day","Was Cassius born. Give me thy hand, Messala:","Be thou my witness that against my will","As Pompey was, am I compell'd to set","Upon one battle all our liberties.","You know that I held Epicurus strong,","And his opinion. Now I change my mind,","And partly credit things that do presage.","Coming from Sardis, on our former ensign","Two mighty eagles fell, and there they perch'd,","Gorging and feeding from our soldiers' hands,","Who to Philippi here consorted us.","This morning are they fled away and gone,","And in their steads do ravens, crows, and kites","Fly o'er our heads, and downward look on us,","As we were sickly prey: their shadows seem","A canopy most fatal, under which","Our army lies, ready to give up the ghost."],"id":857,"m":"Messala, this is my birthday. Cassius was born on this very day. Give me your hand, Messala. Be my witness that, against my will, as Pompey was, I am forced to risk all our freedom on one battle. You know I strongly believed in Epicurus and his teaching that omens mean nothing. Now I have changed my mind, and partly believe in signs that predict the future. On the way from Sardis, two mighty eagles swooped down on our front flag and perched there, gorging and feeding from our soldiers' hands, and they came with us here to Philippi. This morning they have flown away and gone, and in their place ravens, crows and kites fly over our heads and look down on us as if we were sickly prey. Their shadows seem a deadly canopy, under which our army lies, ready to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Believe not so."],"id":858,"m":"Don't believe that."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["I but believe it partly,","For I am fresh of spirit, and resolv'd","To meet all perils very constantly."],"id":859,"m":"I only partly believe it, because my spirit is fresh and I am determined to meet every danger very firmly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Even so, Lucilius."],"id":860,"m":"Just so, Lucilius."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Now, most noble Brutus,","The gods today stand friendly, that we may,","Lovers in peace, lead on our days to age!","But, since the affairs of men rest still incertain,","Let's reason with the worst that may befall.","If we do lose this battle, then is this","The very last time we shall speak together:","What are you then determined to do?"],"id":861,"m":"Now, most noble Brutus, may the gods be friendly today, so that we may live on, friends in peace, to old age! But since men's affairs are always uncertain, let's think about the worst that could happen. If we lose this battle, then this is the very last time we'll speak together. What are you determined to do then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Even by the rule of that philosophy","By which I did blame Cato for the death","Which he did give himself, I know not how,","But I do find it cowardly and vile,","For fear of what might fall, so to prevent","The time of life, arming myself with patience","To stay the providence of some high powers","That govern us below."],"id":862,"m":"I'll follow the rules of the philosophy by which I blamed Cato for killing himself. I don't know why, but I find it cowardly and vile to cut life short for fear of what might happen. Instead I'll arm myself with patience to wait for whatever the high powers that rule us here below have in store."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Then, if we lose this battle,","You are contented to be led in triumph","Thorough the streets of Rome?"],"id":863,"m":"Then if we lose this battle, you are content to be led as a prisoner in a triumphal parade through the streets of Rome?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["No, Cassius, no: think not, thou noble Roman,","That ever Brutus will go bound to Rome;","He bears too great a mind. But this same day","Must end that work the Ides of March begun;","And whether we shall meet again I know not.","Therefore our everlasting farewell take.","For ever, and for ever, farewell, Cassius.","If we do meet again, why, we shall smile;","If not, why then this parting was well made."],"id":864,"m":"No, Cassius, no. Don't think, you noble Roman, that Brutus will ever go to Rome in chains. His mind is too great for that. But this same day must finish the work that the fifteenth of March began, and I don't know whether we shall meet again. So take my everlasting farewell. For ever, and for ever, farewell, Cassius. If we meet again, why, we'll smile. If not, then this parting was well done."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["For ever and for ever farewell, Brutus.","If we do meet again, we'll smile indeed;","If not, 'tis true this parting was well made."],"id":865,"m":"For ever and for ever, farewell, Brutus. If we meet again, we'll smile indeed. If not, it's true this parting was well done."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why then, lead on. O, that a man might know","The end of this day's business ere it come!","But it sufficeth that the day will end,","And then the end is known. Come, ho! away!"],"id":866,"m":"Then lead on. Oh, if only a man could know how this day's business will end before it comes! But it's enough that the day will end, and then the end will be known. Come on, away!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":867},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"The same. The field of battle.","id":868},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter Brutus and Messala.","id":869},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Ride, ride, Messala, ride, and give these bills","Unto the legions on the other side."],"id":870,"m":"Ride, ride, Messala, ride, and give these orders to the legions on the other side."},{"t":"sd","text":"Loud alarum","id":871},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Let them set on at once; for I perceive","But cold demeanor in Octavius' wing,","And sudden push gives them the overthrow.","Ride, ride, Messala; let them all come down."],"id":872,"m":"Let them attack at once, because I see only a lack of fighting spirit in Octavius's wing, and a sudden push will overthrow them. Ride, ride, Messala. Let them all come down."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":873},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":874},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter Cassius and Titinius.","id":875},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["O, look, Titinius, look, the villains fly!","Myself have to mine own turn'd enemy:","This ensign here of mine was turning back;","I slew the coward, and did take it from him."],"id":876,"m":"Oh, look, Titinius, look, the villains are running away! I have turned enemy to my own men. This standard-bearer of mine was turning back. I killed the coward and took the flag from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["O Cassius, Brutus gave the word too early,","Who, having some advantage on Octavius,","Took it too eagerly: his soldiers fell to spoil,","Whilst we by Antony are all enclos'd."],"id":877,"m":"Oh, Cassius, Brutus gave the order too early. He had some advantage over Octavius and took it too eagerly. His soldiers started looting, while we are all surrounded by Antony."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pindarus.","id":878},{"t":"sp","who":"PINDARUS","lines":["Fly further off, my lord, fly further off;","Mark Antony is in your tents, my lord.","Fly, therefore, noble Cassius, fly far off."],"id":879,"m":"Run further off, my lord, run further off. Mark Antony is in your tents, my lord. So run, noble Cassius, run far away."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["This hill is far enough. Look, look, Titinius;","Are those my tents where I perceive the fire?"],"id":880,"m":"This hill is far enough. Look, look, Titinius. Are those my tents where I can see the fire?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["They are, my lord."],"id":881,"m":"They are, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Titinius, if thou lovest me,","Mount thou my horse and hide thy spurs in him,","Till he have brought thee up to yonder troops","And here again, that I may rest assur'd","Whether yond troops are friend or enemy."],"id":882,"m":"Titinius, if you love me, get on my horse and dig your spurs into him until he has carried you to those troops over there and back again, so that I can know for certain whether those troops are friends or enemies."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["I will be here again, even with a thought."],"id":883,"m":"I'll be back here as fast as a thought."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":884},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Go, Pindarus, get higher on that hill,","My sight was ever thick. Regard Titinius,","And tell me what thou notest about the field."],"id":885,"m":"Go, Pindarus, climb higher up that hill. My eyesight was always poor. Watch Titinius, and tell me what you see on the battlefield."},{"t":"sd","text":"Pindarus goes up","id":886},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","cont":1,"lines":["This day I breathed first. Time is come round,","And where I did begin, there shall I end.","My life is run his compass. Sirrah, what news?"],"id":887,"m":"On this day I was born. Time has come full circle, and where I began, there I will end. My life has run its course. You there, what news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PINDARUS","lines":["[Above] O my lord!"],"id":888,"m":"[Above] Oh, my lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["What news?"],"id":889,"m":"What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PINDARUS","lines":["[Above] Titinius is enclosed round about","With horsemen, that make to him on the spur,","Yet he spurs on. Now they are almost on him.","Now, Titinius! Now some light. O, he lights too.","He's ta'en!"],"id":890,"m":"[Above] Titinius is surrounded by horsemen riding at full speed towards him, and still he spurs on. Now they're almost on him. Come on, Titinius! Now some of them get off their horses. Oh, he gets off too. He's taken!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Shout","id":891},{"t":"sp","who":"PINDARUS","cont":1,"lines":["And, hark! they shout for joy."],"id":892,"m":"And listen! They're shouting for joy."},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","lines":["Come down; behold no more.","O, coward that I am, to live so long,","To see my best friend ta'en before my face!"],"id":893,"m":"Come down. Don't watch any more. Oh, what a coward I am to live long enough to see my best friend taken in front of my eyes!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Pindarus descends","id":894},{"t":"sp","who":"CASSIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Come hither, sirrah.","In Parthia did I take thee prisoner;","And then I swore thee, saving of thy life,","That whatsoever I did bid thee do,","Thou shouldst attempt it. Come now, keep thine oath.","Now be a freeman; and with this good sword,","That ran through Caesar's bowels, search this bosom.","Stand not to answer. Here, take thou the hilts;","And when my face is cover'd, as 'tis now,","Guide thou the sword.—Caesar, thou art reveng'd,","Even with the sword that kill'd thee."],"id":895,"m":"Come here, you. In Parthia I took you prisoner, and then, in return for sparing your life, I made you swear that whatever I told you to do, you would attempt it. Come now, keep your oath. Now be a free man, and with this good sword, which ran through Caesar's guts, search out this heart. Don't stop to answer. Here, take the hilt, and when my face is covered, as it is now, guide the sword. Caesar, you are avenged, with the very sword that killed you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies","id":896},{"t":"sp","who":"PINDARUS","lines":["So, I am free, yet would not so have been,","Durst I have done my will. O Cassius!","Far from this country Pindarus shall run,","Where never Roman shall take note of him."],"id":897,"m":"So I'm free, and yet I wouldn't have been free like this if I had dared to do what I wanted. Oh, Cassius! Pindarus will run far from this country, where no Roman will ever notice him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit","id":898},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Titinius with Messala.","id":899},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["It is but change, Titinius; for Octavius","Is overthrown by noble Brutus' power,","As Cassius' legions are by Antony."],"id":900,"m":"It's only an exchange, Titinius, because Octavius has been beaten by noble Brutus's forces, just as Cassius's legions have been by Antony."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["These tidings would well comfort Cassius."],"id":901,"m":"This news will be a great comfort to Cassius."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Where did you leave him?"],"id":902,"m":"Where did you leave him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["All disconsolate,","With Pindarus his bondman, on this hill."],"id":903,"m":"Completely in despair, with Pindarus, his slave, on this hill."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Is not that he that lies upon the ground?"],"id":904,"m":"Isn't that him lying on the ground?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["He lies not like the living. O my heart!"],"id":905,"m":"He isn't lying like a living man. Oh, my heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Is not that he?"],"id":906,"m":"Isn't that him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["No, this was he, Messala,","But Cassius is no more. O setting sun,","As in thy red rays thou dost sink to night,","So in his red blood Cassius' day is set.","The sun of Rome is set. Our day is gone;","Clouds, dews, and dangers come; our deeds are done.","Mistrust of my success hath done this deed."],"id":907,"m":"No, this was him, Messala, but Cassius is no more. Oh, setting sun, just as you sink into night in your red rays, so Cassius's day has set in his red blood. The sun of Rome has set. Our day is over. Clouds, dews and dangers are coming. Our deeds are done. Doubt about how my mission would turn out has done this."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Mistrust of good success hath done this deed.","O hateful Error, Melancholy's child!","Why dost thou show to the apt thoughts of men","The things that are not? O Error, soon conceiv'd,","Thou never com'st unto a happy birth,","But kill'st the mother that engender'd thee!"],"id":908,"m":"Doubt about our success has done this. Oh, hateful error, child of melancholy! Why do you show men's ready imaginations things that aren't true? Oh, error, so quickly conceived, you never come to a happy birth, but kill the mother who gave birth to you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["What, Pindarus! where art thou, Pindarus?"],"id":909,"m":"Pindarus! Where are you, Pindarus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Seek him, Titinius, whilst I go to meet","The noble Brutus, thrusting this report","Into his ears. I may say thrusting it;","For piercing steel and darts envenomed","Shall be as welcome to the ears of Brutus","As tidings of this sight."],"id":910,"m":"Look for him, Titinius, while I go to meet noble Brutus and thrust this news into his ears. I may well say thrust it, because piercing steel and poisoned darts would be as welcome to Brutus's ears as news of this sight."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","lines":["Hie you, Messala,","And I will seek for Pindarus the while."],"id":911,"m":"Hurry, Messala, and I'll look for Pindarus meanwhile."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Messala","id":912},{"t":"sp","who":"TITINIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Why didst thou send me forth, brave Cassius?","Did I not meet thy friends? And did not they","Put on my brows this wreath of victory,","And bid me give it thee? Didst thou not hear their shouts?","Alas, thou hast misconstrued everything!","But, hold thee, take this garland on thy brow;","Thy Brutus bid me give it thee, and I","Will do his bidding. Brutus, come apace,","And see how I regarded Caius Cassius.","By your leave, gods. This is a Roman's part.","Come, Cassius' sword, and find Titinius' heart."],"id":913,"m":"Why did you send me out, brave Cassius? Didn't I meet your friends? And didn't they put this victory wreath on my head and tell me to give it to you? Didn't you hear their shouts? Oh, you misunderstood everything! But wait, take this garland on your head. Your Brutus told me to give it to you, and I will do what he told me. Brutus, come quickly and see how I honoured Caius Cassius. With your permission, gods: this is a Roman's role. Come, Cassius's sword, and find Titinius's heart."},{"t":"sd","text":"Dies","id":914},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter Brutus, Messala, young Cato, Strato, Volumnius and Lucilius.","id":915},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Where, where, Messala, doth his body lie?"],"id":916,"m":"Where, where, Messala, is his body lying?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Lo, yonder, and Titinius mourning it."],"id":917,"m":"Look, over there, and Titinius mourning over it."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Titinius' face is upward."],"id":918,"m":"Titinius's face is turned upwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"CATO","lines":["He is slain."],"id":919,"m":"He has been killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["O Julius Caesar, thou art mighty yet!","Thy spirit walks abroad, and turns our swords","In our own proper entrails."],"id":920,"m":"Oh, Julius Caesar, you are still mighty! Your spirit walks abroad and turns our swords into our own guts."},{"t":"sd","text":"Low alarums","id":921},{"t":"sp","who":"CATO","lines":["Brave Titinius!","Look whether he have not crown'd dead Cassius!"],"id":922,"m":"Brave Titinius! Look how he has crowned dead Cassius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Are yet two Romans living such as these?","The last of all the Romans, fare thee well!","It is impossible that ever Rome","Should breed thy fellow. Friends, I owe more tears","To this dead man than you shall see me pay.","I shall find time, Cassius, I shall find time.","Come therefore, and to Thassos send his body.","His funerals shall not be in our camp,","Lest it discomfort us. Lucilius, come;","And come, young Cato; let us to the field.","Labeo and Flavius, set our battles on.","'Tis three o'clock; and Romans, yet ere night","We shall try fortune in a second fight."],"id":923,"m":"Are there still two Romans alive like these? The last of all the Romans, farewell! It is impossible that Rome should ever produce your equal. Friends, I owe more tears to this dead man than you will see me pay. I'll find time, Cassius, I'll find time. So come, and send his body to Thassos. His funeral must not be held in our camp, in case it disheartens us. Lucilius, come, and come, young Cato. Let's go to the battlefield. Labeo and Flavius, move our armies forward. It's three o'clock, and, Romans, before night we will try our luck in a second fight."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":924},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":925},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Enter fighting soldiers of both armies; then Brutus, Messala, young Cato, Lucilius, Flavius and others.","id":926},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Yet, countrymen, O, yet hold up your heads!"],"id":927,"m":"Still, countrymen, oh, still hold up your heads!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CATO","lines":["What bastard doth not? Who will go with me?","I will proclaim my name about the field.","I am the son of Marcus Cato, ho!","A foe to tyrants, and my country's friend.","I am the son of Marcus Cato, ho!"],"id":928,"m":"Who is so low-born that he wouldn't? Who will come with me? I'll announce my name all over the battlefield. I am the son of Marcus Cato! An enemy of tyrants, and my country's friend. I am the son of Marcus Cato!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Charges the enemy","id":929},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["And I am Brutus, Marcus Brutus, I;","Brutus, my country's friend; know me for Brutus!"],"id":930,"m":"And I am Brutus, Marcus Brutus, I am. Brutus, my country's friend. Know me as Brutus!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit, charging the enemy. Cato is overpowered, and falls","id":931},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["O young and noble Cato, art thou down?","Why, now thou diest as bravely as Titinius,","And mayst be honour'd, being Cato's son."],"id":932,"m":"Oh, young and noble Cato, are you down? Why, now you die as bravely as Titinius, and you may be honoured as Cato's son."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["Yield, or thou diest."],"id":933,"m":"Surrender, or you die."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["Only I yield to die:","There is so much that thou wilt kill me straight;"],"id":934,"m":"I surrender only to die. Here is plenty to persuade you to kill me at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Offering money","id":935},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Kill Brutus, and be honour'd in his death."],"id":936,"m":"Kill Brutus, and be honoured for his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["We must not. A noble prisoner!"],"id":937,"m":"We mustn't. A noble prisoner!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SOLDIER","lines":["Room, ho! Tell Antony, Brutus is ta'en."],"id":938,"m":"Make way! Tell Antony that Brutus has been captured."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","lines":["I'll tell the news. Here comes the General."],"id":939,"m":"I'll tell him the news. Here comes the general."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Antony.","id":940},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SOLDIER","cont":1,"lines":["Brutus is ta'en, Brutus is ta'en, my lord."],"id":941,"m":"Brutus is captured, Brutus is captured, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["Where is he?"],"id":942,"m":"Where is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["Safe, Antony; Brutus is safe enough.","I dare assure thee that no enemy","Shall ever take alive the noble Brutus.","The gods defend him from so great a shame!","When you do find him, or alive or dead,","He will be found like Brutus, like himself."],"id":943,"m":"Safe, Antony. Brutus is safe enough. I promise you that no enemy will ever take noble Brutus alive. May the gods protect him from such a great shame! When you find him, alive or dead, he will be found behaving like Brutus, like himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["This is not Brutus, friend; but, I assure you,","A prize no less in worth. Keep this man safe,","Give him all kindness. I had rather have","Such men my friends than enemies. Go on,","And see whether Brutus be alive or dead;","And bring us word unto Octavius' tent","How everything is chanc'd."],"id":944,"m":"This isn't Brutus, friend, but, I assure you, a prize just as valuable. Keep this man safe. Treat him with every kindness. I'd rather have such men as friends than enemies. Go on and see whether Brutus is alive or dead, and bring us word in Octavius's tent of how everything has turned out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":945},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"V","loc":"Another part of the field.","id":946},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Brutus, Dardanius, Clitus, Strato and Volumnius.","id":947},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come, poor remains of friends, rest on this rock."],"id":948,"m":"Come, poor remnants of my friends, rest on this rock."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["Statilius show'd the torch-light; but, my lord,","He came not back: he is or ta'en or slain."],"id":949,"m":"Statilius showed his torch as a signal, but, my lord, he didn't come back. He's either captured or killed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Sit thee down, Clitus. Slaying is the word;","It is a deed in fashion. Hark thee, Clitus."],"id":950,"m":"Sit down, Clitus. Killed is the word. It's the fashionable thing to do. Listen, Clitus."},{"t":"sd","text":"Whispering","id":951},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["What, I, my lord? No, not for all the world."],"id":952,"m":"What, me, my lord? No, not for all the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Peace then, no words."],"id":953,"m":"Quiet then, not a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["I'll rather kill myself."],"id":954,"m":"I'd rather kill myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Hark thee, Dardanius."],"id":955,"m":"Listen, Dardanius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Whispers him","id":956},{"t":"sp","who":"DARDANIUS","lines":["Shall I do such a deed?"],"id":957,"m":"Shall I do such a deed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["O Dardanius!"],"id":958,"m":"Oh, Dardanius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"DARDANIUS","lines":["O Clitus!"],"id":959,"m":"Oh, Clitus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["What ill request did Brutus make to thee?"],"id":960,"m":"What evil request did Brutus make of you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"DARDANIUS","lines":["To kill him, Clitus. Look, he meditates."],"id":961,"m":"To kill him, Clitus. Look, he's lost in thought."},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["Now is that noble vessel full of grief,","That it runs over even at his eyes."],"id":962,"m":"Now that noble vessel is so full of grief that it overflows even at his eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Come hither, good Volumnius; list a word."],"id":963,"m":"Come here, good Volumnius. Listen to a word."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIUS","lines":["What says my lord?"],"id":964,"m":"What does my lord say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Why, this, Volumnius:","The ghost of Caesar hath appear'd to me","Two several times by night; at Sardis once,","And this last night here in Philippi fields.","I know my hour is come."],"id":965,"m":"Why, this, Volumnius. The ghost of Caesar has appeared to me twice at night: once at Sardis, and last night here on the fields of Philippi. I know my time has come."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIUS","lines":["Not so, my lord."],"id":966,"m":"No, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Nay I am sure it is, Volumnius.","Thou seest the world, Volumnius, how it goes;","Our enemies have beat us to the pit."],"id":967,"m":"No, I'm sure it has, Volumnius. You see how the world is going, Volumnius. Our enemies have driven us to the edge of the pit."},{"t":"sd","text":"Low alarums","id":968},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["It is more worthy to leap in ourselves","Than tarry till they push us. Good Volumnius,","Thou know'st that we two went to school together;","Even for that our love of old, I pr'ythee","Hold thou my sword-hilts, whilst I run on it."],"id":969,"m":"It is more honourable to leap in ourselves than to wait until they push us. Good Volumnius, you know we went to school together. For the sake of that old friendship, I beg you, hold my sword hilt while I run on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VOLUMNIUS","lines":["That's not an office for a friend, my lord."],"id":970,"m":"That's no job for a friend, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums still","id":971},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["Fly, fly, my lord! there is no tarrying here."],"id":972,"m":"Run, run, my lord! You can't stay here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Farewell to you; and you; and you, Volumnius.","Strato, thou hast been all this while asleep;","Farewell to thee too, Strato.—Countrymen,","My heart doth joy, that yet in all my life","I found no man but he was true to me.","I shall have glory by this losing day","More than Octavius and Mark Antony","By this vile conquest shall attain unto.","So fare you well at once; for Brutus' tongue","Hath almost ended his life's history.","Night hangs upon mine eyes; my bones would rest,","That have but labour'd to attain this hour."],"id":973,"m":"Goodbye to you, and you, and you, Volumnius. Strato, you've been asleep all this time. Goodbye to you too, Strato. Countrymen, my heart is glad that in all my life I never found a man who wasn't true to me. I will have more glory from this day of defeat than Octavius and Mark Antony will gain by their vile victory. So goodbye to you all at once, because Brutus's tongue has almost finished telling the story of his life. Night hangs on my eyes. My bones want to rest, after working only to reach this hour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarums. Cry within, \"Fly, fly, fly!\"","id":974},{"t":"sp","who":"CLITUS","lines":["Fly, my lord, fly!"],"id":975,"m":"Run, my lord, run!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Hence! I will follow."],"id":976,"m":"Go! I'll follow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Clitus, Dardanius and Volumnius","id":977},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","cont":1,"lines":["I pr'ythee, Strato, stay thou by thy lord.","Thou art a fellow of a good respect;","Thy life hath had some smatch of honour in it.","Hold then my sword, and turn away thy face,","While I do run upon it. Wilt thou, Strato?"],"id":978,"m":"Please, Strato, stay with your lord. You are a man with a good reputation. Your life has had some taste of honour in it. So hold my sword and turn your face away while I run on it. Will you, Strato?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STRATO","lines":["Give me your hand first. Fare you well, my lord."],"id":979,"m":"Give me your hand first. Goodbye, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BRUTUS","lines":["Farewell, good Strato.—Caesar, now be still:","I kill'd not thee with half so good a will."],"id":980,"m":"Goodbye, good Strato. Caesar, now rest in peace. I didn't kill you half as willingly as I kill myself."},{"t":"sd","text":"He runs on his sword, and dies","id":981},{"t":"sd","text":"Alarum. Retreat. Enter Antony, Octavius, Messala, Lucilius and the Army.","id":982},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["What man is that?"],"id":983,"m":"Who is that man?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["My master's man. Strato, where is thy master?"],"id":984,"m":"My master's servant. Strato, where is your master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STRATO","lines":["Free from the bondage you are in, Messala.","The conquerors can but make a fire of him;","For Brutus only overcame himself,","And no man else hath honour by his death."],"id":985,"m":"Free from the slavery you are in, Messala. The winners can only burn his body, because only Brutus defeated Brutus, and no one else gets honour from his death."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["So Brutus should be found. I thank thee, Brutus,","That thou hast prov'd Lucilius' saying true."],"id":986,"m":"That is how Brutus should be found. Thank you, Brutus, for proving what Lucilius said was true."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["All that serv'd Brutus, I will entertain them.","Fellow, wilt thou bestow thy time with me?"],"id":987,"m":"I'll take into my service all the men who served Brutus. Fellow, will you spend your time with me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STRATO","lines":["Ay, if Messala will prefer me to you."],"id":988,"m":"Yes, if Messala will recommend me to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["Do so, good Messala."],"id":989,"m":"Do so, good Messala."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["How died my master, Strato?"],"id":990,"m":"How did my master die, Strato?"},{"t":"sp","who":"STRATO","lines":["I held the sword, and he did run on it."],"id":991,"m":"I held the sword, and he ran on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSALA","lines":["Octavius, then take him to follow thee,","That did the latest service to my master."],"id":992,"m":"Octavius, then take him into your service, the man who did the last service for my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"ANTONY","lines":["This was the noblest Roman of them all.","All the conspirators save only he,","Did that they did in envy of great Caesar;","He only, in a general honest thought","And common good to all, made one of them.","His life was gentle, and the elements","So mix'd in him that Nature might stand up","And say to all the world, \"This was a man!\""],"id":993,"m":"This was the noblest Roman of them all. All the conspirators except him did what they did out of envy of great Caesar. He alone joined them with honest thoughts for the general good of all. His life was gentle, and the elements were so well mixed in him that Nature could stand up and say to all the world, \"This was a man!\""},{"t":"sp","who":"OCTAVIUS","lines":["According to his virtue let us use him","With all respect and rites of burial.","Within my tent his bones tonight shall lie,","Most like a soldier, order'd honourably.","So call the field to rest, and let's away,","To part the glories of this happy day."],"id":994,"m":"Let's treat him as his virtue deserves, with all respect and funeral rites. His body will lie in my tent tonight, laid out with honour like a soldier. So call off the army to rest, and let's go and share out the glories of this happy day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt","id":995}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Julius Caesar","group":"Caesar's party","desc":"The most powerful man in Rome, just returned in triumph after defeating the sons of his old rival Pompey. Publicly he is godlike and fearless; privately he is deaf in one ear, subject to fits and swayed by flattery. He refuses the crown three times, ignores every warning and is stabbed in the Senate in Act 3. His ghost, and his name, dominate the rest of the play."},{"name":"Calphurnia","group":"Caesar's party","desc":"Caesar's wife. She has no children, and Caesar asks Antony to touch her in the Lupercal race to cure her barrenness. Terrified by omens and a dream of Caesar's statue running with blood, she persuades him to stay at home, until Decius talks him out of it."},{"name":"Mark Antony","group":"Caesar's party","desc":"Caesar's loyal friend, a lover of sport, plays and parties whom the conspirators underestimate. After the murder he shakes their bloody hands, then turns the crowd against them with his funeral speech. With Octavius and Lepidus he rules Rome, condemns enemies to death and defeats Brutus and Cassius at Philippi. He speaks the final tribute to Brutus."},{"name":"Octavius Caesar","group":"Caesar's party","desc":"Caesar's young great-nephew and adopted heir. He arrives in Rome after the murder and joins Antony and Lepidus as a ruler. Cool and quietly assertive, he insists on his own way at Philippi and has the last word in the play."},{"name":"Lepidus","group":"Caesar's party","desc":"The third member of the triumvirate. Antony privately dismisses him as a mere tool, fit only to carry burdens like a donkey."},{"name":"Marcus Brutus","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A respected senator, admired for his honesty and descended from the Brutus who drove the last king out of Rome. He loves Caesar but fears what he may become. Persuaded by Cassius and forged letters, he leads the conspiracy and strikes the last blow. His idealism leads to a series of mistakes: sparing Antony, letting him speak, and marching to Philippi. He kills himself after defeat, and is called 'the noblest Roman of them all'."},{"name":"Caius Cassius","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A sharp, envious, practical politician who resents Caesar's rise. He wins Brutus to the plot with flattery and planted letters, but repeatedly gives way to him against his own better judgement. Hot-tempered and loving, he quarrels with Brutus at Sardis and makes it up. At Philippi he kills himself on his birthday after misreading the battle."},{"name":"Casca","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A sour, blunt-speaking senator who mocks the crown-offering, is terrified by the storm and strikes the first blow at Caesar."},{"name":"Decius Brutus","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A conspirator who knows how to flatter Caesar. He reinterprets Calphurnia's dream and brings Caesar to the Senate."},{"name":"Cinna","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A conspirator who plants Cassius's forged letters for Brutus to find."},{"name":"Metellus Cimber","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A conspirator whose petition for his banished brother gives the others an excuse to crowd round Caesar."},{"name":"Trebonius","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A conspirator who argues Antony should be spared and draws him out of the Senate before the murder."},{"name":"Caius Ligarius","group":"The conspirators","desc":"A sick man who throws off his illness to follow Brutus, a sign of how Brutus's name draws men to the cause."},{"name":"Portia","group":"Brutus's household","desc":"Brutus's wife, daughter of the famous Cato. She demands to share his secrets, proving her strength with a self-inflicted wound. Left at home on the Ides, she can hardly bear the suspense. Later she kills herself by swallowing fire, and Brutus hears the news at Sardis."},{"name":"Lucius","group":"Brutus's household","desc":"Brutus's young servant, who sleeps soundly while his master cannot, and plays music to him in the tent before the Ghost appears."},{"name":"Cicero","group":"Senators and others","desc":"A famous orator. He speaks Greek at the games, warns that men interpret signs to suit themselves, and is later put to death by the triumvirs."},{"name":"Publius","group":"Senators and others","desc":"An old senator who is stunned by the murder."},{"name":"Popilius Lena","group":"Senators and others","desc":"A senator whose good wishes to Cassius make the conspirators fear they have been discovered."},{"name":"Flavius and Marullus","group":"Senators and others","desc":"Tribunes of the people who scold the crowd for celebrating Caesar and strip his statues. They are 'put to silence' for it."},{"name":"Artemidorus","group":"Senators and others","desc":"A teacher of rhetoric who writes Caesar a warning naming the conspirators. Caesar refuses to read it."},{"name":"The Soothsayer","group":"Senators and others","desc":"A fortune-teller who warns Caesar to beware the Ides of March, and reminds him on the day that it has not yet gone."},{"name":"Cinna the poet","group":"Senators and others","desc":"An innocent poet torn apart by the mob because he shares a name with a conspirator."},{"name":"The citizens","group":"Senators and others","desc":"The people of Rome: a cobbler and carpenter in the first scene, then the crowd at the Forum who cheer Brutus, then riot for Antony."},{"name":"Titinius","group":"The armies at Philippi","desc":"Cassius's loyal friend. When Cassius dies after mistaking his capture, he crowns the body with a victory wreath and kills himself."},{"name":"Messala","group":"The armies at Philippi","desc":"An officer who brings news of Portia's death and the proscriptions, and finds Cassius dead."},{"name":"Lucilius","group":"The armies at Philippi","desc":"An officer of Brutus who pretends to be Brutus to protect him, and is spared by Antony."},{"name":"Pindarus","group":"The armies at Philippi","desc":"Cassius's slave, once a prisoner from Parthia. He wrongly reports Titinius's capture and is ordered to kill Cassius."},{"name":"Young Cato","group":"The armies at Philippi","desc":"Portia's brother, who dies fighting bravely at Philippi."},{"name":"Strato, Clitus, Dardanius and Volumnius","group":"The armies at Philippi","desc":"Brutus's last companions. Each refuses to help him die except Strato, who holds the sword."},{"name":"The Ghost of Caesar","group":"Senators and others","desc":"Appears to Brutus in his tent at Sardis as 'thy evil spirit' and promises to meet him at Philippi."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"On a public holiday, the tribunes Flavius and Marullus find working men in their best clothes, out to cheer Caesar's triumph over the sons of Pompey. A cheeky cobbler answers them with puns. Marullus reminds the crowd how they once cheered Pompey and sends them home ashamed. The tribunes go off to strip Caesar's statues of decorations, hoping to keep him from rising too high.","key":["The play begins with a crowd that changes sides, which foreshadows the Forum scene.","Caesar is threatening even before he appears: his enemies talk of clipping his wings.","Comic prose from the workmen contrasts with the tribunes' angry verse."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why are the workmen out in the street?","o":["To protest against Caesar","To celebrate Caesar's triumph","To look for work","To see a play"],"a":1},{"q":"Whose defeat is Caesar celebrating?","o":["Pompey's sons","Antony's","The Gauls","Cassius's"],"a":0},{"q":"What do the tribunes plan to do?","o":["Kill Caesar","Strip decorations from Caesar's statues","Join the celebrations","Arrest the cobbler"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"During the feast of Lupercal, a soothsayer warns Caesar to beware the Ides of March; Caesar dismisses him. Left alone with Brutus, Cassius works on him, flattering his reputation and mocking Caesar's physical weakness, while shouts offstage suggest Caesar is being made king. Caesar returns, tells Antony he distrusts Cassius's 'lean and hungry look'. Casca describes how Antony offered Caesar a crown three times and how Caesar fainted. Alone, Cassius plans to throw forged letters through Brutus's window.","key":["Cassius's persuasion of Brutus is a model of manipulation: flattery, jealousy and appeals to ancestry.","We learn of the crown only through Casca's sarcastic report, so we never know Caesar's true intention.","Caesar's judgement of Cassius is accurate, but his deafness hints at the weakness behind his godlike pose."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Soothsayer warn Caesar about?","o":["The Ides of March","Brutus","A storm","Antony"],"a":0},{"q":"How many times was Caesar offered the crown?","o":["Once","Twice","Three times","Four times"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Cassius plan at the end of the scene?","o":["To leave Rome","To throw forged letters into Brutus's house","To warn Caesar","To kill Casca"],"a":1}]},"a1s3":{"sum":"That night a violent storm breaks over Rome. Casca, sword drawn, tells Cicero of terrifying wonders: a burning hand, a lion near the Capitol, men on fire. Cassius welcomes the storm, interprets it as heaven's warning against Caesar, and recruits Casca. Cinna arrives and is sent to plant the forged letters for Brutus. They agree to visit Brutus before dawn.","key":["Omens can be read in different ways, as Cicero warns; Cassius reads them to suit his plot.","Cassius speaks of suicide as a way out of tyranny, which foreshadows his own death.","The conspirators need Brutus for his reputation: he will make their act look like virtue."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Cassius react to the storm?","o":["He hides","He bares his chest to the lightning","He prays","He goes home"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Casca say the senators mean to do tomorrow?","o":["Exile Caesar","Make Caesar king","Arrest Cassius","Close the Senate"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does Cinna put one of the letters?","o":["In the praetor's chair","On Caesar's door","In the Forum","In Antony's house"],"a":0}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Unable to sleep, Brutus decides in a soliloquy that Caesar must die, not for what he is but for what he might become. He reads a forged letter urging him to act. The conspirators arrive; Brutus refuses an oath, rejects Cicero and overrules Cassius's plan to kill Antony, insisting they be 'sacrificers, but not butchers'. Decius promises to bring Caesar to the Senate. Portia begs Brutus to share his secret, showing the wound she gave herself, and he promises to tell her. Ligarius, though sick, joins him.","key":["Brutus's reasoning is built on 'may': he kills Caesar for possible future crimes.","Brutus makes the key decisions now, and every one of them helps ruin the conspiracy.","Portia's scene shows the private cost of the plot and the strain on Brutus's marriage."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Brutus decide Caesar must die?","o":["Caesar insulted him","Caesar might become a tyrant if crowned","Cassius paid him","Caesar killed his father"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Brutus refuse to let the conspirators do?","o":["Swear an oath","Carry daggers","Meet at night","Wear cloaks"],"a":0},{"q":"How has Portia proved her strength?","o":["By fasting","By wounding herself in the thigh","By fighting a soldier","By walking in the storm"],"a":1}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"In the same stormy night, Calphurnia has dreamed three times of Caesar's murder. She begs him to stay at home; the priests report a sacrificed beast with no heart. Caesar at first agrees to stay to please her. Decius arrives, reinterprets Calphurnia's dream as a sign of Caesar's greatness, tells him the Senate will crown him today and hints he will be laughed at. Caesar goes. The conspirators and Antony arrive to escort him.","key":["Caesar swings between heroic defiance and giving way, first to his wife, then to flattery.","Decius's reading of the dream is false, yet in another sense true: Rome will draw life from Caesar's blood.","Brutus's aside shows his conscience: they are not truly friends."],"quiz":[{"q":"What did Calphurnia dream?","o":["Caesar was crowned","Caesar's statue ran with blood","Rome burned","Brutus wept"],"a":1},{"q":"What do the priests find?","o":["A beast with no heart","Two hearts","A snake","Nothing unusual"],"a":0},{"q":"What finally persuades Caesar to go?","o":["Antony's advice","Decius's flattery and news of a crown","Brutus's order","The Soothsayer"],"a":1}]},"a2s3":{"sum":"Artemidorus reads aloud a letter naming all the conspirators and warning Caesar. He plans to hand it to Caesar as he passes.","key":["The plot is known: Caesar has one more chance to escape.","Artemidorus blames envy ('emulation'), not liberty.","A short scene that builds suspense before the murder."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has Artemidorus written?","o":["A poem for Caesar","A warning naming the conspirators","A petition for money","A letter to Brutus"],"a":1},{"q":"How does he plan to deliver it?","o":["Post it on a statue","Hand it to Caesar as a petition","Send it with Lucius","Read it in the Senate"],"a":1},{"q":"What does he say virtue cannot escape?","o":["Death","The teeth of envy","The gods","The crowd"],"a":1}]},"a2s4":{"sum":"Portia, nearly frantic with suspense, sends Lucius to the Senate without being able to tell him why. She meets the Soothsayer, who is going to warn Caesar again. Almost betraying the secret, she feels faint and sends Lucius to Brutus with a cheerful message.","key":["Portia's agitation shows the toll of keeping the secret.","Her words 'a man's mind, but a woman's might' raise the play's ideas about gender and strength.","The Soothsayer's reappearance raises tension as Caesar heads to the Capitol."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can't Portia tell Lucius his errand?","o":["She has forgotten","She cannot reveal the secret","She is ill","Brutus forbade it"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Portia meet?","o":["Artemidorus","The Soothsayer","Cassius","Antony"],"a":1},{"q":"What message does she send Brutus?","o":["Come home","That she is merry","That Caesar knows","To stop the plot"],"a":1}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Caesar ignores the Soothsayer and refuses to read Artemidorus's letter first. Trebonius leads Antony away. Metellus Cimber kneels for his banished brother; Caesar refuses, boasting he is 'constant as the northern star'. The conspirators stab him, Brutus last ('Et tu, Brute?'). They bathe their hands in his blood and plan to proclaim liberty. Antony sends a servant, then arrives, shakes their hands and asks to speak at the funeral. Brutus agrees against Cassius's advice. Alone, Antony begs Caesar's forgiveness and prophesies civil war. He learns Octavius is near Rome.","key":["Caesar's most arrogant speech comes moments before his death.","The 'sacrificers' bathe in blood, fulfilling Calphurnia's dream and undermining Brutus's ideal.","Brutus's decision to let Antony speak is the turning point of the play."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who strikes the first blow?","o":["Brutus","Cassius","Casca","Decius"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Caesar compare himself to?","o":["The sun","The northern star","A lion","Mount Olympus"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Brutus allow Antony to do?","o":["Leave Rome","Speak at Caesar's funeral","Join the conspiracy","Rule with Cassius"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"Brutus explains to the citizens, in prose, that he killed Caesar because he loved Rome more and Caesar was ambitious. They cheer him and even want to make him Caesar. He leaves them to hear Antony. Antony, pretending to praise the conspirators as 'honourable men', disproves the charge of ambition, shows Caesar's wounds and cloak, and finally reads the will, which leaves money and parks to every citizen. The crowd riots, seeking to burn the conspirators' houses. Antony hears that Octavius has arrived and that Brutus and Cassius have fled.","key":["Brutus appeals to reason; Antony appeals to emotion, with evidence, props and irony.","The crowd's switch from Brutus to Antony shows how easily the people are moved.","Antony's 'Mischief, thou art afoot' reveals a cool political mind behind his grief."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Brutus say he killed Caesar?","o":["For money","Because he loved Rome more","Because Cassius told him to","Out of jealousy"],"a":1},{"q":"What phrase does Antony repeat?","o":["Caesar was ambitious","Brutus is an honourable man","Friends, Romans, countrymen","Revenge!"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Caesar's will leave each citizen?","o":["Land in Gaul","Seventy-five drachmas","A statue","Freedom from taxes"],"a":1}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Cinna the poet, who has dreamed of feasting with Caesar, is on his way to the funeral. The mob questions him and, hearing his name, tears him apart, though he insists he is not Cinna the conspirator.","key":["The 'liberty' the conspirators proclaimed has become mob violence.","The scene is darkly comic and then horrifying.","The confusion of names echoes the play's interest in what a name like 'Caesar' or 'Brutus' means."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who is Cinna in this scene?","o":["The conspirator","A poet","A soldier","A senator"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does the mob attack him?","o":["He insults Caesar","He has the same name as a conspirator","He is rich","He refuses to answer"],"a":1},{"q":"Where is he going?","o":["To the Senate","To Caesar's funeral","To Brutus's house","Out of Rome"],"a":1}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Antony, Octavius and Lepidus draw up a list of enemies to be executed, bargaining over their own relatives. Lepidus is sent for Caesar's will, which Antony now wants to cut back. Antony scorns Lepidus as a mere tool. They prepare to fight Brutus and Cassius.","key":["The new rulers are cold and ruthless, executing relatives with a pen mark.","Antony plans to reduce the will he used to win the crowd.","Tension between Antony and Octavius is already visible."],"quiz":[{"q":"What are the three men doing?","o":["Planning a funeral","Listing men to be put to death","Writing a new law","Dividing Caesar's money"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Antony describe Lepidus?","o":["As a great general","As a donkey carrying gold","As a dangerous rival","As his best friend"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Antony send for the will?","o":["To read it again","To cut some of the legacies","To burn it","To give it to Octavius"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"At Sardis, Brutus receives Cassius's messenger and senses their friendship cooling. Cassius arrives and accuses Brutus of wronging him. Brutus insists they argue privately in his tent, not before the armies.","key":["The conspirators' unity is breaking down.","Brutus still cares about appearances before the soldiers.","Brutus's image of hollow men as horses that fail under the spur sets up the quarrel."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where are Brutus and Cassius camped?","o":["Rome","Sardis","Philippi","Athens"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Brutus describe Cassius's friendship?","o":["As growing stronger","As a hot friend cooling","As false from the start","As unchanged"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Brutus want to argue in the tent?","o":["It is raining","So the armies don't see them quarrel","So Lucius can listen","To stay warm"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"In the tent, Brutus and Cassius quarrel bitterly over bribery and money. Cassius offers Brutus his dagger; they make up. Brutus reveals that Portia has killed herself. Messala brings news that the triumvirs have executed many senators, including Cicero. Brutus overrules Cassius again, deciding to march to Philippi. That night, as Lucius sleeps over his music, Caesar's Ghost appears to Brutus, promising to meet him at Philippi.","key":["The quarrel exposes the cracks in the cause: money, pride and wounded love.","Brutus's Stoic calm about Portia can be read as strength or coldness.","The Ghost confirms that Caesar's spirit, which Brutus meant to kill, is still alive."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Brutus accuse Cassius of?","o":["Cowardice","Taking bribes","Plotting against him","Killing Portia"],"a":1},{"q":"How did Portia die?","o":["In battle","By swallowing fire","Of fever","By poison"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does the Ghost say they will meet?","o":["Rome","Sardis","Philippi","The Capitol"],"a":2}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"On the plains of Philippi, Antony and Octavius disagree over who takes which wing. The generals meet and trade insults before battle. Cassius tells Messala it is his birthday and that he now half believes in omens. Brutus and Cassius discuss suicide, and say an everlasting farewell.","key":["The insults recall the murder and show bitterness on both sides.","Cassius, who once denied fate, now reads omens.","Brutus says suicide is cowardly, then says he will never be led in triumph: his contradiction shows the pressure he is under."],"quiz":[{"q":"What day is it for Cassius?","o":["His wedding day","His birthday","The Ides of March","A feast day"],"a":1},{"q":"What birds now follow the army?","o":["Eagles","Ravens, crows and kites","Doves","Owls"],"a":1},{"q":"What will Brutus never do?","o":["Fight Antony","Go bound to Rome","Forgive Cassius","Leave Philippi"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Brutus sends Messala with orders for his legions to attack Octavius's wing at once.","key":["Brutus acts quickly, sensing weakness.","His attack succeeds, but leaves Cassius exposed.","A short scene that shows battle as confused and fast."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does Brutus send with orders?","o":["Titinius","Messala","Lucilius","Pindarus"],"a":1},{"q":"Which wing does Brutus see is weak?","o":["Antony's","Octavius's","Lepidus's","Cassius's"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Brutus want his legions to do?","o":["Retreat","Attack at once","Wait for night","Surrender"],"a":1}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"Cassius's men are losing to Antony. Seeing horsemen surround Titinius, Pindarus reports wrongly that he has been captured. Cassius, believing all is lost, has Pindarus stab him with the sword that killed Caesar. Titinius returns with a victory wreath from Brutus, finds Cassius dead, crowns him and kills himself. Brutus arrives, mourns 'the last of all the Romans' and leads a second attack.","key":["Cassius dies through a misreading, as Cicero warned men misread signs.","Both Cassius and Titinius use Roman suicide as an honourable end.","Brutus admits Caesar's spirit is still 'mighty'."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Pindarus wrongly report?","o":["That Brutus is dead","That Titinius has been captured","That Antony has fled","That the camp is safe"],"a":1},{"q":"With what sword does Cassius die?","o":["Brutus's","The one that killed Caesar","Antony's","Titinius's"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Titinius bring Cassius?","o":["A letter","A victory wreath","Wine","News of Portia"],"a":1}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"In the second battle, young Cato dies bravely. Lucilius pretends to be Brutus and is captured. Antony recognises him, orders him treated kindly and sends soldiers to find whether Brutus is alive or dead.","key":["Lucilius's loyalty shows the love Brutus inspires.","Antony's respect for an enemy shows his generosity in victory.","The fight is now hopeless for Brutus's side."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whose son is young Cato?","o":["Marcus Cato's","Cassius's","Antony's","Cicero's"],"a":0},{"q":"Who pretends to be Brutus?","o":["Lucilius","Messala","Strato","Clitus"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Antony treat the prisoner?","o":["Has him killed","Orders kindness","Sends him to Rome","Ignores him"],"a":1}]},"a5s5":{"sum":"Defeated, Brutus asks his companions one by one to help him die. Clitus, Dardanius and Volumnius refuse. Brutus tells them the Ghost has appeared again. Finally Strato holds the sword and Brutus runs on it, telling Caesar to be still. Antony and Octavius arrive. Antony praises Brutus as 'the noblest Roman of them all', and Octavius orders an honourable burial.","key":["Brutus's death is calm and dignified, unlike the chaos of Caesar's.","His last words address Caesar, completing their story.","Antony's tribute leaves the audience to judge whether Brutus was truly noble."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who helps Brutus die?","o":["Clitus","Volumnius","Strato","Lucilius"],"a":2},{"q":"How many times has the Ghost appeared to Brutus?","o":["Once","Twice","Three times","Never"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Antony call Brutus?","o":["A traitor","The noblest Roman of them all","Caesar's murderer","A fool"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"2":"The play opens with authority telling the people off. Notice who speaks verse (the tribunes) and who speaks prose (the workmen).","7":"The cobbler's 'soles' and 'souls' pun is the first of many jokes that the tribunes cannot follow.","16":"Marullus remembers Pompey, Caesar's great rival. The crowd once cheered him the same way: they will switch sides again.","21":"Caesar as a bird to be clipped: power is pictured as height and flight.","32":"Calphurnia's barrenness matters: Caesar has no son to inherit, which makes the question of a crown and succession pressing.","41":"The Ides of March is the 15th. Caesar is warned twice and calls the man a dreamer.","55":"Brutus describes himself 'with himself at war', the divided mind that Cassius will exploit.","57":"Cassius's mirror image: he offers to show Brutus himself, but shows only what serves the plot.","65":"The swimming story is meant to show Caesar is no god; notice it also shows Cassius's envy and wounded pride.","68":"The Colossus of Rhodes was a giant statue said to straddle the harbour. Cassius makes Caesar huge and the Romans tiny.","78":"Caesar judges Cassius exactly, then says he does not fear him. Notice he asks Antony to move to his good ear.","96":"Casca's prose report is full of contempt for both Caesar and the crowd. We never see the crown-offering ourselves.","100":"Cassius twists 'falling sickness': it is Rome that has fallen, under Caesar.","122":"Cassius's soliloquy shows his method: forged letters in different handwriting. He thinks Brutus can be 'wrought' like metal.","127":"Storms in Shakespeare often mirror disorder in the state. Casca, the cynic of Scene 2, is now terrified.","130":"Cicero's point is the key to all the omens: people read signs to fit what they already think.","148":"Cassius talks of suicide as a Roman's freedom. He will use his dagger on himself in Act 5.","151":"Cassius blames the people: Caesar is a wolf only because the Romans are sheep.","178":"Brutus starts with his conclusion, 'It must be by his death', then searches for reasons. He admits Caesar has done nothing wrong yet.","189":"The letter is from Cassius, not 'Rome'. Brutus fills in the gaps ('&c.') with his own meaning.","195":"'Phantasma': the time between deciding and acting is a waking nightmare. Brutus compares himself to a kingdom in revolt.","222":"Brutus's speech against an oath is full of noble idealism, and it is the first time he overrules Cassius.","232":"Brutus wants a clean ritual killing. The actual murder, and the bathing in blood, will look very different.","234":"Brutus underestimates Antony as a mere party-goer. This is his second great mistake.","241":"Decius explains how to trap Caesar: through his love of being told he is above flattery.","260":"Portia's question about the 'suburbs' of Brutus's affection is a powerful complaint about being shut out of his life.","262":"Portia's wound is her proof that she can be as tough as a Roman man. She is Cato's daughter, a famous Stoic family.","297":"Caesar's most heroic lines. Compare his giving way to Calphurnia, then to Decius, only minutes later.","312":"Decius's reading of the dream flatters Caesar, but it is also true in a sense: Caesar's blood will 'revive' his name and his heirs.","328":"Brutus's aside: those who seem friends are not. His conscience is still active.","388":"Caesar's refusal is grand and proud. The conspirators use Metellus's petition to gather round him.","393":"The northern star stays fixed while all others move. Caesar's boast is his last full speech.","400":"'Et tu, Brute?' is Latin for 'You too, Brutus?'. It is the only Latin in the play.","418":"The conspirators bathe in Caesar's blood, as in Calphurnia's dream. The 'sacrifice' looks like butchery.","419":"Cassius imagines the murder being acted in future: a theatrical joke, since the audience is watching just that.","435":"Brutus explains the killing as an act of pity. Notice the strange logic of 'pity pity'.","438":"Antony shakes each bloody hand by name: a way of marking every murderer.","446":"Cassius sees the danger at once. Brutus overrules him a third time.","453":"Antony's soliloquy reveals his true feelings and predicts the civil war of Acts 4 and 5.","471":"Brutus speaks in prose, with balanced, logical sentences. He appeals to reason and honour.","479":"The crowd want to make Brutus 'Caesar': they have missed his whole point.","499":"Antony's speech is in verse. Count how often he calls Brutus 'honourable'; each time it sounds more sarcastic.","508":"Antony mentions the will, then refuses to read it: the best way to make the crowd demand it.","527":"Antony comes down among the people and uses Caesar's cloak and body as props.","538":"Antony claims to be 'no orator'. It is one of the most skilful speeches ever written.","557":"Alone, Antony is pleased with the riot. His grief and his politics are both real.","590":"The mob kills an innocent poet. This is what 'liberty' has become.","602":"Antony marks his own nephew for death. The triumvirate is crueller than Caesar ever was shown to be.","651":"Brutus reminds Cassius of the murder's ideal. He cannot accept compromise.","673":"Brutus will not raise money unjustly himself, but is angry Cassius did not send him the money he raised.","682":"Cassius offers his dagger and his heart. The scene turns from anger to love.","712":"Brutus knew of Portia's death during the quarrel. Some think the second report (to Messala) shows a play revised, others that Brutus is performing Stoic calm.","753":"The 'tide' speech is one of Brutus's finest, and it leads to defeat.","790":"The Ghost appears as the candle burns low, a traditional sign of a spirit.","857":"Cassius was a follower of Epicurus, who taught that the gods do not interfere in human life. Now he half believes the omens.","862":"Brutus says suicide is cowardly. Moments later he says he will never be led captive to Rome.","895":"Cassius dies by the same sword that killed Caesar.","913":"Titinius's 'misconstrued everything' is a key line: misreading destroys more people than enemies do.","920":"Brutus admits Caesar's spirit is still mighty: the murder has failed.","973":"Brutus is proud that no man was untrue to him. The audience may remember Caesar.","993":"Antony's tribute: of all the conspirators, only Brutus acted for the common good. Is he right?"},"gloss":{"mechanical":"working men, manual labourers","naughty":"good-for-nothing, worthless","awl":"a shoemaker's pointed tool","withal":"nevertheless; also with an awl","neat's leather":"cowhide","tributaries":"conquered rulers who pay tribute","replication":"echo","concave":"hollow","intermit":"hold back, delay","images":"statues","ceremonies":"decorations, ornaments","Lupercal":"a Roman fertility festival in February","vulgar":"common people","pitch":"height (of a falcon's flight)","servile":"slavish","barren":"unable to have children","press":"crowd","soothsayer":"fortune-teller","gamesome":"in the mood for games","quick":"lively","wont":"used to","construe":"interpret, read","cogitations":"thoughts","glass":"mirror","jealous on":"suspicious of","laugher":"joker","stale":"cheapen","protester":"someone who declares friendship","fawn":"flatter servilely","rout":"rabble, mob","indifferently":"impartially, calmly","lief":"willingly","buffet":"strike against","controversy":"rivalry, competition","Aeneas":"Trojan hero, legendary ancestor of the Romans","Anchises":"Aeneas's father, carried out of burning Troy","temper":"constitution, character","palm":"the victor's prize","Colossus":"giant statue said to straddle the harbour at Rhodes","underlings":"inferiors","conjure":"call up spirits","brook'd":"tolerated","chidden":"scolded","ferret":"small fierce animal with red eyes","sleek-headed":"with neatly groomed hair","coronets":"small crowns","rabblement":"rabble","chopt":"chapped","swooned":"fainted","falling-sickness":"epilepsy","tag-rag":"ragged, low-class","doublet":"close-fitting jacket","mettle":"spirit, quality","tardy":"slow, sluggish","humour":"win over; mood","riv'd":"split","portentous":"full of omens","prodigies":"unnatural events, omens","climate":"country, region","thunder-stone":"thunderbolt","unbraced":"with clothes unfastened","heralds":"messengers","thews":"muscles","bondman":"slave","hinds":"female deer; also servants","offal":"garbage, waste","fleering":"sneering","factious":"active in forming a party","redress":"putting right, remedy","praetor":"Roman magistrate, judge","alchemy":"the art of turning metals into gold","taper":"candle","spurn":"kick, strike","adder":"poisonous snake","remorse":"pity, mercy","affections":"passions","round":"rung of a ladder","exhalations":"meteors","phantasma":"nightmare, hallucination","genius":"a person's guardian spirit, mind","insurrection":"rebellion","moe":"more","Erebus":"the dark underworld","fret":"decorate with lines","palter":"cheat, go back on one's word","cautelous":"cautious, deceitful","carrions":"living corpses","envy":"malice, hatred","purgers":"healers who cleanse","augurers":"priests who read omens","unicorns":"mythical horned beasts","toils":"nets, snares","kerchief":"cloth worn on the head by the sick","exorcist":"someone who calls up spirits","mortified":"deadened","whelped":"given birth","yawn'd":"gaped open","hurtled":"clashed","tinctures":"cloths dipped in a martyr's blood","cognizance":"badges of service","ague":"fever","schedule":"written paper, document","couchings":"bowings","fond":"foolish","spaniel":"a fawning dog","cur":"mongrel dog","firmament":"the sky","Olympus":"mountain home of the gods","bootless":"in vain","enfranchisement":"freedom","pulpits":"public platforms for speakers","abide":"answer for, pay for","besmear":"smear","vouchsafe":"allow, grant","untrod":"new, unknown","misgiving":"suspicion","rank":"diseased, overgrown","purpled":"bloodstained","leaden":"made of lead, blunt","hart":"stag (a pun on heart)","lethe":"lifeblood; the river of forgetfulness","corse":"corpse","prick'd":"marked down","compact":"agreement","havoc":"signal for slaughter without mercy","Ate":"Greek goddess of strife and ruin","carrion":"rotting","cumber":"burden, overwhelm","issue":"deed; children","lovers":"friends","censure":"judge","extenuated":"played down","enforc'd":"exaggerated","interred":"buried","general coffers":"public treasury","grievously":"seriously, severely","napkins":"handkerchiefs","testament":"will","o'ershot":"gone too far","mantle":"cloak","Nervii":"a fierce tribe in Gaul, defeated by Caesar","dint":"force, impression","vesture":"clothing","mutiny":"riot, rebellion","drachmas":"silver coins","brands":"burning wood, torches","forms":"benches","proscription":"condemnation to death","legacies":"bequests in a will","provender":"food for animals","property":"tool, thing to be used","covert":"hidden","levying":"raising (troops)","jades":"worn-out horses","crests":"necks, heads","Sardians":"people of Sardis","chastisement":"punishment","choler":"anger","spleen":"bad temper","waspish":"irritable","indirection":"crooked means","counters":"worthless tokens","Plutus":"Greek god of wealth","yoked":"joined like oxen","cynic":"rude philosopher","jigging":"rhyming","insupportable":"unbearable","niggard":"satisfy sparingly","o'er-watch'd":"tired from staying awake","mace":"staff of office used to arrest people","bloody sign":"red battle flag","exigent":"crisis","parley":"talks","Hybla":"a place in Sicily famous for honey","masker":"someone who goes to masked parties","reveller":"party-goer","peevish":"silly, childish","Epicurus":"Greek philosopher who denied that the gods sent omens","presage":"foretell","ensign":"standard, flag","kites":"birds of prey","consorted":"accompanied","canopy":"covering","smatch":"taste, trace","Thassos":"an island near Philippi","Parthia":"a kingdom east of the Roman Empire","envenomed":"poisoned","apace":"quickly","bastard":"low-born man","entertain":"take into service","prefer":"recommend"}};
const PARTS={"16":[[0,4,"Celebrate what? What conquest is he bringing home? What captive kings follow him to Rome, chained to his chariot wheels to decorate his parade? You blocks, you stones, you are worse than senseless things! You hard-hearted, cruel men of Rome, didn't you know Pompey?"],[5,10,"Many times you climbed up onto walls and battlements, towers and windows, even chimney tops, with your babies in your arms, and sat there all day waiting patiently to see great Pompey pass through the streets of Rome."],[11,15,"And when you saw just his chariot appear, didn't you shout together so loudly that the Tiber trembled in its banks to hear the echo of your noise in its hollow shores?"],[16,23,"And now you put on your best clothes? And now you pick out a holiday? And now you scatter flowers in the path of the man who comes in triumph over Pompey's blood? Go! Run home, fall on your knees, and pray to the gods to hold back the punishment that is sure to fall on such ingratitude."]],"55":[[0,6,"Cassius, don't be misled. If I've looked troubled, my frown is aimed only at myself. Lately I have been troubled by conflicting feelings, thoughts that concern only me, which perhaps spoil my behaviour a little."],[7,11,"But don't let my good friends be upset by it (and you are one of them, Cassius), or read anything more into my neglect than that poor Brutus, at war with himself, forgets to show his affection to other men."]],"58":[[0,3,"That's right. And it's a great pity, Brutus, that you have no mirrors that could show your hidden worth to your own eyes, so you could see your reflection."],[4,8,"I have heard many of the most respected men in Rome (except immortal Caesar), speaking of Brutus and groaning under the oppression of these times, wish that noble Brutus could see himself as they do."]],"60":[[0,4,"So, good Brutus, get ready to listen. And since you know you can't see yourself as well as by reflection, I will be your mirror and modestly show you the part of yourself you don't yet know about."],[5,12,"And don't be suspicious of me, gentle Brutus. If I were a common joker, or cheapened my friendship by swearing it to every new person who claimed to be my friend; if you know that I flatter men and hug them hard and then slander them behind their backs; or if you know that I declare friendship to all the riff-raff when I'm feasting, then consider me dangerous."]],"65":[[0,6,"I know you have that virtue, Brutus, as well as I know your face. Well, honour is the subject of my story. I can't say what you and other men think of this life, but for my part I would rather not exist than live in awe of a man no better than I am."],[7,9,"I was born as free as Caesar, and so were you. We have both eaten as well as he has, and we can both stand the winter cold as well as he can."],[10,16,"Once, on a raw, windy day, when the stormy Tiber was crashing against its banks, Caesar said to me, \"Do you dare, Cassius, to leap into this angry river with me now and swim to that point over there?\" At that, dressed as I was, I plunged in and told him to follow, and so he did."],[17,21,"The torrent roared, and we fought it with strong muscles, throwing it aside and pushing against it with competitive hearts. But before we could reach the point, Caesar cried, \"Help me, Cassius, or I'll sink!\""],[22,28,"Just as Aeneas, our great ancestor, carried old Anchises out of the flames of Troy on his shoulder, so I carried the tired Caesar out of the waves of the Tiber. And this man has now become a god, and Cassius is a wretched creature who must bow down if Caesar even carelessly nods at him."],[29,34,"He had a fever when he was in Spain, and when the fit was on him I saw how he shook. It's true: this god shook. His cowardly lips lost their colour, and that same eye whose glance terrifies the world lost its shine. I heard him groan."],[35,41,"Yes, and that tongue of his, which ordered the Romans to listen to him and write his speeches down in their books, cried, \"Give me a drink, Titinius,\" like a sick girl. You gods, it amazes me that a man with such a weak constitution should get ahead of the whole majestic world and carry off the victor's prize alone."]],"68":[[0,6,"Why, man, he stands over the narrow world like a giant statue, and we little men walk under his huge legs and peer about to find ourselves shameful graves. At some point men are masters of their own fates. The fault, dear Brutus, is not in our stars but in ourselves, that we are underlings."],[7,12,"\"Brutus\" and \"Caesar\": what is so special about \"Caesar\"? Why should that name be spoken more than yours? Write them side by side: yours is just as fine a name. Say them: yours sounds as good. Weigh them: it's as heavy. Use them in spells: \"Brutus\" will call up a spirit as fast as \"Caesar.\""],[13,16,"Now, in the names of all the gods at once, what food does this Caesar of ours eat that makes him grow so great? Our age should be ashamed! Rome, you have lost the breed of noble men!"],[17,22,"Since the great flood, was there ever an age that wasn't famous for more than one man? Until now, when could anyone talking of Rome say that her wide walls held only one man? Now it really is Rome, and room enough, when there's only one man in it."],[23,26,"Oh, you and I have heard our fathers say that there was once a Brutus who would have let the devil himself rule in Rome as easily as he would have allowed a king."]],"69":[[0,4,"I don't doubt at all that you love me. I have some idea what you want to persuade me to do. What I think about this, and about these times, I'll tell you later. For now, I ask you as a friend, don't push me any further."],[5,8,"I will think about what you have said; I will listen patiently to whatever else you have to say, and find a suitable time both to hear and answer such serious matters."],[9,13,"Until then, my noble friend, think about this: Brutus would rather be a villager than call himself a son of Rome under the hard conditions these times are likely to put on us."]],"80":[[0,4,"I wish he were fatter! But I'm not afraid of him. Yet if Caesar could feel fear, I don't know any man I would avoid sooner than that thin Cassius. He reads a lot, he watches everything, and he sees right through what men do to their motives."],[5,9,"He doesn't like plays, as you do, Antony; he doesn't listen to music. He rarely smiles, and when he does, it's as if he were mocking himself and despising his own spirit for being moved to smile at anything."],[10,14,"Men like him are never comfortable while they see someone greater than themselves, and that's why they are very dangerous. I'm telling you what ought to be feared, not what I fear, because I am always Caesar."],[15,16,"Come round to my right side, because this ear is deaf, and tell me honestly what you think of him."]],"96":[[0,3,"I might as well be hanged as tell you how it happened. It was pure nonsense; I didn't pay attention. I saw Mark Antony offer him a crown, though it wasn't really a crown, it was one of those little coronets, and, as I told you, he pushed it away once. But all the same, I think he'd have liked to have it."],[4,5,"Then Antony offered it to him again, and he pushed it away again, but I think he was very unwilling to take his fingers off it. And then Antony offered it the third time, and he pushed it away the third time."],[6,9,"And each time he refused it, the mob hooted and clapped their chapped hands and threw up their sweaty nightcaps, and let out such a quantity of stinking breath because Caesar refused the crown that it almost choked Caesar, because he fainted and fell down from it."],[10,12,"And for my part, I didn't dare laugh, for fear of opening my lips and breathing in the bad air."]],"103":[[0,3,"Well, before he fell down, when he saw the common herd was glad he refused the crown, he pulled open his jacket and offered them his throat to cut. If I had been a working man with a tool in my hand, I'd have taken him at his word, or I hope I go to hell with the scoundrels. And so he fell."],[4,9,"When he came round again, he said that if he had done or said anything wrong, he wanted their worships to put it down to his illness. Three or four women near me cried, \"Oh, the poor man!\" and forgave him with all their hearts. But you can't take any notice of them: if Caesar had stabbed their mothers, they would have done the same."]],"122":[[0,4,"Well, Brutus, you are noble. And yet I see your honourable metal can be bent away from its natural shape. So it's right that noble minds should always keep company with their own kind, because who is so firm that he can't be led astray?"],[5,6,"Caesar holds a grudge against me, but he loves Brutus. If I were Brutus now and he were Cassius, he wouldn't win me round."],[7,14,"Tonight I will throw letters in through his windows in different handwriting, as if they came from different citizens, all about the high opinion Rome has of him, and hinting at Caesar's ambition. And after this, let Caesar sit tight, because we will shake him off, or suffer worse times."]],"127":[[0,5,"Aren't you disturbed when the whole earth shakes as if it were unsteady? Oh, Cicero, I have seen storms when the howling winds have split the gnarled oaks, and I have seen the ambitious ocean swell and rage and foam to reach as high as the threatening clouds."],[6,10,"But never until tonight, never until now, have I walked through a storm raining fire. Either there is civil war in heaven, or else the world has been so insolent to the gods that it has provoked them to destroy it."]],"129":[[0,6,"A common slave, you'd know him by sight, held up his left hand, which flamed and burned like twenty torches put together, and yet his hand felt no fire and stayed unburnt. Besides that (I haven't put my sword away since), by the Capitol I met a lion, which glared at me and walked sullenly past without attacking me."],[7,10,"And a hundred terrified women were huddled in a heap, changed out of all recognition by fear, who swore they saw men all on fire walking up and down the streets."],[11,17,"And yesterday the owl, the bird of night, sat in the market-place even at midday, hooting and shrieking. When these unnatural signs all happen together, don't let anyone say, \"There are reasons for them, they're natural,\" because I believe they are omens of disaster for the country they point to."]],"144":[[0,4,"You are slow, Casca, and you either lack the spark of life a Roman should have or you don't use it. You look pale and stare, and you are afraid and amazed to see the heavens so strangely angry."],[5,10,"But if you thought about the real reason why there are all these fires, all these gliding ghosts, why birds and animals act against their nature, why old men, fools and children make prophecies, why all these things change from their normal ways and their natural abilities to become monstrous,"],[11,14,"you would find that heaven has filled them with these spirits to make them instruments of fear, warning of a monstrous state of affairs."],[15,21,"Now, Casca, I could name a man to you who is very like this dreadful night, who thunders, flashes, opens graves and roars like the lion in the Capitol: a man no stronger than you or me in himself, and yet grown huge and frightening, like these strange outbreaks."]],"148":[[0,3,"Then I know where I'll put this dagger: Cassius will free Cassius from slavery. That is how, you gods, you make the weak strongest; that is how, you gods, you defeat tyrants."],[4,8,"No stone tower, no walls of hammered brass, no airless dungeon, no strong iron chains can imprison a determined spirit. Life, when it is tired of these earthly prison bars, always has the power to set itself free."],[9,11,"If I know this, let the rest of the world know too: the share of tyranny I suffer I can throw off whenever I like."]],"151":[[0,3,"Then why should Caesar be a tyrant? Poor man! I know he wouldn't be a wolf if he didn't see that the Romans are only sheep. He wouldn't be a lion if the Romans weren't deer."],[4,7,"People who want to make a great fire in a hurry start it with feeble straws. What trash Rome is, what rubbish, what garbage, when it serves as the worthless fuel to light up something as vile as Caesar!"],[8,12,"But, oh grief, where have you led me? Perhaps I am saying this in front of a willing slave. Then I know I'll have to answer for it. But I am armed, and dangers mean nothing to me."]],"153":[[0,4,"That's a bargain. Now you should know, Casca, that I have already persuaded some of the noblest Romans to undertake with me an enterprise that is both honourable and dangerous."],[5,10,"And I know that by now they are waiting for me in Pompey's Porch, because on this frightening night no one is moving or walking in the streets, and the look of the sky is like the work we have in hand: bloody, fiery and terrible."]],"178":[[0,3,"It will have to be by his death. And for my own part, I have no personal reason to strike at him, only the good of the people. He wants to be crowned. How that might change his nature, that's the question."],[4,7,"It's the bright sunshine that brings the snake out, and that calls for careful walking. Crown him? That's it. And then, I admit, we put a sting in him that he can use to do harm whenever he likes."],[8,10,"Greatness is abused when it separates power from mercy. And to tell the truth about Caesar, I have never known his feelings rule his reason."],[11,16,"But it's common experience that humility is the ladder young ambition climbs, with his face turned towards it as he goes up. But once he reaches the top rung, he turns his back on the ladder and looks into the clouds, scorning the lower steps he climbed by."],[17,21,"Caesar might do that. So, in case he might, we must prevent it. And since our quarrel can't be justified by what he is now, put it like this: what he is, if increased, would run to these and these extremes."],[22,24,"So think of him as a serpent's egg which, once hatched, would grow harmful like the rest of its kind, and kill him in the shell."]],"189":[[0,4,"\"Brutus, you are asleep. Wake up and see yourself. Shall Rome, etc. Speak, strike, put things right!\" \"Brutus, you are asleep. Wake up!\" Messages urging me like this have often been dropped where I have picked them up."],[5,8,"\"Shall Rome, etc.\" I must fill in the rest like this: shall Rome stand in awe of one man? What, Rome? My ancestors drove Tarquin out of the streets of Rome when he was called a king."],[9,12,"\"Speak, strike, put things right!\" Am I being begged to speak and strike? Oh, Rome, I promise you, if putting things right will follow, you will get everything you ask for from the hand of Brutus."]],"195":[[0,4,"Since Cassius first sharpened me against Caesar, I haven't slept. Between doing a dreadful thing and first thinking of it, all the time in between is like a nightmare or a hideous dream."],[5,8,"The mind and the body's powers are then arguing it out, and a man, like a little kingdom, goes through a kind of rebellion."]],"204":[[0,3,"They are the conspirators. Oh, conspiracy, are you ashamed to show your dangerous face even at night, when evil is most free? Oh, then, by day where will you find a cave dark enough to hide your monstrous face?"],[4,8,"Don't look for one, conspiracy. Hide it in smiles and friendliness. Because if you walk about in your true appearance, not even the darkness of the underworld would be dark enough to keep you from being stopped."]],"222":[[0,4,"No, not an oath. If the look on men's faces, the suffering of our souls and the corruption of the times are weak motives, let's give up now and every man go back to his lazy bed. Then let ambitious tyranny run wild until each of us dies at the tyrant's whim."],[5,9,"But if these motives, as I am sure they do, have fire enough to set cowards alight and to harden with courage the soft spirits of women, then, countrymen, what spur do we need but our own cause to drive us to put things right?"],[10,14,"What other bond than that we are Romans who can keep a secret, who have given our word and won't go back on it? And what other oath than honest men pledged to each other that this will happen or we will die for it?"],[15,17,"Let priests and cowards and cautious men swear, and feeble old wrecks and those meek souls who welcome being wronged. Let creatures that no one trusts swear to bad causes."],[18,26,"But don't stain the pure virtue of our enterprise or the unconquerable courage of our spirits by thinking that either our cause or our action needs an oath, when every drop of blood in every Roman, nobly carried, would prove itself not truly his, if he broke the smallest part of any promise he had made."]],"232":[[0,4,"Our action will seem too bloody, Caius Cassius, to cut the head off and then hack at the limbs, as if we killed in anger and kept up the hatred afterwards, because Antony is only a limb of Caesar. Let us be sacrificers, not butchers, Caius."],[5,8,"We are all standing up against the spirit of Caesar, and in men's spirits there is no blood. Oh, if only we could get at Caesar's spirit and not cut up Caesar! But, alas, Caesar must bleed for it."],[9,12,"And, gentle friends, let's kill him boldly but not angrily. Let's carve him like a dish fit for the gods, not hack him like a carcass fit for dogs."],[13,16,"And let our hearts, like cunning masters, stir up their servants, our hands, to a violent act, and then seem to scold them for it. This will make our purpose look necessary and not spiteful."],[17,18,"And if it looks like that to ordinary people, we'll be called healers who purge the state, not murderers."],[19,21,"As for Mark Antony, don't think about him, because he can do no more than Caesar's arm can once Caesar's head is off."]],"240":[[0,4,"But it's still uncertain whether Caesar will come out today or not, because he has grown superstitious lately, quite unlike the strong opinion he once held about fancies, dreams and omens."],[5,8,"It may be that these obvious signs, the unusual terrors of this night and the persuasion of his fortune-tellers will keep him away from the Capitol today."]],"241":[[0,4,"Don't worry about that. If he has made up his mind to stay, I can win him round, because he loves to hear that unicorns can be trapped by trees, bears with mirrors, elephants with pits, lions with nets, and men with flatterers."],[5,9,"But when I tell him he hates flatterers, he says he does, and that's when he is most flattered. Let me work on him, because I can turn his mood the right way, and I will bring him to the Capitol."]],"254":[[0,5,"Nor for yours either. You crept unkindly from my bed, Brutus. And last night at supper you suddenly got up and walked about, brooding and sighing, with your arms folded. And when I asked you what was wrong, you stared at me unkindly."],[6,9,"I pressed you further; then you scratched your head and stamped your foot too impatiently. Still I insisted, and still you didn't answer, but with an angry wave of your hand signalled me to leave you."],[10,14,"So I did, afraid of making worse an impatience that seemed already too inflamed, and hoping too that it was only a passing mood, which every man has sometimes."],[15,19,"It won't let you eat or talk or sleep, and if it could change your appearance as much as it has changed your temper, I wouldn't recognise you, Brutus. My dear lord, tell me the cause of your unhappiness."]],"258":[[0,5,"Is Brutus sick, and is it healthy to walk about with his clothes undone, breathing in the damp morning air? What, is Brutus sick, and will he creep out of his healthy bed to risk the foul diseases of the night and tempt the damp, unclean air to make him sicker?"],[6,8,"No, my Brutus, you have some illness in your mind, which I have a right, as your wife, to know about."],[9,17,"And on my knees I beg you, by my beauty which you once praised, by all your vows of love, and by that great vow which joined us into one, that you reveal to me, your self, your other half, why you are so sad, and what men have visited you tonight. Six or seven have been here, who hid their faces even from the darkness."]],"260":[[0,5,"I wouldn't need to if you were gentle Brutus. Tell me, Brutus, is it a condition of our marriage that I shouldn't know any secrets that concern you? Am I part of you only in a limited way, to keep you company at meals, comfort you in bed and talk to you sometimes?"],[6,8,"Do I live only on the outskirts of your affection? If that's all, Portia is Brutus's whore, not his wife."]],"262":[[0,4,"If that were true, I would know this secret. I admit I am a woman, but a woman whom Lord Brutus took as his wife. I admit I am a woman, but a woman with a good name, Cato's daughter."],[5,7,"Do you think I am no stronger than other women, with such a father and such a husband? Tell me your plans; I won't reveal them."],[8,11,"I have given strong proof of my firmness by deliberately wounding myself here, in the thigh. Can I bear that patiently and not my husband's secrets?"]],"294":[[0,3,"Caesar, I never paid attention to omens, but now they frighten me. There is someone in the house who, besides the things we have heard and seen, tells of the most horrible sights seen by the night watch."],[4,8,"A lioness has given birth in the streets, and graves have opened and given up their dead. Fierce, fiery warriors fight on the clouds in ranks and squadrons and proper battle formation, and they drizzled blood on the Capitol."],[9,13,"The noise of battle clashed in the air, horses neighed, dying men groaned, and ghosts shrieked and squealed in the streets. Oh, Caesar, these things are beyond all experience, and I'm afraid of them!"]],"311":[[0,3,"The reason is my will: I will not come. That is enough to satisfy the Senate. But for your private satisfaction, because I love you, I'll tell you."],[4,8,"Calphurnia here, my wife, is keeping me at home. Last night she dreamt she saw my statue, which ran with pure blood like a fountain with a hundred spouts, and many strong, cheerful Romans came smiling and bathed their hands in it."],[9,11,"She interprets these as warnings and omens of evils about to happen, and on her knees she has begged me to stay at home today."]],"314":[[0,3,"I have, as you'll see when you've heard what I have to say. And know this now: the Senate has decided to give a crown to mighty Caesar today. If you send word that you won't come, they may change their minds."],[4,9,"Besides, someone would be likely to make a joke of it and say, \"Close the Senate until another time, when Caesar's wife has better dreams.\" If Caesar hides, won't they whisper, \"Look, Caesar is afraid\"?"],[10,12,"Forgive me, Caesar, because my deep, deep love for your advancement makes me tell you this, and my love overrules my sense of what is proper."]],"332":[[0,2,"\"Caesar, beware of Brutus; watch out for Cassius; don't go near Casca; keep an eye on Cinna; don't trust Trebonius; watch Metellus Cimber closely; Decius Brutus doesn't love you; you have wronged Caius Ligarius."],[3,6,"All these men have one purpose, and it is aimed at Caesar. If you are not immortal, look about you: overconfidence opens the way to conspiracy. May the mighty gods defend you! Your friend, Artemidorus.\""],[7,10,"I'll stand here until Caesar passes, and give him this as if it were a petition. My heart grieves that goodness cannot live beyond the reach of envy's teeth."],[11,12,"If you read this, Caesar, you may live. If not, the Fates are plotting with the traitors."]],"356":[[0,5,"I must go in. [Aside] Oh dear, what a weak thing a woman's heart is! Oh, Brutus, may the heavens help you in your enterprise! I'm sure the boy heard me. Brutus has a petition that Caesar won't grant. Oh, I feel faint."],[6,8,"Run, Lucius, and give my love to my husband. Tell him I'm cheerful, then come back to me and tell me what he says to you."]],"388":[[0,8,"I must stop you, Cimber. This crouching and humble bowing might fire up the blood of ordinary men and turn what has already been decided into a child's game. Don't be so foolish as to think that Caesar's blood is so unruly that it can be melted from its true nature by what melts fools: I mean sweet words, low bows and the cringing of a spaniel."],[9,13,"Your brother has been banished by law. If you bow and beg and fawn for him, I'll kick you out of my way like a dog. Know that Caesar does no wrong, and he will not be persuaded without good reason."]],"393":[[0,4,"I could easily be moved if I were like you. If I could beg others to change their minds, other people's begging would move me. But I am as constant as the northern star, which has no equal in the sky for staying fixed and still."],[5,7,"The skies are painted with countless sparks. They are all fire, and every one shines, but only one of them all keeps its place."],[8,13,"It's the same on earth. It is well supplied with men, and men are flesh and blood and can think. Yet out of all of them I know only one who keeps his position without being shaken or moved, and that I am he, let me show a little, even in this:"],[14,15,"I was firm that Cimber should be banished, and I remain firm in keeping him banished."]],"426":[[0,4,"Brutus, my master told me to kneel like this. Mark Antony told me to fall down like this, and when I lay at your feet, he told me to say this: Brutus is noble, wise, brave and honest; Caesar was mighty, bold, royal and loving."],[5,6,"Say I love Brutus and honour him. Say I feared Caesar, honoured him and loved him."],[7,9,"If Brutus will allow Antony to come to him safely and have it explained to him how Caesar deserved to die,"],[10,14,"Mark Antony will not love Caesar dead as much as Brutus living, but will follow noble Brutus's fortunes and affairs through all the dangers of this new state of things, with complete loyalty. That is what my master Antony says."]],"434":[[0,2,"Oh, mighty Caesar! Are you lying so low? Have all your conquests, glories, triumphs and spoils shrunk to this small space? Goodbye."],[3,8,"I don't know, gentlemen, what you intend, who else must have blood let, who else is diseased. If it's me, there is no hour as fitting as the hour of Caesar's death, and no weapon half as worthy as your swords, made rich with the noblest blood in the whole world."],[9,12,"I beg you, if you have something against me, do what you want now, while your bloodstained hands are still steaming. If I live a thousand years, I won't find myself so ready to die."],[13,15,"No place will please me as much, and no way of dying, as here beside Caesar, cut down by you, the finest and most powerful spirits of this age."]],"435":[[0,4,"Oh, Antony, don't beg us for your death. Though we must look bloody and cruel now, as you can see from our hands and what we have just done, you only see our hands and the bleeding work they have done."],[5,7,"You don't see our hearts. They are full of pity, and pity for the wrong done to all of Rome (as fire drives out fire, so pity drives out pity) has done this to Caesar."],[8,12,"As for you, Mark Antony, our swords have blunt lead points. Our arms, though strong enough to seem hostile, and our hearts, full of brotherly feeling, welcome you in with all kind love, good thoughts and respect."]],"438":[[0,6,"I don't doubt your wisdom. Let each man give me his bloody hand. First, Marcus Brutus, I'll shake yours. Next, Caius Cassius, I take your hand. Now, Decius Brutus, yours; now yours, Metellus; yours, Cinna; and, my brave Casca, yours. Last, but not least in my love, yours, good Trebonius."],[7,10,"Gentlemen all—oh, what can I say? My reputation now stands on such slippery ground that you must think of me in one of two bad ways, either as a coward or as a flatterer."],[11,16,"That I loved you, Caesar, oh, it's true! If your spirit is looking down on us now, won't it grieve you more than your death to see your Antony making his peace, shaking the bloody fingers of your enemies, most noble Caesar, in the presence of your body?"],[17,20,"If I had as many eyes as you have wounds, weeping as fast as they pour out your blood, it would suit me better than making friends with your enemies."],[21,23,"Forgive me, Julius! Here you were cornered, brave stag. Here you fell, and here your hunters stand, marked with your slaughter and red with your lifeblood."],[24,27,"Oh, world, you were the forest for this stag, and this, oh world, was truly your heart. How like a deer struck down by many princes you lie here!"]],"453":[[0,4,"Oh, forgive me, you bleeding piece of earth, for being meek and gentle with these butchers. You are the ruins of the noblest man who ever lived in the course of time. Woe to the hand that shed this precious blood!"],[5,8,"Over your wounds, which open their red lips like mouths that cannot speak, begging my tongue to speak for them, I now prophesy: a curse will fall on men's bodies."],[9,15,"Fighting within families and fierce civil war will overrun every part of Italy. Blood and destruction will be so common, and dreadful sights so familiar, that mothers will only smile when they see their babies cut to pieces by the hands of war, all pity choked by the habit of cruel deeds."],[16,21,"And Caesar's spirit, roaming for revenge, with Ate, goddess of ruin, hot from hell at his side, will cry \"Havoc!\" throughout this land in a king's voice and let loose the dogs of war, so that this foul deed will stink above the earth with rotting men groaning to be buried."]],"461":[[0,3,"Ride back fast and tell him what has happened. This is a mourning Rome, a dangerous Rome, not yet a safe Rome for Octavius. Hurry and tell him so. But wait a moment."],[4,10,"Don't go back until I have carried this body into the market-place. There, in my speech, I will test how the people take the cruel act of these bloody men, and according to that you will report to young Octavius how things stand. Give me a hand."]],"471":[[0,3,"Be patient until I've finished. Romans, countrymen and friends, hear me for the sake of my cause, and be silent so that you can hear. Believe me because of my honour, and respect my honour so that you can believe me. Judge me with your wisdom, and wake up your minds so that you can judge better."],[4,7,"If there is anyone in this crowd, any dear friend of Caesar's, I say to him that Brutus's love for Caesar was no less than his. If that friend then asks why Brutus rose against Caesar, this is my answer: not that I loved Caesar less, but that I loved Rome more."],[8,8,"Would you rather Caesar were alive and all of you died slaves, than that Caesar were dead and all of you lived as free men?"],[9,12,"Because Caesar loved me, I weep for him; because he was fortunate, I rejoice at it; because he was brave, I honour him; but because he was ambitious, I killed him. There are tears for his love, joy for his good fortune, honour for his courage and death for his ambition."],[13,17,"Who here is so low that he would be a slave? If anyone, speak, because I have offended him. Who here is so uncivilised that he would not be a Roman? If anyone, speak, because I have offended him. Who here is so vile that he will not love his country? If anyone, speak, because I have offended him. I pause for a reply."]],"499":[[0,3,"Friends, Romans, countrymen, listen to me. I have come to bury Caesar, not to praise him. The evil that men do lives on after them; the good is often buried with their bones. Let it be so with Caesar."],[4,7,"The noble Brutus has told you that Caesar was ambitious. If that was so, it was a serious fault, and Caesar has paid seriously for it."],[8,11,"Here, with the permission of Brutus and the rest (for Brutus is an honourable man; so are they all, all honourable men), I have come to speak at Caesar's funeral."],[12,14,"He was my friend, faithful and fair to me. But Brutus says he was ambitious, and Brutus is an honourable man."],[15,21,"He brought many captives home to Rome, whose ransoms filled the public treasury. Did this seem ambitious in Caesar? When the poor cried, Caesar wept. Ambition should be made of harder stuff. Yet Brutus says he was ambitious, and Brutus is an honourable man."],[22,26,"You all saw that at the Lupercal I offered him a king's crown three times, and three times he refused it. Was this ambition? Yet Brutus says he was ambitious, and he is certainly an honourable man."],[27,30,"I'm not speaking to disprove what Brutus said, but I am here to say what I know. You all loved him once, and not without reason. What reason stops you mourning for him, then?"],[31,34,"Oh, judgement, you have run away to the brute beasts, and men have lost their reason. Bear with me. My heart is in the coffin there with Caesar, and I must pause until it comes back to me."]],"508":[[0,2,"Only yesterday the word of Caesar could have stood against the whole world. Now he lies there, and no one is so low as to show him respect."],[3,9,"Oh, friends! If I wanted to stir your hearts and minds to rebellion and rage, I would wrong Brutus and wrong Cassius, who, you all know, are honourable men. I will not wrong them. I would rather wrong the dead, wrong myself and you, than wrong such honourable men."],[10,13,"But here's a document with Caesar's seal. I found it in his study. It's his will. If the common people could only hear this will (which, forgive me, I don't intend to read),"],[14,19,"they would go and kiss dead Caesar's wounds and dip their handkerchiefs in his sacred blood, yes, beg for a hair of his to remember him by, and when they died they would mention it in their own wills, leaving it as a precious inheritance to their children."]],"527":[[0,4,"If you have tears, get ready to shed them now. You all know this cloak. I remember the first time Caesar ever put it on. It was a summer evening in his tent, the day he defeated the Nervii."],[5,11,"Look, here is where Cassius's dagger went through. See what a tear spiteful Casca made. Through this one the much-loved Brutus stabbed, and as he pulled his cursed steel away, see how Caesar's blood followed it, as if rushing out of doors to find out for certain whether it really was Brutus knocking so unkindly or not."],[12,14,"Because Brutus, as you know, was Caesar's darling. Judge, you gods, how dearly Caesar loved him! This was the most unkind cut of all."],[15,20,"Because when noble Caesar saw him stab, ingratitude, stronger than traitors' weapons, completely defeated him. Then his mighty heart burst, and, covering his face with his cloak, great Caesar fell at the foot of Pompey's statue, which ran with blood all the while."],[21,23,"Oh, what a fall that was, my countrymen! Then I and you and all of us fell down, while bloody treason triumphed over us."],[24,28,"Oh, now you are weeping, and I see you feel the force of pity. These are gracious tears. Kind souls, why do you weep when you only see our Caesar's clothes wounded? Look here, here is the man himself, mutilated, as you see, by traitors."]],"538":[[0,5,"Good friends, sweet friends, don't let me stir you up into such a sudden flood of rebellion. The men who did this deed are honourable. What private grudges they had that made them do it, alas, I don't know. They are wise and honourable, and no doubt they will give you reasons."],[6,10,"I haven't come, friends, to steal your hearts. I am no speaker, as Brutus is, but, as you all know, a plain, blunt man who loves his friend, and the men who gave me public permission to speak about him know that very well."],[11,15,"Because I have neither cleverness, nor words, nor standing, nor gestures, nor delivery, nor the power of speech to stir men's blood. I just speak straight out. I tell you what you already know, show you sweet Caesar's wounds, poor, poor speechless mouths, and tell them to speak for me."],[16,20,"But if I were Brutus, and Brutus were Antony, then there would be an Antony who would stir up your spirits and put a tongue in every one of Caesar's wounds that would move the very stones of Rome to rise up and rebel."]],"608":[[0,5,"Octavius, I have lived longer than you. And though we give these honours to this man to take some of the blame off ourselves, he will only carry them as a donkey carries gold, groaning and sweating under the work, either led or driven, as we point the way."],[6,9,"And when he has carried our treasure where we want it, then we take off his load and turn him out, like an unloaded donkey, to shake his ears and graze on the common."]],"610":[[0,5,"So is my horse, Octavius, and for that I give him a supply of food. It is a creature I teach to fight, to turn, to stop, to run straight on, its bodily movements controlled by my mind. And in some ways Lepidus is just like that."],[6,9,"He has to be taught and trained and told to go. He's a man with no ideas of his own, who lives on curiosities, skills and fashions that have already been used up and grown stale with other men before he takes them up."],[10,13,"Don't talk of him except as a tool. And now, Octavius, listen to important things. Brutus and Cassius are raising armies. We must raise a force at once."],[14,18,"So let's join our alliance together, gather our best friends and stretch our resources as far as they will go, and let's sit down in council at once to decide how hidden dangers can best be discovered and open dangers most safely met."]],"651":[[0,2,"Remember March, remember the fifteenth of March. Didn't great Julius bleed for the sake of justice? What villain touched his body and stabbed him, except for justice?"],[3,8,"What, shall one of us who struck down the greatest man in all this world just for protecting robbers, shall we now dirty our fingers with low bribes and sell the great range of our high honours for as much trash as can be grabbed like this?"],[9,10,"I would rather be a dog and howl at the moon than be a Roman like that."]],"661":[[0,3,"All this? Yes, more. Fume until your proud heart breaks. Go and show your slaves how bad-tempered you are, and make your servants tremble. Must I flinch? Must I treat you with respect? Must I stand and cower under your irritable mood?"],[4,8,"By the gods, you will swallow the poison of your own anger, even if it splits you open, because from today on I'll use you for my amusement, yes, for my laughter, when you are being waspish."]],"673":[[0,3,"You have done something you should be sorry for. There is nothing frightening in your threats, Cassius, because I am so strongly armed with honesty that they pass me by like the empty wind, which I ignore."],[4,6,"I sent to you for certain sums of gold, which you refused me, because I can't raise money by dishonest means."],[7,9,"By heaven, I would rather mint my heart into coins and drop my blood for drachmas than squeeze their wretched pennies out of the hard hands of peasants by crooked means."],[10,13,"I sent to you for gold to pay my legions, which you refused me. Was that done like Cassius? Would I have answered Caius Cassius like that?"],[14,17,"When Marcus Brutus grows so greedy that he locks such worthless coins away from his friends, be ready, gods, with all your thunderbolts, and smash him to pieces!"]],"682":[[0,5,"Come, Antony, and young Octavius, come, take your revenge on Cassius alone, because Cassius is tired of the world: hated by someone he loves, defied by his brother, scolded like a slave, all his faults watched, written in a notebook, studied and learned by heart to throw in my face."],[6,9,"Oh, I could weep my spirit out through my eyes! There is my dagger, and here is my bare chest, and inside it a heart more precious than the mines of Plutus, god of riches, richer than gold."],[10,14,"If you are a Roman, take it out. I, who refused you gold, will give you my heart. Strike as you did at Caesar, because I know that when you hated him most, you loved him more than you ever loved Cassius."]],"751":[[0,3,"Good reasons must give way to better ones. The people between Philippi and here are only on our side because they have been forced to be, since they have resented giving us supplies."],[4,9,"The enemy, marching through their country, will add to his numbers from them and come on refreshed, reinforced and encouraged. We will cut him off from that advantage if we face him at Philippi, with these people behind us."]],"753":[[0,4,"Excuse me. You must also bear in mind that we have got all we can from our friends. Our legions are full to the brim, our cause is ripe. The enemy grows stronger every day; we, at our peak, are about to decline."],[5,8,"There is a tide in the affairs of men which, taken at high water, leads on to fortune. If it is missed, the whole voyage of their life is stuck in shallows and misery."],[9,11,"We are now afloat on such a high sea, and we must take the current while it is with us or lose everything we have risked."]],"857":[[0,5,"Messala, this is my birthday. Cassius was born on this very day. Give me your hand, Messala. Be my witness that, against my will, as Pompey was, I am forced to risk all our freedom on one battle."],[6,8,"You know I strongly believed in Epicurus and his teaching that omens mean nothing. Now I have changed my mind, and partly believe in signs that predict the future."],[9,12,"On the way from Sardis, two mighty eagles swooped down on our front flag and perched there, gorging and feeding from our soldiers' hands, and they came with us here to Philippi."],[13,18,"This morning they have flown away and gone, and in their place ravens, crows and kites fly over our heads and look down on us as if we were sickly prey. Their shadows seem a deadly canopy, under which our army lies, ready to die."]],"864":[[0,4,"No, Cassius, no. Don't think, you noble Roman, that Brutus will ever go to Rome in chains. His mind is too great for that. But this same day must finish the work that the fifteenth of March began, and I don't know whether we shall meet again."],[5,8,"So take my everlasting farewell. For ever, and for ever, farewell, Cassius. If we meet again, why, we'll smile. If not, then this parting was well done."]],"895":[[0,3,"Come here, you. In Parthia I took you prisoner, and then, in return for sparing your life, I made you swear that whatever I told you to do, you would attempt it."],[4,6,"Come now, keep your oath. Now be a free man, and with this good sword, which ran through Caesar's guts, search out this heart."],[7,10,"Don't stop to answer. Here, take the hilt, and when my face is covered, as it is now, guide the sword. Caesar, you are avenged, with the very sword that killed you."]],"913":[[0,4,"Why did you send me out, brave Cassius? Didn't I meet your friends? And didn't they put this victory wreath on my head and tell me to give it to you? Didn't you hear their shouts? Oh, you misunderstood everything!"],[5,8,"But wait, take this garland on your head. Your Brutus told me to give it to you, and I will do what he told me. Brutus, come quickly and see how I honoured Caius Cassius."],[9,10,"With your permission, gods: this is a Roman's role. Come, Cassius's sword, and find Titinius's heart."]],"923":[[0,4,"Are there still two Romans alive like these? The last of all the Romans, farewell! It is impossible that Rome should ever produce your equal. Friends, I owe more tears to this dead man than you will see me pay."],[5,7,"I'll find time, Cassius, I'll find time. So come, and send his body to Thassos. His funeral must not be held in our camp, in case it disheartens us."],[8,12,"Lucilius, come, and come, young Cato. Let's go to the battlefield. Labeo and Flavius, move our armies forward. It's three o'clock, and, Romans, before night we will try our luck in a second fight."]],"973":[[0,4,"Goodbye to you, and you, and you, Volumnius. Strato, you've been asleep all this time. Goodbye to you too, Strato. Countrymen, my heart is glad that in all my life I never found a man who wasn't true to me."],[5,7,"I will have more glory from this day of defeat than Octavius and Mark Antony will gain by their vile victory."],[8,11,"So goodbye to you all at once, because Brutus's tongue has almost finished telling the story of his life. Night hangs on my eyes. My bones want to rest, after working only to reach this hour."]],"993":[[0,4,"This was the noblest Roman of them all. All the conspirators except him did what they did out of envy of great Caesar. He alone joined them with honest thoughts for the general good of all."],[5,7,"His life was gentle, and the elements were so well mixed in him that Nature could stand up and say to all the world, \"This was a man!\""]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"power":["Power and ambition","Caesar's rise, the fear of a king, and the new rulers who replace him."],"honour":["Honour and nobility","What Brutus means by honour, and how others use the word against him."],"rhetoric":["Persuasion and rhetoric","Letters, flattery, oratory: how words move men to act."],"fate":["Fate, omens and free will","Storms, dreams, soothsayers and ghosts, against 'the fault is in ourselves'."],"friendship":["Friendship and loyalty","Brutus and Cassius, Caesar and Antony, masters and servants."],"crowd":["The people and the mob","The citizens who cheer Pompey, Caesar, Brutus and Antony in turn."],"public":["Public and private selves","Masks, wives, sleepless nights and the gap between the Forum and the orchard."],"violence":["Violence and sacrifice","Blood, butchery, sacrifice and the civil war that follows."]},"quotes":[{"id":"16","who":"MARULLUS","text":"You blocks, you stones, you worse than senseless things!","themes":["crowd"],"note":"The play's first image of the people: fickle and forgetful, cheering Caesar over Pompey's blood."},{"id":"21","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"These growing feathers pluck'd from Caesar's wing Will make him fly an ordinary pitch,","themes":["power"],"note":"Caesar as a bird of prey that must be clipped before he soars out of reach."},{"id":"41","who":"SOOTHSAYER","text":"Beware the Ides of March.","themes":["fate"],"note":"Four words that hang over the first half of the play."},{"id":"48","who":"CAESAR","text":"He is a dreamer; let us leave him. Pass.","themes":["fate","power"],"note":"Caesar brushes aside the warning in a single line."},{"id":"55","who":"BRUTUS","text":"poor Brutus, with himself at war,","themes":["public","honour"],"note":"Brutus's inner conflict, named in his first long speech."},{"id":"60","who":"CASSIUS","text":"I, your glass, Will modestly discover to yourself That of yourself which you yet know not of.","themes":["rhetoric","friendship"],"note":"Cassius offers to be Brutus's mirror, and shows him a flattering picture."},{"id":"64","who":"BRUTUS","text":"I love The name of honour more than I fear death.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Brutus's creed: honour above life."},{"id":"65","who":"CASSIUS","text":"Alas, it cried, \"Give me some drink, Titinius,\" As a sick girl.","themes":["power","rhetoric"],"note":"Cassius mocks Caesar's weakness: the god who shook with fever."},{"id":"68","who":"CASSIUS","text":"Why, man, he doth bestride the narrow world Like a Colossus, and we petty men Walk under his huge legs, and peep about To find ourselves dishonourable graves.","themes":["power"],"note":"Caesar as a giant statue straddling the world."},{"id":"68","who":"CASSIUS","text":"The fault, dear Brutus, is not in our stars, But in ourselves, that we are underlings.","themes":["fate","power"],"note":"Cassius denies fate: men choose to be ruled."},{"id":"69","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Brutus had rather be a villager Than to repute himself a son of Rome Under these hard conditions as this time Is like to lay upon us.","themes":["honour","power"],"note":"Brutus admits his discontent but promises nothing."},{"id":"78","who":"CAESAR","text":"Yond Cassius has a lean and hungry look; He thinks too much: such men are dangerous.","themes":["power","friendship"],"note":"Caesar reads Cassius exactly."},{"id":"80","who":"CAESAR","text":"I rather tell thee what is to be fear'd Than what I fear; for always I am Caesar.","themes":["power","public"],"note":"Caesar speaks of himself in the third person, then asks Antony to come to his good ear."},{"id":"109","who":"CASCA","text":"it was Greek to me.","themes":["rhetoric"],"note":"Casca's joke, now a common saying."},{"id":"122","who":"CASSIUS","text":"Well, Brutus, thou art noble; yet I see, Thy honourable metal may be wrought From that it is dispos'd:","themes":["honour","rhetoric"],"note":"Alone, Cassius admits he is bending Brutus like metal."},{"id":"127","who":"CASCA","text":"Either there is a civil strife in heaven, Or else the world too saucy with the gods, Incenses them to send destruction.","themes":["fate"],"note":"The storm as heaven's civil war."},{"id":"130","who":"CICERO","text":"But men may construe things after their fashion, Clean from the purpose of the things themselves.","themes":["fate","rhetoric"],"note":"Cicero's warning about interpretation."},{"id":"151","who":"CASSIUS","text":"I know he would not be a wolf, But that he sees the Romans are but sheep:","themes":["power","crowd"],"note":"Cassius blames the Romans for making Caesar a tyrant."},{"id":"167","who":"CASCA","text":"His countenance, like richest alchemy, Will change to virtue and to worthiness.","themes":["honour","public"],"note":"Brutus's reputation can turn crime into virtue."},{"id":"178","who":"BRUTUS","text":"It must be by his death: and for my part, I know no personal cause to spurn at him, But for the general.","themes":["honour","power"],"note":"Brutus begins with the conclusion and then looks for reasons."},{"id":"178","who":"BRUTUS","text":"And therefore think him as a serpent's egg Which hatch'd, would, as his kind grow mischievous; And kill him in the shell.","themes":["power","violence"],"note":"Brutus kills Caesar for what he might become."},{"id":"178","who":"BRUTUS","text":"That lowliness is young ambition's ladder,","themes":["power"],"note":"Ambition climbs humbly, then scorns the ladder."},{"id":"189","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Brutus, thou sleep'st: awake and see thyself.","themes":["rhetoric","honour"],"note":"The forged letter works because it echoes what Brutus already thinks."},{"id":"195","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Between the acting of a dreadful thing And the first motion, all the interim is Like a phantasma, or a hideous dream:","themes":["public"],"note":"The time between deciding and doing is a nightmare."},{"id":"204","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Hide it in smiles and affability:","themes":["public"],"note":"Conspiracy must wear a smiling mask."},{"id":"222","who":"BRUTUS","text":"No, not an oath.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Brutus overrules Cassius for the first time."},{"id":"232","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Let us be sacrificers, but not butchers, Caius.","themes":["violence","honour"],"note":"Brutus wants the murder to look like a religious rite."},{"id":"232","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Let's carve him as a dish fit for the gods, Not hew him as a carcass fit for hounds.","themes":["violence"],"note":"An elegant image for a bloody act."},{"id":"232","who":"BRUTUS","text":"We shall be call'd purgers, not murderers.","themes":["violence","public"],"note":"Brutus cares how the act will look."},{"id":"241","who":"DECIUS","text":"But when I tell him he hates flatterers, He says he does, being then most flattered.","themes":["rhetoric","power"],"note":"Decius knows how to work on Caesar's vanity."},{"id":"248","who":"BRUTUS","text":"But bear it as our Roman actors do,","themes":["public"],"note":"The conspirators must act a part."},{"id":"260","who":"PORTIA","text":"Dwell I but in the suburbs Of your good pleasure? If it be no more, Portia is Brutus' harlot, not his wife.","themes":["public","friendship"],"note":"Portia demands to share his secrets as a true wife."},{"id":"262","who":"PORTIA","text":"Giving myself a voluntary wound Here, in the thigh:","themes":["honour","public"],"note":"Portia proves her strength by wounding herself."},{"id":"297","who":"CAESAR","text":"Cowards die many times before their deaths; The valiant never taste of death but once.","themes":["fate","honour"],"note":"Caesar's famous answer to fear."},{"id":"296","who":"CALPHURNIA","text":"When beggars die, there are no comets seen; The heavens themselves blaze forth the death of princes.","themes":["fate","power"],"note":"Great men's deaths are announced by the sky."},{"id":"301","who":"CAESAR","text":"Danger knows full well That Caesar is more dangerous than he.","themes":["power"],"note":"Caesar compares himself to danger itself."},{"id":"312","who":"DECIUS","text":"Signifies that from you great Rome shall suck Reviving blood,","themes":["rhetoric","fate"],"note":"Decius reinterprets the dream to flatter Caesar."},{"id":"328","who":"BRUTUS","text":"That every like is not the same, O Caesar, The heart of Brutus yearns to think upon.","themes":["friendship","public"],"note":"Brutus grieves that friends in appearance are not friends in fact."},{"id":"332","who":"ARTEMIDORUS","text":"My heart laments that virtue cannot live Out of the teeth of emulation.","themes":["power"],"note":"Artemidorus blames envy."},{"id":"338","who":"PORTIA","text":"I have a man's mind, but a woman's might.","themes":["public"],"note":"Portia struggles to keep the secret."},{"id":"361","who":"SOOTHSAYER","text":"Ay, Caesar; but not gone.","themes":["fate"],"note":"The Soothsayer's reply turns Caesar's joke back on him."},{"id":"393","who":"CAESAR","text":"But I am constant as the northern star, Of whose true-fix'd and resting quality There is no fellow in the firmament.","themes":["power"],"note":"Caesar compares himself to the fixed pole star."},{"id":"400","who":"CAESAR","text":"Et tu, Brute?—Then fall, Caesar!","themes":["friendship","violence"],"note":"Caesar's last words: betrayal by Brutus ends his will to resist."},{"id":"402","who":"CINNA","text":"Liberty! Freedom! Tyranny is dead!","themes":["power"],"note":"The conspirators' slogan."},{"id":"418","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Stoop, Romans, stoop, And let us bathe our hands in Caesar's blood Up to the elbows, and besmear our swords:","themes":["violence"],"note":"Calphurnia's dream comes true."},{"id":"419","who":"CASSIUS","text":"How many ages hence Shall this our lofty scene be acted over In States unborn, and accents yet unknown!","themes":["public","violence"],"note":"Cassius imagines the murder as a play performed for ever."},{"id":"453","who":"ANTONY","text":"O, pardon me, thou bleeding piece of earth, That I am meek and gentle with these butchers.","themes":["violence","friendship"],"note":"Alone, Antony drops the mask."},{"id":"453","who":"ANTONY","text":"Cry havoc and let slip the dogs of war,","themes":["violence","fate"],"note":"Antony prophesies civil war."},{"id":"471","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Not that I loved Caesar less, but that I loved Rome more.","themes":["honour","rhetoric"],"note":"Brutus's case in one balanced sentence."},{"id":"471","who":"BRUTUS","text":"as he was ambitious, I slew him.","themes":["power","rhetoric"],"note":"Ambition is the only charge Brutus makes."},{"id":"479","who":"THIRD CITIZEN","text":"Let him be Caesar.","themes":["crowd"],"note":"The crowd wants to crown the man who killed Caesar for wanting a crown."},{"id":"499","who":"ANTONY","text":"Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears;","themes":["rhetoric","crowd"],"note":"Antony begins by putting the people first."},{"id":"499","who":"ANTONY","text":"The evil that men do lives after them, The good is oft interred with their bones;","themes":["rhetoric"],"note":"Antony seems to concede, while preparing to praise."},{"id":"499","who":"ANTONY","text":"For Brutus is an honourable man,","themes":["rhetoric","honour"],"note":"Repeated until the word means its opposite."},{"id":"499","who":"ANTONY","text":"When that the poor have cried, Caesar hath wept; Ambition should be made of sterner stuff:","themes":["power","rhetoric"],"note":"Antony answers 'ambitious' with examples."},{"id":"499","who":"ANTONY","text":"My heart is in the coffin there with Caesar, And I must pause till it come back to me.","themes":["rhetoric"],"note":"Antony breaks off, letting the crowd talk."},{"id":"527","who":"ANTONY","text":"This was the most unkindest cut of all;","themes":["friendship","rhetoric"],"note":"A double superlative for Brutus's stab."},{"id":"527","who":"ANTONY","text":"If you have tears, prepare to shed them now. You all do know this mantle.","themes":["rhetoric","violence"],"note":"Antony uses a prop: Caesar's torn cloak."},{"id":"534","who":"CITIZENS","text":"Revenge,—about,—seek,—burn,—fire,—kill,—slay,—let not a traitor live!","themes":["crowd","violence"],"note":"The crowd turns into a mob."},{"id":"538","who":"ANTONY","text":"I am no orator, as Brutus is; But, as you know me all, a plain blunt man,","themes":["rhetoric"],"note":"Antony, the finest speaker in the play, claims he cannot speak."},{"id":"557","who":"ANTONY","text":"Now let it work. Mischief, thou art afoot, Take thou what course thou wilt!","themes":["violence","power"],"note":"Antony lets loose the violence he has caused."},{"id":"590","who":"FOURTH CITIZEN","text":"Tear him for his bad verses, tear him for his bad verses.","themes":["crowd","violence"],"note":"The mob kills an innocent poet for his name."},{"id":"602","who":"ANTONY","text":"He shall not live; look, with a spot I damn him.","themes":["power","violence"],"note":"The new rulers kill their own relatives with a pen mark."},{"id":"608","who":"ANTONY","text":"He shall but bear them as the ass bears gold,","themes":["power","friendship"],"note":"Antony plans to use and discard Lepidus."},{"id":"647","who":"BRUTUS","text":"you yourself Are much condemn'd to have an itching palm,","themes":["honour","friendship"],"note":"Brutus accuses Cassius of taking bribes."},{"id":"651","who":"BRUTUS","text":"I had rather be a dog, and bay the moon, Than such a Roman.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Brutus's contempt for corruption."},{"id":"673","who":"BRUTUS","text":"By Heaven, I had rather coin my heart, And drop my blood for drachmas,","themes":["honour"],"note":"Brutus will not raise money by unjust means, but wants Cassius's."},{"id":"680","who":"CASSIUS","text":"A friendly eye could never see such faults.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Cassius asks for love, not judgement."},{"id":"712","who":"BRUTUS","text":"No man bears sorrow better. Portia is dead.","themes":["public","friendship"],"note":"Brutus hides his grief behind Stoic calm."},{"id":"753","who":"BRUTUS","text":"There is a tide in the affairs of men, Which, taken at the flood, leads on to fortune;","themes":["fate"],"note":"Brutus argues for attacking now."},{"id":"791","who":"GHOST","text":"Thy evil spirit, Brutus.","themes":["fate"],"note":"The Ghost names itself as Brutus's evil genius."},{"id":"887","who":"CASSIUS","text":"This day I breathed first. Time is come round, And where I did begin, there shall I end.","themes":["fate"],"note":"Cassius dies on his birthday."},{"id":"895","who":"CASSIUS","text":"Caesar, thou art reveng'd, Even with the sword that kill'd thee.","themes":["violence","fate"],"note":"Cassius dies by the sword that killed Caesar."},{"id":"913","who":"TITINIUS","text":"Alas, thou hast misconstrued everything!","themes":["fate","friendship"],"note":"Cassius killed himself over a misreading."},{"id":"920","who":"BRUTUS","text":"O Julius Caesar, thou art mighty yet! Thy spirit walks abroad, and turns our swords In our own proper entrails.","themes":["power","fate","violence"],"note":"Brutus admits the spirit of Caesar has won."},{"id":"923","who":"BRUTUS","text":"The last of all the Romans, fare thee well!","themes":["friendship","honour"],"note":"Brutus's tribute to Cassius."},{"id":"973","who":"BRUTUS","text":"My heart doth joy, that yet in all my life I found no man but he was true to me.","themes":["friendship","honour"],"note":"Brutus's final pride."},{"id":"980","who":"BRUTUS","text":"Caesar, now be still: I kill'd not thee with half so good a will.","themes":["fate","friendship"],"note":"Brutus's last words speak to Caesar."},{"id":"993","who":"ANTONY","text":"This was the noblest Roman of them all.","themes":["honour"],"note":"Antony's epitaph for Brutus."},{"id":"993","who":"ANTONY","text":"His life was gentle, and the elements So mix'd in him that Nature might stand up And say to all the world, \"This was a man!\"","themes":["honour"],"note":"The four elements perfectly balanced in Brutus."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Julius Caesar has returned to Rome in triumph and is close to becoming king. A group of senators, led by the envious Cassius, fear he will become a tyrant. They win over Brutus, the most respected man in Rome, who loves Caesar but loves the Roman republic more.","On the Ides of March, ignoring omens, his wife's dream and a soothsayer's warning, Caesar goes to the Senate and is stabbed to death. Brutus explains the killing to the people, but then lets Caesar's friend Mark Antony speak at the funeral. Antony turns the crowd against the conspirators, who flee Rome.","Antony joins Caesar's heir Octavius to make war on Brutus and Cassius. The two friends quarrel and make up; Brutus learns his wife has killed herself and sees Caesar's ghost. At Philippi, Cassius and then Brutus kill themselves, and Antony calls Brutus 'the noblest Roman of them all'."],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The tribunes scold the crowd for cheering Caesar","id":16},{"s":"a1s2","t":"The Soothsayer: 'Beware the Ides of March'","id":41},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Cassius begins to work on Brutus","id":60},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Casca reports the crown offered three times","id":96},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Cassius plans the forged letters","id":122},{"s":"a1s3","t":"A storm of omens; Cassius recruits Casca","id":144},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Brutus decides Caesar must die","id":178},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The conspirators meet; Brutus spares Antony","id":232},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Portia shows her wound","id":262},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Calphurnia's dream; Decius flatters Caesar into going","id":312},{"s":"a2s3","t":"Artemidorus writes his warning","id":332},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Caesar refuses Artemidorus and Metellus","id":393},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Caesar is stabbed: 'Et tu, Brute?'","id":400},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Antony shakes the murderers' hands","id":438},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Alone, Antony prophesies civil war","id":453},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Brutus's speech to the people","id":471},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Antony's funeral speech","id":499},{"s":"a3s2","t":"The will is read and the crowd riots","id":546},{"s":"a3s3","t":"The mob kills Cinna the poet","id":590},{"s":"a4s1","t":"The triumvirs list their enemies for death","id":602},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Brutus and Cassius quarrel and make up","id":682},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Brutus reveals Portia's death","id":712},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Caesar's Ghost appears to Brutus","id":791},{"s":"a5s3","t":"Cassius dies after misreading the battle","id":895},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Brutus runs on his sword","id":980},{"s":"a5s5","t":"Antony: 'the noblest Roman of them all'","id":993}],"irony":{"48":"Caesar calls the Soothsayer a dreamer; the audience knows the warning is true.","79":"Antony says Cassius is not dangerous, as Cassius is already plotting.","80":"Caesar says he does not fear Cassius, but he names the danger exactly.","189":"Brutus believes the letter speaks for Rome; the audience knows Cassius wrote it.","234":"Brutus says Antony can do nothing but grieve; Antony will destroy the conspiracy.","297":"Caesar speaks of death coming 'when it will come' on the day he will die.","312":"Decius's flattering reading of the dream is a lie, but Romans really will bathe in Caesar's blood.","319":"Caesar greets his murderers as friends and is glad they came to fetch him.","327":"Caesar invites the conspirators to drink wine with him 'like friends'.","393":"Caesar claims to be unshakeable seconds before he is struck down.","427":"Brutus promises Antony safety, giving his future enemy the chance he needs.","430":"Brutus is sure Antony will be a friend.","473":"Brutus says the crowd would do to him what he did to Caesar; within minutes they want to.","479":"The crowd want to make Brutus 'Caesar', the very thing he killed Caesar to prevent.","499":"Antony says he comes 'not to praise' Caesar, and then praises him.","538":"Antony claims he is 'no orator' while delivering a masterpiece of oratory.","973":"Brutus says no man was ever untrue to him; he was untrue to Caesar."},"devices":{"blood":"Blood and wounds","storm":"Storms and omens","animals":"Animals and hunting","theatre":"Acting and theatre","letters":"Letters and writing","stars":"Stars, sun and fire"},"dev":{"16":[["animals","Marullus calls the crowd 'blocks' and 'stones': not even animals, but things.","You blocks, you stones"]],"21":[["animals","Caesar is a falcon whose feathers must be plucked so he cannot soar.","growing feathers pluck'd"]],"68":[["stars","Cassius rejects the stars as the cause of men's fortunes.","not in our stars"]],"101":[["theatre","The crowd treats Caesar like an actor, clapping and hissing.","players in the theatre"]],"122":[["letters","Cassius plans letters in different handwriting to fake public opinion.","In several hands"]],"127":[["storm","The storm is 'a tempest dropping fire', civil strife in heaven.","a tempest dropping fire"]],"129":[["storm","A burning hand, a lion and an owl at noon: omens of disaster.","prodigies"]],"144":[["storm","Cassius reads the storm as a warning about Caesar.","instruments of fear and warning"]],"151":[["animals","Caesar is a wolf and a lion only because Romans are sheep and deer.","the Romans are but sheep"]],"178":[["animals","Caesar as a snake: an adder in sunshine and a serpent's egg.","serpent's egg"]],"189":[["letters","The forged letter, which Brutus completes himself.","Shall Rome, &c."]],"232":[["blood","Brutus wishes they could kill Caesar's spirit without spilling blood.","Caesar must bleed for it"],["animals","Caesar must be carved for the gods, not hacked for hounds.","fit for hounds"]],"241":[["animals","Decius lists how beasts are trapped, and men by flattery.","unicorns may be betray'd with trees"]],"248":[["theatre","The conspirators must behave like Roman actors.","Roman actors"]],"294":[["storm","Calphurnia's omens: graves open, warriors fight in the clouds.","drizzled blood upon the Capitol"],["blood","Blood falls from the sky onto the Capitol.","drizzled blood"]],"296":[["stars","Comets announce the death of princes.","no comets seen"]],"311":[["blood","The statue running blood like a fountain.","Did run pure blood"]],"332":[["letters","Artemidorus's warning, which Caesar never reads.","Caesar, beware of Brutus"]],"393":[["stars","Caesar as the fixed northern star.","constant as the northern star"]],"418":[["blood","The conspirators bathe their hands in Caesar's blood.","bathe our hands in Caesar's blood"]],"419":[["theatre","The murder imagined as a scene acted again and again.","this our lofty scene be acted over"]],"438":[["animals","Caesar as a hunted stag, a 'hart' that is the 'heart' of the world.","brave hart"],["blood","The hunters are 'crimson'd' in Caesar's lifeblood.","crimson'd in thy lethe"]],"453":[["animals","The 'dogs of war' let loose.","dogs of war"],["blood","Caesar's wounds are dumb mouths with ruby lips.","ruby lips"]],"527":[["blood","The blood follows Brutus's dagger as if to see who knocked.","the blood of Caesar follow'd it"]],"608":[["animals","Lepidus as a donkey carrying gold.","as the ass bears gold"]],"624":[["animals","False friends are horses that fail under the spur.","like horses hot at hand"]],"857":[["animals","Eagles leave and ravens, crows and kites arrive.","ravens, crows, and kites"]],"907":[["stars","Cassius's death as a red sun setting.","O setting sun"]]},"stage":{"street":"A street in Rome","orchard":"Brutus's orchard","palace":"Caesar's house","senate":"The Senate at the Capitol","forum":"The Forum","room":"Antony's house","tent":"Brutus's camp at Sardis","field":"The plains of Philippi"},"why":{"0":"Sets up the crowd as a force that switches loyalty, which the Forum scene will prove.","1":"The first statement of the conspirators' argument, made before any conspiracy exists.","2":"Creates suspense and dramatic irony: the audience knows the date, Caesar dismisses it.","3":"Shows Caesar's confidence, or arrogance, in the face of omens.","4":"Introduces the divided mind that makes Brutus the play's tragic hero.","5":"Shows how Cassius manipulates Brutus by appealing to his reputation.","6":"The key to Brutus. Cassius immediately uses it to steer him.","7":"Cassius's case against Caesar is personal and jealous, not political.","8":"A vivid image of one man's power shrinking everyone else.","9":"The play's central statement of free will, tested by every omen that follows.","10":"Cassius's words have worked: Brutus is already halfway to the conspiracy.","11":"Shows Caesar's sharp judgement of men, which he then ignores.","12":"The gap between the public Caesar and the deaf, ageing man.","13":"Shows Casca's pose as a blunt, plain man, which hides a sharp political mind.","14":"Cassius's soliloquy confirms the manipulation and reveals the forged letters plan.","15":"Links the omens to the civil strife the murder will cause.","16":"The whole play turns on men reading signs to suit themselves.","17":"Power depends on the people allowing it.","18":"Explains why the conspirators need Brutus: for his name.","19":"The decisive soliloquy: Brutus decides to kill a man he admits has done nothing wrong yet.","20":"A pre-emptive murder justified by a metaphor rather than evidence.","21":"Brutus's general theory of ambition, applied to Caesar without proof.","22":"Brutus is moved by letters Cassius wrote, believing them the voice of Rome.","23":"Shows Brutus's torment and the inner 'insurrection' of a divided man.","24":"Brutus, the honest man, chooses deception.","25":"The first of Brutus's fatal decisions, made from idealism.","26":"Brutus's attempt to make killing noble; the murder scene will test it.","27":"Shows how Brutus uses language to clean up violence.","28":"Brutus also thinks about public image, despite his claims.","29":"Sets up Decius's flattery in Act 2 Scene 2.","30":"Theatre and pretence run through the play.","31":"The private cost of the public plot: Brutus shuts out his wife.","32":"Portia claims Roman, 'manly' constancy through self-harm, foreshadowing her suicide.","33":"Caesar at his most heroic, just before he is persuaded by flattery.","34":"Links the omens directly to Caesar.","35":"Arrogance that the audience knows is misplaced.","36":"Both readings of the dream come true: the conspirators bathe in his blood, and Caesar's name lives on.","37":"Brutus's conscience shows even at the last moment.","38":"Another reading of the conspiracy: envy, not liberty.","39":"Shows the strain the conspiracy puts on those at home.","40":"Suspense at its height as Caesar walks to the Senate.","41":"His most god-like claim comes seconds before he is stabbed.","42":"The emotional centre of the murder: friendship betrayed.","43":"Their cause put in three words, which the rest of the play tests.","44":"The 'sacrificers' look like butchers; the image will feed Antony's speech.","45":"A theatrical joke: the audience is watching exactly that.","46":"Turns 'sacrificers' into 'butchers' and reveals Antony as the conspirators' enemy.","47":"Predicts the second half of the play.","48":"A clear, logical defence in prose, which wins the crowd briefly.","49":"The word Antony will take apart.","50":"Shows the people have not understood Brutus at all.","51":"Compare Brutus's 'Romans, countrymen, and lovers'.","52":"Opens the speech by pretending not to do what it does.","53":"The most famous use of irony in the play.","54":"Evidence against assertion: why Antony wins.","55":"A pause used as a weapon.","56":"Turns the crowd against Brutus by naming his betrayal.","57":"Moves from words to things the crowd can see.","58":"The result of Antony's speech, in broken shouts.","59":"False modesty as a technique.","60":"Shows Antony's cynicism once the crowd has gone.","61":"Shows the senseless violence the speeches have released.","62":"The triumvirate is as tyrannical as anything Caesar did.","63":"Shows the new power's cold calculation.","64":"The cause begins to rot from within.","65":"Brutus still measures everything by his ideal of Rome.","66":"Brutus's idealism depends on others doing the dirty work.","67":"The emotional heart of the quarrel.","68":"Explains his anger and shows his self-control, or coldness.","69":"A fine image used to justify a fatal tactical error.","70":"Caesar's spirit, which Brutus wanted to kill, returns.","71":"The man who denied fate sees his life as a circle.","72":"Caesar's revenge works through his killers' own hands.","73":"Echoes Cicero: men misread the signs.","74":"The conspirators wanted to kill Caesar's spirit; it survives them.","75":"Brutus honours the friend he quarrelled with.","76":"Ironic: Brutus himself was not true to Caesar.","77":"Brutus's death feels like a final settling of accounts with Caesar.","78":"The enemy's verdict: Brutus alone acted from honest motives.","79":"Ends the play with praise, leaving the audience to weigh it."},"arc":{"Brutus's resolve":[1,3,3,9,9,9,9,10,10,8,7,6,6,5,7,4,4,9],"Brutus's doubt":[1,7,7,5,4,4,4,2,1,2,3,5,8,7,4,8,8,2]},"arcnote":"Brutus begins 'with himself at war': he loves Caesar and fears his power. Once he decides in his orchard, his resolve stays high through the murder and his speech. From the moment the crowd turns, doubt creeps back: the quarrel, Portia's death, the Ghost and Cassius's suicide shake him. Only at the end, choosing death, is he calm and sure again.","context":[["Date","First performed in 1599, probably as one of the first plays at the new Globe Theatre. A Swiss traveller, Thomas Platter, saw a play about Caesar at a playhouse by the Thames in September 1599."],["Source","Shakespeare used Plutarch's Lives of Brutus, Caesar and Antony, in Thomas North's English translation (1579). Some speeches, such as Portia's wound, follow North closely; Antony's funeral speech is almost entirely Shakespeare's."],["Real history","Caesar was murdered on 15 March 44 BC. Shakespeare compresses years into days: the triumph (45 BC), the Lupercal (February 44 BC) and the two battles of Philippi (42 BC), which were weeks apart."],["The Roman republic","Rome had been ruled for centuries by elected officials and the Senate, not kings. The Brutus of legend had expelled the last king, Tarquin, and Romans hated the title 'king'."],["Elizabethan politics","Queen Elizabeth I was old, unmarried and had no heir. Audiences in 1599 feared civil war over the succession, and writing about killing a ruler was dangerous."],["Divine right","Many Elizabethans believed that kings were appointed by God and that rebellion was a sin. The play lets the audience weigh rebellion without taking a clear side."],["Omens and astrology","Elizabethans took comets, storms and dreams seriously as signs from heaven. The play shows characters reading the same signs in different ways."],["Stoicism","Brutus follows the Stoic belief that a wise man controls his emotions; Cassius follows Epicurus, who taught that the gods do not send omens. Both philosophies are tested."],["Rhetoric","Educated Elizabethans studied classical rhetoric at grammar school. Brutus's balanced prose and Antony's emotional verse show two different styles taught in the schools."],["Suicide","For Romans, suicide could be an honourable death; for Christians, it was a sin. The play's Roman suicides (Portia, Cassius, Titinius, Brutus) are presented as noble."],["Prose and verse","Most of the play is in blank verse. Brutus's speech to the crowd and the citizens' talk are in prose, which makes Antony's verse stand out."],["After the play","Octavius went on to defeat Antony (the story of Antony and Cleopatra) and became Augustus, the first Roman emperor. 'Caesar' became the title of every emperor after him."]],"critics":{"power":[["Ben Jonson, Timber, or Discoveries (published 1641)","Paraphrased: Jonson mocked a line he remembered Shakespeare giving Caesar, 'Caesar did never wrong but with just cause', as nonsense. The line does not survive in the printed play, which suggests it was changed."],["George Bernard Shaw, preface to Caesar and Cleopatra (1901)","Paraphrased: Shaw thought Shakespeare's Caesar a pompous, feeble figure, a poor portrait of one of history's greatest men, and wrote his own play partly to correct it."]],"honour":[["Samuel Taylor Coleridge, notes on Shakespeare (1810s)","Paraphrased: Coleridge was puzzled by Brutus's orchard soliloquy: a true republican would object to Caesar's power as it already is, not to what a crown might make him."],["Ernest Schanzer, The Problem Plays of Shakespeare (1963)","Paraphrased: Schanzer argued the play is a problem play, deliberately dividing the audience's sympathies between Caesar and Brutus so that neither is simply right."]],"rhetoric":[["Samuel Johnson, notes in his edition of Shakespeare (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson admired parts of the play but found it, as a whole, rather cold and unmoving, because Shakespeare kept too close to his Roman sources."]],"violence":[["René Girard, 'Collective Violence and Sacrifice in Shakespeare's Julius Caesar' (1984)","Paraphrased: Girard read the murder as a sacrifice that fails: the conspirators want one ritual killing to end disorder, but it spreads violence through the whole community."]],"public":[["Coppélia Kahn, Roman Shakespeare: Warriors, Wounds and Women (1997)","Paraphrased: Kahn argued that Roman virtue in the play is defined as masculine, so Portia must wound herself to claim it, and the women are pushed to the edge of the political world."]]},"insults":[{"id":2,"f":"you idle creatures","who":"Flavius","n":"Authority speaks down to the workers."},{"id":8,"f":"Thou naughty knave","who":"Marullus","n":"Marullus loses patience with the cobbler's puns."},{"id":16,"f":"You blocks, you stones, you worse than senseless things!","who":"Marullus","n":"The crowd is less than human."},{"id":65,"f":"As a sick girl","who":"Cassius","n":"Cassius insults Caesar's strength by calling him girlish."},{"id":101,"f":"the tag-rag people","who":"Casca","n":"Casca's contempt for the crowd."},{"id":144,"f":"You are dull, Casca","who":"Cassius","n":"Cassius goads Casca into joining."},{"id":151,"f":"What trash is Rome, What rubbish, and what offal","who":"Cassius","n":"Cassius insults the whole city."},{"id":388,"f":"base spaniel fawning","who":"Caesar","n":"Caesar despises flattery as dog-like."},{"id":388,"f":"I spurn thee like a cur out of my way","who":"Caesar","n":"Caesar treats a senator like a dog."},{"id":453,"f":"these butchers","who":"Antony","n":"Alone, Antony names the conspirators truly."},{"id":516,"f":"They were villains, murderers.","who":"Second Citizen","n":"The crowd's verdict has flipped."},{"id":606,"f":"a slight unmeritable man","who":"Antony","n":"Antony scorns his own ally."},{"id":657,"f":"Away, slight man!","who":"Brutus","n":"Brutus at his most scornful."},{"id":841,"f":"You show'd your teeth like apes, and fawn'd like hounds","who":"Antony","n":"Animal insults for the murderers."},{"id":847,"f":"A peevish school-boy","who":"Cassius","n":"Cassius sneers at Octavius's youth."},{"id":847,"f":"a masker and a reveller","who":"Cassius","n":"And at Antony's partying."},{"id":698,"f":"How vilely doth this cynic rhyme!","who":"Cassius","n":"The generals mock the poet."}],"pronouns":[{"id":2,"f":"Speak, what trade art thou?","n":"Flavius uses 'thou' to a social inferior."},{"id":65,"f":"Dar'st thou, Cassius, now","n":"Caesar uses 'thou' to Cassius as a challenge between equals, or a command."},{"id":122,"f":"Well, Brutus, thou art noble","n":"Alone, Cassius uses 'thou' to Brutus: intimate and a little contemptuous."},{"id":261,"f":"You are my true and honourable wife","n":"Brutus answers Portia with formal 'you'."},{"id":438,"f":"That I did love thee, Caesar, O, 'tis true","n":"Antony turns to 'thee' when he speaks to the dead Caesar."},{"id":682,"f":"If that thou be'st a Roman, take it forth.","n":"Cassius switches to 'thou' in his passionate appeal to Brutus."},{"id":683,"f":"O Cassius, you are yoked with a lamb","n":"Brutus calms down with 'you'."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Caesar is welcomed as a hero","The tribunes are punished","The people turn on Caesar"],"a1s2":["Brutus joins Cassius at once","Brutus refuses Cassius","Brutus asks for time to think"],"a1s3":["Brutus finds the letters","Casca warns Caesar","The plot is abandoned"],"a2s1":["Caesar stays at home","Caesar goes to the Senate","Portia warns Caesar"],"a2s2":["Artemidorus warns Caesar","Caesar turns back","The Senate crowns Caesar"],"a2s3":["Caesar reads the warning","Caesar ignores the warning","Artemidorus is arrested"],"a2s4":["The murder happens","Portia gives the plot away","Brutus comes home"],"a3s1":["Antony attacks Brutus at once","The people cheer Brutus","Antony turns the people against him"],"a3s2":["The mob burns Rome","The conspirators flee","Brutus is captured"],"a3s3":["Antony takes power","Brutus returns to Rome","Octavius makes peace"],"a4s1":["Brutus and Cassius fall out","Antony and Octavius quarrel","Brutus surrenders"],"a4s2":["They make up","They split their armies","One kills the other"],"a4s3":["Cassius's plan wins","They fight at Philippi","The Ghost returns"],"a5s1":["Brutus wins","Cassius wins","Both lose"],"a5s2":["Cassius's side breaks","Octavius is killed","Antony retreats"],"a5s3":["Brutus fights on","Brutus surrenders","Brutus kills himself"],"a5s4":["Brutus escapes","Brutus dies","Antony is killed"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Brutus and Cassius","pts":["Both lead the conspiracy, but Brutus acts for 'the general good' and Cassius from envy.","Cassius is the better politician and judge of men; Brutus overrules him every time and is wrong.","Both kill themselves at Philippi, each refusing to be captured."],"ids":[178,122]},{"t":"Brutus and Antony as speakers","pts":["Brutus speaks in prose and appeals to reason and honour.","Antony speaks in verse and appeals to emotion, with props, evidence and irony.","Brutus trusts the crowd to stay convinced; Antony watches and plays them."],"ids":[471,499]},{"t":"Portia and Calphurnia","pts":["Both wives fear for their husbands and plead with them.","Portia demands to share Brutus's secret; Calphurnia begs Caesar to stay at home.","Both are overruled by the public world, and Portia dies of the strain."],"ids":[260,302]},{"t":"Caesar in public and in private","pts":["In public he speaks of himself in the third person, as fearless and fixed.","In private he is deaf, has fits, is swayed by his wife and by flattery.","The murder kills the man, but the public name lives on."],"ids":[393,80]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Tragedy of Julius Caesar"],["Written","1599; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Tragedy: a Roman tragedy that is also a history play"],["Setting","Rome, 44 BC, then the camp at Sardis and the battlefield of Philippi, 42 BC"],["Protagonist","Marcus Brutus"],["Title character","Julius Caesar, killed halfway through the play"],["Antagonists","Cassius tempts Brutus; Antony and Octavius defeat him"],["Inciting incident","Cassius persuades Brutus that Caesar is a danger to Rome"],["Turning point","Antony's funeral speech turns the people against the conspirators"],["Climax","The murder of Caesar in the Senate (Act 3)"],["Ending","The suicides of Cassius and Brutus at Philippi"],["Speeches","826 in this text; Brutus has the most (209), then Cassius (142)"]],"analysis":{"BRUTUS":["Brutus is admired by everyone, friend and enemy. He is honest, serious and devoted to the idea of the Roman republic, and proud of his ancestor who drove out the kings. His weakness is that he cares deeply about his honour and his name, which lets Cassius steer him.","He decides to kill Caesar alone, at night, by reasoning about what Caesar 'may' become. He wants the murder to be a sacrifice, not a butchery, and makes every key decision for the conspirators: no oath, no Cicero, spare Antony, let Antony speak. Each is noble, and each is a mistake.","After the murder, his trust in reason costs him the crowd. At Sardis he is harsh and proud with Cassius, then loving; he takes Portia's death with Stoic calm. He insists on fighting at Philippi and is defeated.","He dies calmly, by his own will, asking his friends one by one to help. Antony calls him 'the noblest Roman of them all'. Whether he was a hero, a fool or a traitor is the play's central question."],"CASSIUS":["Cassius is lean, watchful and envious. He reads people well and knows how to use them: he flatters Brutus, mocks Caesar's weakness and plants forged letters.","His hatred of Caesar is personal: he saved Caesar's life and resents bowing to him. Yet he talks movingly of freedom and is willing to die rather than live under a tyrant.","He gives way to Brutus again and again, against his better judgement, because he loves and needs him. Their quarrel shows his hot temper and his longing to be loved.","At Philippi he half believes in omens and kills himself on his birthday after mistaking a victory for a defeat. Brutus calls him 'the last of all the Romans'."],"CAESAR":["Caesar appears in only three scenes before he dies, but dominates the play. In public he is grand and godlike, speaking of himself as 'Caesar' and claiming to be as constant as the northern star.","In private he is deaf in one ear, has epilepsy, and is swayed first by his wife's fear and then by Decius's flattery. Cassius's stories of his weakness make him seem a man, not a god.","We never learn for sure whether he wanted the crown. Casca says he did; Antony says he refused it. The play leaves the evidence open.","After his death his spirit becomes stronger: Antony uses his body and will, his Ghost visits Brutus, and his heir Octavius wins. 'Thou art mighty yet', Brutus admits."],"ANTONY":["Antony is first seen as a sportsman and party-goer, whom Brutus dismisses as harmless. He is Caesar's devoted friend.","After the murder he shows remarkable nerve: he shakes the murderers' hands and asks only to speak at the funeral. Alone, he reveals his grief and fury.","His funeral speech is the most brilliant piece of persuasion in Shakespeare: he praises the 'honourable men' until the phrase is an insult, shows the wounds and reads the will.","In power he is ruthless: he condemns his own nephew, plans to cut back the will and scorns Lepidus. At the end he is generous to Brutus in death."],"PORTIA":["Portia is Brutus's wife and the daughter of Cato, a famous enemy of Caesar. She insists on being treated as an equal partner who should share her husband's secrets.","She proves her strength by stabbing herself in the thigh, a gesture that claims a man's Roman courage.","On the Ides of March she can barely control her fear: she has 'a man's mind, but a woman's might'.","She dies offstage by swallowing fire, and Brutus reports her death with almost no visible emotion."],"CASCA":["Casca plays the blunt, sarcastic man who mocks the crowd and Caesar alike. His prose report of the crown-offering is our only account of it.","In the storm he is terrified and sees the omens as heaven's warning.","Cassius easily recruits him, and he strikes the first blow at Caesar: 'Speak, hands, for me!'"]},"motifs":[["Omens and the supernatural","Storms, a lion in the Capitol, a heartless sacrifice, Calphurnia's dream, the Soothsayer, the Ghost and the birds at Philippi. The question is always how to read them.",[2,15,16,34,70]],["Blood","Brutus wants a bloodless killing of Caesar's spirit, then bathes in blood; Antony shows the wounds and turns the blood against him.",[26,44,46,56]],["Letters and writing","Cassius's forged letters persuade Brutus; Artemidorus's letter goes unread; Caesar's will turns the crowd; the triumvirs write a death list.",[22,38,62]],["Acting and theatre","The conspirators must be 'Roman actors', the crowd claps Caesar like a player, and Cassius imagines the murder acted in ages to come.",[30,45,12]],["Names","'Brutus' and 'Caesar', the name of honour, Cinna killed for his name: names carry power of their own.",[9,61,6]],["Suicide","Cassius speaks of it in Act 1; Portia, Cassius, Titinius and Brutus all die by their own hands.",[71,72,77]]],"symbols2":[["The crown","Kingship, which Romans hated. It is offered three times and refused, but the conspirators kill Caesar for wanting it."],["Caesar's mantle","The torn, bloody cloak Antony shows the crowd: the murder made visible."],["The northern star","Caesar's claim to be fixed and unshakeable, just before he falls."],["The storm","Disorder in the heavens that mirrors disorder in the state."],["Caesar's Ghost","The spirit Brutus wanted to kill, which survives and has its revenge."],["The forged letters","Public opinion faked by Cassius, which Brutus completes with his own wishes."]],"bigq":[["Is Brutus right to kill Caesar?",["Caesar may have become a tyrant, and the Romans hated kings","Brutus admits Caesar has done nothing wrong yet","The result is civil war and harsher rulers","Brutus acts for Rome, not himself"],[19,20,48,62]],["Who is the hero of the play?",["Caesar gives the play its name and its power","Brutus has the inner conflict and the tragic fall","Antony has the greatest speech and wins","Perhaps no one: the play divides sympathy"],[74,4,78]],["Was Caesar ambitious?",["He refused the crown three times","Casca thinks he wanted it","He talks of himself as a god","He wept for the poor and left money to the people"],[54,41,12]],["Why does Antony win the crowd?",["He appeals to emotion, not reason","He uses props: the body, the mantle, the will","He uses irony on 'honourable'","The crowd is fickle and easily led"],[53,57,51,50]],["Fate or free will?",["The omens all come true","Cassius says the fault is in ourselves","Cicero says men misread signs","Cassius dies through a mistake, not fate"],[9,16,73,69]],["Is idealism a weakness in politics?",["Brutus's principles lead to every mistake","Cassius and Antony succeed by being practical","Brutus keeps his honour to the end","The practical new rulers are crueller"],[25,65,63,78]]],"symbols":{"Blood":"\\bblood|\\bbleed","Stars and heaven":"\\bstars?\\b|\\bheavens?\\b|\\bcomets?\\b","Honour":"\\bhonou?r","Ambition":"\\bambiti","Lion":"\\blions?\\b|\\blioness","Fire":"\\bfire\\b|\\bfiery\\b|\\bflame"},"stagerules":[["senate","senate"],["forum","forum"],["orchard","orchard"],["palace","palace"],["antony","room"],["tent","tent"],["philippi|field","field"],["street|public","street"]],"solo":{"122":1,"178":1,"195":1,"204":1,"453":1,"788":1},"iago":[["a1s2","Admits he is 'with himself at war' and fears the people will choose Caesar as king."],["a1s2","Lets Cassius flatter him and promises to think about what he has said."],["a2s1","Decides alone in his orchard that Caesar 'must be' killed, for what he might become."],["a2s1","Reads the forged letter and promises Rome redress 'at the hand of Brutus'."],["a2s1","Takes charge of the conspiracy: no oath, no Cicero, no killing Antony."],["a2s1","Shuts out Portia, then promises to tell her his secret."],["a2s2","Joins the escort for Caesar; grieves aside that seeming friends are not friends."],["a3s1","Kneels to Caesar with the others, then strikes the last blow."],["a3s1","Bathes in Caesar's blood and lets Antony speak at the funeral."],["a3s2","Explains the killing to the people and leaves them to Antony."],["a4s3","Quarrels with Cassius over money and honour, and learns of Portia's death."],["a4s3","Overrules Cassius to march to Philippi; sees Caesar's Ghost."],["a5s3","Finds Cassius dead and admits Caesar's spirit is 'mighty yet'."],["a5s5","Runs on his sword, telling Caesar to be still."]],"rel":{"1":[["CASSIUS","CAESAR","hate"],["CASSIUS","BRUTUS","deceive"],["BRUTUS","CAESAR","love"],["ANTONY","CAESAR","love"],["CAESAR","ANTONY","trust"],["CASCA","CAESAR","hate"],["CASSIUS","CASCA","trust"]],"2":[["BRUTUS","CAESAR","hate"],["BRUTUS","PORTIA","love"],["PORTIA","BRUTUS","trust"],["DECIUS","CAESAR","deceive"],["CALPHURNIA","CAESAR","love"],["CASSIUS","BRUTUS","trust"],["CAESAR","BRUTUS","trust"]],"3":[["BRUTUS","CAESAR","hate"],["ANTONY","BRUTUS","deceive"],["BRUTUS","ANTONY","trust"],["ANTONY","CAESAR","love"],["CASSIUS","ANTONY","hate"],["BRUTUS","CASSIUS","trust"]],"4":[["ANTONY","LEPIDUS","deceive"],["ANTONY","OCTAVIUS","trust"],["BRUTUS","CASSIUS","hate"],["CASSIUS","BRUTUS","love"],["BRUTUS","PORTIA","love"],["GHOST","BRUTUS","hate"]],"5":[["ANTONY","BRUTUS","hate"],["OCTAVIUS","ANTONY","hate"],["BRUTUS","CASSIUS","love"],["TITINIUS","CASSIUS","love"],["CASSIUS","PINDARUS","trust"],["STRATO","BRUTUS","love"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Brutus as a tragic hero?",[4,19,26,48,65,78]],["How does Shakespeare present the power of persuasion?",[5,22,29,53,57,48]],["How does Shakespeare present Caesar?",[11,12,33,41,42,74]],["How does Shakespeare present the Roman people?",[0,17,50,58,61,51]],["How does Shakespeare present fate and the supernatural?",[2,9,16,34,70,73]],["How does Shakespeare present friendship and betrayal?",[42,56,67,75,76,37]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (soliloquy, rhetoric, irony, image, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation","Referred to the whole play, not just one scene","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"place":{"a1s1":"Rome","a1s2":"Rome","a1s3":"Rome","a2s1":"Rome","a2s2":"Rome","a2s3":"Rome","a2s4":"Rome","a3s1":"Rome","a3s2":"Rome","a3s3":"Rome","a4s1":"Rome","a4s2":"Sardis","a4s3":"Sardis","a5s1":"Philippi","a5s2":"Philippi","a5s3":"Philippi","a5s4":"Philippi","a5s5":"Philippi"},"models":[[19,"Shakespeare presents Brutus as a tragic hero whose noble reasoning leads him to a terrible act.","In 'It must be by his death', the verb 'must' turns a choice into a necessity before Brutus has given a single reason.","The audience watch him admit he knows 'no personal cause', so his tragedy is that his virtue, not his vice, makes him a murderer.","Roman ideals of public duty above private love would have made his choice look heroic to some and treacherous to others.","Some critics, like Coleridge, find his reasoning so weak that it undermines his nobility."],[53,"Shakespeare shows that persuasion depends less on truth than on reading the audience.","Antony's repeated 'Brutus is an honourable man' changes meaning each time until 'honourable' sounds like an insult.","The crowd's shift from 'Let him be Caesar' to 'villains, murderers' shows how quickly feeling overcomes reason.","Elizabethan schoolboys studied rhetoric, so the audience could appreciate the contrast between Brutus's balanced prose and Antony's verse.","A darker reading sees Antony as a demagogue whose skill unleashes a mob that kills an innocent poet."],[41,"Shakespeare presents Caesar as a man whose public image is grander than the man himself.","Comparing himself to 'the northern star', Caesar claims to be fixed and unique, as if above humanity.","The audience have seen his deafness and his swaying between Calphurnia and Decius, so the boast feels hollow and ironic.","For an Elizabethan audience with an ageing queen, the gap between a ruler's public and private body was a live issue.","Yet the second half proves him 'mighty yet': his spirit outlives the man."],[50,"Shakespeare presents the Roman people as easily moved and dangerous.","'Let him be Caesar' shows the citizens cheering Brutus with the very title he killed to abolish.","Their switch to revenge and the murder of Cinna the poet show what happens when a crowd is roused.","The play was written when London authorities feared riots and unrest.","Some productions show the crowd more sympathetically, as people manipulated by their leaders."],[16,"Shakespeare presents fate as real but always open to misreading.","Cicero's 'men may construe things after their fashion' warns that signs mean what people want them to mean.","Cassius reads the storm against Caesar and later misreads the battle and dies: the omens come true, but characters misunderstand them.","Elizabethans took omens seriously, so the audience would feel the weight of the storm and the Ghost.","Cassius's 'The fault... is not in our stars' offers a counter-argument that the play never fully settles."],[42,"Shakespeare presents betrayal of friendship as the deepest wound in the play.","'Et tu, Brute?' is a short, shocked question: Caesar stops resisting once he sees Brutus.","Antony makes the most of it, calling Brutus's stab 'the most unkindest cut of all'.","Roman ideals of friendship and loyalty to the state pull in opposite directions in Brutus.","Yet the reconciliation of Brutus and Cassius, and Titinius's loyalty, show friendship can also survive."]],"lang":{"un":[[64,"Set honour in one eye and death i' the other, And I will look on both indifferently;","Put honour in front of one eye and death in front of the other, and I will look at both without fear."],[68,"The fault, dear Brutus, is not in our stars, But in ourselves, that we are underlings.","The fault that we are underlings, dear Brutus, is not in our stars but in ourselves."],[499,"The evil that men do lives after them, The good is oft interred with their bones;","The evil men do lives on after them; the good is often buried with their bones."],[753,"There is a tide in the affairs of men, Which, taken at the flood, leads on to fortune;","In men's affairs there is a tide which leads on to fortune if it is taken at the flood."]],"pun":[[7,"a mender of bad soles","soles","pun","Soles of shoes and souls of men: the cobbler claims to mend both."],[13,"all that I live by is with the awl","awl","pun","Awl, the cobbler's tool, sounds like 'all'."],[68,"Now is it Rome indeed, and room enough,","Rome","pun","Rome and 'room' sounded alike: a whole city with room for only one man."],[438,"Here wast thou bay'd, brave hart;","hart","pun","Hart is a stag, and sounds like heart: Caesar was the heart of the world."]],"st":[[400,"Et tu, Brute?—Then fall, Caesar!",[["tu","You, of all people."],["Brute","The name of a friend, said in shock."],["fall","Caesar gives up: he chooses to fall."]]],[499,"For Brutus is an honourable man,",[["Brutus","Pointing at the man himself."],["is","Insisting, so it sounds forced."],["honourable","Heavy irony: the word is turning into an insult."]]],[993,"This was the noblest Roman of them all.",[["This","Pointing to the body: a man, not a cause."],["noblest","Praise from an enemy."],["all","Brutus set apart from every other conspirator."]]]]},"dir":[[78,"Yond Cassius has a lean and hungry look;","How should Caesar say this?",[["Loudly, for Cassius to hear","Caesar's open contempt; Cassius's hatred grows."],["Quietly to Antony, smiling","Caesar's private shrewdness."],["Nervously, glancing back","Caesar is already afraid."]]],[178,"It must be by his death:","How should Brutus begin his soliloquy?",[["Calmly, as a settled decision","He has already decided; the rest is justification."],["In anguish, after a long silence","A man forcing himself to a conclusion."],["Testing the idea, as a question","The audience sees him persuading himself."]]],[400,"Et tu, Brute?—Then fall, Caesar!","How should Caesar die?",[["Shocked, reaching out to Brutus","A personal betrayal."],["Proudly, covering his face","He dies like a Roman, choosing to fall."],["Struggling, then giving up","A man, not a god."]]],[499,"Friends, Romans, countrymen, lend me your ears;","How should Antony begin?",[["Quietly, so the crowd must hush","He takes control by making them listen."],["Shouting over the noise","He commands the crowd."],["Choked with grief","Emotion first, argument later."]]],[682,"There is my dagger, And here my naked breast;","How should Cassius play this moment?",[["Sincere and in tears","A wounded friend asking for love."],["Theatrical, knowing Brutus will refuse","Cassius is manipulating again."],["Furious, pressing the dagger on Brutus","The quarrel is close to violence."]]],[791,"Thy evil spirit, Brutus.","How should the Ghost appear?",[["As Caesar in bloodstained robes","The murder made visible."],["Only as a voice and a dim light","Perhaps Brutus imagines it."],["Calm and kingly","Caesar's spirit as conqueror."]]]]};
